(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e2b.43baea47ee.js"]={"26":[{"id":"sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic α-amylase digests starch into which products?","choices":{"A":"Glucose, galactose, and free fructose units","B":"Maltose, maltotriose, α-limit dextrins","C":"Sucrose, lactose, and trehalose units","D":"Glycogen, amylopectin, and cellulose","E":"Galactose, maltose, and sucrose units"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancreatic amylase hydrolyzes interior 1,4-glycosidic bonds in starch and yields three oligosaccharides: α-limit dextrins, maltose, and maltotriose. Brush-border α-dextrinase, maltase, and sucrase then produce glucose. Monosaccharides are the brush-border end products, not amylase products. Sucrose, lactose, and trehalose are dietary disaccharides. Glycogen and amylopectin are substrates, and cellulose is not digested. Glucose, galactose, and fructose are the final monosaccharides made by brush-border enzymes. Sucrose, lactose, and trehalose are the dietary disaccharides that skip the amylase step, not products of starch digestion. In Costanzo, galactose comes from lactase digestion of lactose and sucrose is a dietary disaccharide split by sucrase, so neither is a product of amylase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.27). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: salivary amylase starts starch digestion but is inactivated by low gastric pH (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: human amylase does not hydrolyze the β-1,4 bonds of cellulose (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Glucose, galactose, and fructose are the final monosaccharides made by brush-border enzymes.","C":"Sucrose, lactose, and trehalose are the dietary disaccharides that skip the amylase step, not products of starch digestion.","E":"In Costanzo, galactose comes from lactase digestion of lactose and sucrose is a dietary disaccharide split by sucrase, so neither is a product of amylase."}},{"id":"sdl26_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Brush-border lactase digests lactose into which monosaccharides?","choices":{"A":"Glucose and fructose","B":"Two glucose molecules","C":"Glucose and galactose","D":"Galactose and fructose","E":"Glucose and maltose"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lactase splits each lactose into one glucose and one galactose. Glucose plus fructose is the sucrase product, and two glucose molecules come from trehalose or maltose. Galactose with fructose is not a dietary disaccharide product, and maltose is a disaccharide. Glucose and fructose are the products of sucrase acting on sucrose, not of lactase. Two glucose molecules come from trehalase acting on trehalose, not from lactase acting on lactose. Galactose comes only from lactose and fructose only from sucrose, so no single brush-border disaccharidase yields galactose plus fructose. Maltose is itself a disaccharide that maltase digests to glucose, so it is not a product of lactose digestion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sucrase splits sucrose into glucose and fructose, and trehalase splits trehalose into two glucose molecules (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: about 80 percent of carbohydrate end product is glucose, with galactose and fructose making up the rest (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Glucose and fructose are the products of sucrase acting on sucrose, not of lactase.","B":"Two glucose molecules come from trehalase acting on trehalose, not from lactase acting on lactose.","D":"Galactose comes only from lactose and fructose only from sucrose, so no single brush-border disaccharidase yields galactose plus fructose.","E":"Maltose is itself a disaccharide that maltase digests to glucose, so it is not a product of lactose digestion."}},{"id":"sdl26_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Undigested lactose in lactase deficiency causes diarrhea by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Active chloride secretion by crypts","B":"Faster colonic motility from gastrin","C":"Mucosal inflammation and blood loss","D":"Bile acid irritation of colonocytes","E":"Osmotic water retention in the lumen"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Nonabsorbable lactose stays in the lumen and holds an equivalent amount of water to keep contents isosmotic, which causes osmotic diarrhea. Crypt chloride secretion drives secretory diarrhea. Gastrin and motility changes are not the mechanism. Inflammation with blood loss suggests colitis. Bile acid diarrhea follows ileal disease. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption, Box 8.2). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic bacteria ferment undigested lactose and produce gas, causing bloating and flatulence (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: lactose intolerance is managed by avoiding milk products or adding lactase (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"Which apical transporter moves glucose and galactose into intestinal epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"GLUT5","B":"GLUT2","C":"GLUT4","D":"SGLT1","E":"NHE3"},"correct":"D","explanation":"SGLT1, the Na⁺-glucose cotransporter, carries glucose and galactose across the apical membrane against an electrochemical gradient. GLUT5 is the apical fructose transporter. GLUT2 moves monosaccharides across the basolateral membrane. GLUT4 is the insulin-responsive carrier of muscle and fat. NHE3 is an apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger. GLUT5 is the apical fructose-specific facilitated-diffusion transporter, not the carrier for glucose and galactose. GLUT2 sits on the basolateral membrane and lets monosaccharides exit into blood by facilitated diffusion; it is not the apical uptake step. Costanzo describes GLUT4 as the insulin-directed glucose transporter inserted into muscle and adipose cell membranes, not the apical intestinal carrier. First Aid states glucose and galactose are taken up into enterocytes by Na+-dependent SGLT1, not NHE3. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.28). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: SGLT1 in intestine and SGLT2 in the proximal tubule both use the sodium gradient for glucose uptake (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: glucose and galactose absorption resembles transport in the early proximal convoluted tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"GLUT5 is the apical fructose-specific facilitated-diffusion transporter, not the carrier for glucose and galactose.","B":"GLUT2 sits on the basolateral membrane and lets monosaccharides exit into blood by facilitated diffusion; it is not the apical uptake step.","C":"Costanzo describes GLUT4 as the insulin-directed glucose transporter inserted into muscle and adipose cell membranes, not the apical intestinal carrier.","E":"First Aid states glucose and galactose are taken up into enterocytes by Na+-dependent SGLT1, not NHE3."}},{"id":"sdl26_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"What supplies the energy for glucose uptake by SGLT1?","choices":{"A":"Direct ATP hydrolysis by the SGLT1 protein","B":"Sodium gradient set by the Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase","C":"Proton gradient set by apical exchange","D":"Glucose gradient into the enterocyte","E":"Potassium gradient through apical channels"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that the energy for SGLT1 does not come directly from ATP but from the Na⁺ gradient across the apical membrane, which the basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase creates and maintains. This is secondary active transport. Proton gradients drive peptide uptake. Glucose moves against its gradient on SGLT1. Potassium gradients do not drive it. SGLT1 does not hydrolyze ATP itself. An H+ gradient set by apical Na+-H+ exchange drives dipeptide and tripeptide uptake, not glucose entry on SGLT1. SGLT1 moves glucose into the cell against its electrochemical gradient, so a glucose gradient cannot supply the energy. Costanzo states the energy for SGLT1 uptake comes from the apical Na+ gradient kept by the basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, not a potassium gradient. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary active transport couples uphill solute movement to downhill Na⁺ movement (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Na⁺-glucose cotransport in the small intestine resembles transport in the early proximal tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"SGLT1 does not hydrolyze ATP itself.","C":"An H+ gradient set by apical Na+-H+ exchange drives dipeptide and tripeptide uptake, not glucose entry on SGLT1.","D":"SGLT1 moves glucose into the cell against its electrochemical gradient, so a glucose gradient cannot supply the energy.","E":"Costanzo states the energy for SGLT1 uptake comes from the apical Na+ gradient kept by the basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, not a potassium gradient."}},{"id":"sdl26_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"How does fructose cross the apical membrane of intestinal epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"Sodium cotransport via SGLT1","B":"Facilitated diffusion via GLUT4","C":"Proton cotransport via PepT1","D":"Primary active ATP pumping","E":"Facilitated diffusion via GLUT5"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fructose crosses the apical membrane by facilitated diffusion on the fructose-specific transporter GLUT5 and leaves across the basolateral membrane on GLUT2. No cotransporter or energy-requiring step is involved, so fructose moves down its gradient. SGLT1 carries glucose and galactose. GLUT4 is insulin regulated in muscle and fat. PepT1 carries small peptides. SGLT1 carries glucose and galactose with Na⁺; fructose uptake involves no cotransporter or energy-requiring step. Fructose absorption uses only facilitated diffusion, so it has no energy-requiring step and cannot move against its gradient. H+-dependent cotransporters carry dipeptides and tripeptides into the enterocyte, whereas fructose enters by facilitated diffusion on GLUT5. GLUT4 is the transporter insulin inserts into muscle and adipose membranes in Costanzo, not the apical route for intestinal fructose. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: fructose absorption by facilitated diffusion does not proceed against an electrochemical gradient (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: glucose and galactose exit the basolateral membrane on GLUT2, as fructose does (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"SGLT1 carries glucose and galactose with Na⁺; fructose uptake involves no cotransporter or energy-requiring step.","D":"Fructose absorption uses only facilitated diffusion, so it has no energy-requiring step and cannot move against its gradient.","C":"H+-dependent cotransporters carry dipeptides and tripeptides into the enterocyte, whereas fructose enters by facilitated diffusion on GLUT5.","B":"GLUT4 is the transporter insulin inserts into muscle and adipose membranes in Costanzo, not the apical route for intestinal fructose."}},{"id":"sdl26_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":1,"stem":"Which protease begins protein digestion in the stomach?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin","B":"Elastase","C":"Renin","D":"Pepsin","E":"Thrombin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Protein digestion begins with pepsin, formed when pepsinogen from chief cells meets low gastric pH. Trypsin and elastase are pancreatic proteases that act in the small intestine. Renin is a renal enzyme, and thrombin is a coagulation protease. Trypsin is a pancreatic protease that works in the small intestine after enterokinase activates trypsinogen, not in the stomach. Elastase is secreted by the pancreas as proelastase and is activated by trypsin in the small intestine, so it does not begin protein digestion in the stomach. First Aid lists pepsin from gastric chief cells as the stomach protease for protein digestion, not renin. First Aid lists pepsin from gastric chief cells as the stomach protease for protein digestion, not thrombin. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chief cells secrete pepsinogen, and parietal cells secrete H⁺ that activates it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: pepsin is an endopeptidase that digests collagen in meat (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Trypsin is a pancreatic protease that works in the small intestine after enterokinase activates trypsinogen, not in the stomach.","B":"Elastase is secreted by the pancreas as proelastase and is activated by trypsin in the small intestine, so it does not begin protein digestion in the stomach.","C":"Renin is a renal enzyme, and thrombin is a coagulation protease.","E":"First Aid lists pepsin from gastric chief cells as the stomach protease for protein digestion, not thrombin."}},{"id":"sdl26_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":5,"stem":"Above what pH is pepsin denatured and inactivated?","choices":{"A":"Above pH 5","B":"Above pH 2","C":"Above pH 3","D":"Above pH 9","E":"Above pH 11"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The three pepsin isozymes have pH optima between 1 and 3, and pepsin is denatured above pH 5, so it stops acting once chyme is neutralized by duodenal bicarbonate. pH 2 and 3 fall in the optimum range. pH 9 and 11 overshoot the textbook threshold. Pepsin isozymes have optimum activity between pH 1 and 3, so pepsin is active at pH 2, not denatured. Pepsin isozymes have optimum activity between pH 1 and 3, so pepsin is still active at pH 3, not denatured. Pepsin is already denatured above pH 5, so pH 9 is far past the point of inactivation. Pepsin is already denatured above pH 5, so pH 11 is far past the point of inactivation. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic HCO₃⁻ neutralizes duodenal chyme and inactivates pepsin (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: pepsin handles about 10 to 20 percent of total protein digestion (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"B":"Pepsin isozymes have optimum activity between pH 1 and 3, so pepsin is active at pH 2, not denatured.","C":"Pepsin isozymes have optimum activity between pH 1 and 3, so pepsin is still active at pH 3, not denatured.","D":"Pepsin is already denatured above pH 5, so pH 9 is far past the point of inactivation.","E":"Pepsin is already denatured above pH 5, so pH 11 is far past the point of inactivation."}},{"id":"sdl26_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pancreatic proteases are exopeptidases?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin and chymotrypsin","B":"Pepsin and elastase","C":"Carboxypeptidase A and B","D":"Elastase and trypsin","E":"Enterokinase and pepsin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Exopeptidases hydrolyze one amino acid at a time from the C-terminal end; carboxypeptidases A and B are the pancreatic exopeptidases. Trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, and pepsin are endopeptidases that cleave interior bonds. Enterokinase is a brush-border activating enzyme. Trypsin and chymotrypsin are endopeptidases that cut interior peptide bonds, not exopeptidases. Pepsin and elastase are endopeptidases that cut interior peptide bonds, not exopeptidases. Elastase and trypsin are both endopeptidases that hydrolyze interior peptide bonds. Pepsin is a gastric endopeptidase and enterokinase is the brush-border enzyme that activates trypsinogen, so neither is a pancreatic exopeptidase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: brush-border aminopeptidases and dipeptidases finish oligopeptide digestion to absorbable units (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: elastase digests the elastin fibers that hold meat together (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Trypsin and chymotrypsin are endopeptidases that cut interior peptide bonds, not exopeptidases.","B":"Pepsin is a gastric endopeptidase and enterokinase is the brush-border enzyme that activates trypsinogen, so neither is a pancreatic exopeptidase.","D":"Elastase and trypsin are both endopeptidases that hydrolyze interior peptide bonds; only carboxypeptidases A and B remove.","E":"Enterokinase is a brush-border activator of trypsinogen, not a pancreatic exopeptidase."}},{"id":"sdl26_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme first converts trypsinogen to trypsin?","choices":{"A":"Aminopeptidase","B":"Chymotrypsin","C":"Elastase","D":"Cathepsin B","E":"Enterokinase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brush-border enterokinase converts trypsinogen to trypsin; trypsin then activates the remaining zymogens and more trypsinogen. Aminopeptidase is a brush-border exopeptidase that trims oligopeptides. Chymotrypsin and elastase are themselves products of trypsin activation. Cathepsin B is a lysosomal protease. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins, Fig. 8.29). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enterokinase is also called enteropeptidase (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic secretions). COMLEX: trypsin activates trypsinogen in an autocatalytic step after the first activation (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — zymogen secretion and enteropeptidase activation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Chymotrypsin is made from chymotrypsinogen by trypsin, so it acts downstream of trypsin rather than starting the cascade.","A":"Aminopeptidase is a brush-border exopeptidase that trims oligopeptides.","C":"Elastase is itself a product of trypsin activation.","D":"Cathepsin B is a lysosomal protease."}},{"id":"sdl26_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is enterokinase located?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic zymogen granules","B":"Gastric chief cells","C":"Intestinal brush border","D":"Pancreatic ductal cells","E":"Hepatic bile canaliculi"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Enterokinase is a brush-border enzyme of the small intestinal epithelium, so trypsinogen is first activated in the intestinal lumen, not in the pancreas. Zymogen granules hold inactive precursors. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen. Ductal cells secrete HCO₃⁻-rich fluid. Bile canaliculi carry bile. Gastric chief cells secrete pepsinogen, the precursor of pepsin, not enterokinase. Zymogen granules store the pancreatic peptidases as inactive precursors inside acinar cells, and trypsinogen is activated only after secretion, by enterokinase on the intestinal brush border. Pancreatic ductal and centroacinar cells secrete the aqueous HCO₃⁻-rich component of pancreatic juice, not enterokinase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Exocrine pancreas; Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: separating zymogen storage from the activating enzyme protects the pancreas from autodigestion (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: trypsin from the first activation step converts further trypsinogen to trypsin (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — zymogen secretion and enteropeptidase activation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Gastric chief cells secrete pepsinogen, the precursor of pepsin, not enterokinase.","A":"Zymogen granules store the pancreatic peptidases as inactive precursors inside acinar cells, and trypsinogen is activated only after secretion, by enterokinase on the intestinal brush border.","D":"Pancreatic ductal and centroacinar cells secrete the aqueous HCO₃⁻-rich component of pancreatic juice, not enterokinase."}},{"id":"sdl26_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme converts chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, and the procarboxypeptidases to active forms?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin","B":"Enterokinase","C":"Pepsin","D":"Lipase","E":"Amylase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Once a small amount of trypsin is made by enterokinase, trypsin catalyzes the conversion of the other inactive precursors to chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidases A and B. Enterokinase acts on trypsinogen. Pepsin works in the stomach. Lipase and amylase digest fat and starch and do not activate proteases. Enterokinase only starts the cascade by activating trypsinogen; trypsin then converts the other inactive proteases. Pepsin works only at low gastric pH and is denatured above pH 5, so its action ends in the duodenum and it does not activate the pancreatic zymogens. Pancreatic amylase hydrolyzes interior 1,4-glycosidic bonds of starch, not the peptide bonds that convert protease precursors to active enzymes. Pancreatic lipase is secreted as the active enzyme and hydrolyzes triglyceride; it does not activate the protease precursors. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: absence of trypsin alone looks like loss of the pancreatic proteases because trypsin activates each precursor (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: trypsin also activates procolipase and prophospholipase A₂ (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic secretions).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — zymogen secretion and enteropeptidase activation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Enterokinase only starts the cascade by activating trypsinogen.","C":"Pepsin works only at low gastric pH and is denatured above pH 5, so its action ends in the duodenum and it does not activate the pancreatic zymogens.","E":"Pancreatic amylase hydrolyzes interior 1,4-glycosidic bonds of starch, not the peptide bonds that convert protease precursors to active enzymes.","D":"Pancreatic lipase is secreted as the active enzyme and hydrolyzes triglyceride; it does not activate the protease precursors."}},{"id":"sdl26_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":1,"stem":"Dipeptides and tripeptides cross the apical membrane by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Sodium cotransport","B":"Facilitated diffusion","C":"Primary active transport","D":"Proton cotransport","E":"Receptor endocytosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Separate H⁺-dependent cotransporters carry dipeptides and tripeptides into the cell, using an H⁺ gradient created by the apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger. Sodium cotransport carries free L-amino acids. Facilitated diffusion is the basolateral exit route for amino acids. Primary active transport and endocytosis are not the described routes. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins, Fig. 8.31). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the apical peptide carrier is PepT1 (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: peptide carriers allow protein nutrition despite single amino acid transporter defects (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sodium cotransport carries free L-amino acids.","B":"Facilitated diffusion is the basolateral exit route for amino acids.","C":"Primary active transport is not the described apical route.","E":"Endocytosis is not the described apical route."}},{"id":"sdl26_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":1,"stem":"In which form is most ingested protein absorbed?","choices":{"A":"Free single amino acids","B":"Dipeptides and tripeptides","C":"Intact dietary protein molecules","D":"Large oligopeptides","E":"Long polypeptide chains"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that most ingested protein is absorbed in the dipeptide and tripeptide forms rather than as free amino acids. Intact proteins and larger oligopeptides are not absorbable and must be broken down by brush-border peptidases. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proteins are absorbable in larger units than carbohydrates, which must reach monosaccharides (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: oligopeptides are hydrolyzed by brush-border proteases before absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Free amino acids are absorbed on their own carriers.","D":"Oligopeptides are not absorbable until brush-border peptidases shorten them.","C":"Intact dietary protein is not absorbable until proteases cut it.","E":"Long polypeptide chains are not absorbable until brush-border peptidases shorten them."}},{"id":"sdl26_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":1,"stem":"What happens to most dipeptides and tripeptides after they enter the epithelial cell?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis by cytoplasmic peptidases","B":"Export back out through apical PepT1","C":"Re-esterification in the smooth ER","D":"Packaging into nascent chylomicrons","E":"Conversion to cellular glycogen"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Inside the cell, most dipeptides and tripeptides are hydrolyzed to amino acids by cytoplasmic peptidases, and the amino acids exit across the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion; a few peptides are absorbed unchanged. PepT1 imports peptides. Re-esterification and chylomicrons belong to lipid absorption. Glycogen storage is unrelated. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal blood receives mostly free amino acids after protein absorption (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: basolateral amino acid exit uses separate carriers for neutral, acidic, basic, and imino amino acids (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has bloating, gas, and watery diarrhea within two hours of drinking milk. Symptoms began in adolescence and stop when he avoids dairy, without fever or weight loss. Temperature is 36.8°C and the abdomen is soft. Breath hydrogen rises by 30 ppm after lactose (a rise above 20 ppm is abnormal), and stool pH is 5.0 (reference range: 6.5–7.5). Which stool finding is most likely while he is symptomatic?","choices":{"A":"High stool sodium concentration","B":"Fecal leukocytes and blood","C":"Fat globules on Sudan stain","D":"Diarrhea persisting during fasting","E":"Increased stool osmotic gap"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lactase deficiency leaves milk sugar unabsorbed, so unmeasured osmoles widen the stool osmotic gap and diarrhea stops when lactose is withdrawn. A positive lactose hydrogen breath test, acid stool, and absent blood or leukocytes match that pattern. A high stool sodium with a narrow gap is the pattern of secretory diarrhea. Leukocytes and blood in stool indicate inflammatory or invasive mucosal disease. Sudan-positive fat globules indicate malabsorption of dietary triglyceride. Diarrhea that continues throughout fasting indicates a secretory process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bacterial fermentation of lactose raises breath hydrogen and lowers stool pH (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: the other brush-border enzymes are preserved in isolated lactase deficiency (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A high stool sodium with a narrow gap is the pattern of secretory diarrhea","B":"Leukocytes and blood in stool indicate inflammatory or invasive mucosal disease","C":"Sudan-positive fat globules indicate malabsorption of dietary triglyceride","D":"Diarrhea that continues throughout fasting indicates a secretory process"}},{"id":"sdl26_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-month-old girl has had watery diarrhea with every lactose-containing formula feeding for three weeks since rotavirus. She tolerated the same formula before the illness, and there is no blood in the stool. She is mildly dehydrated and the abdomen is soft. Stool pH is 5.0 (reference range: 6.5–7.5), and stool reducing substances are positive. Which change in her small intestine best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"Low pancreatic amylase output","B":"Loss of brush-border lactase","C":"Defective apical glucose cotransport","D":"Absent enterokinase activity","E":"Deficient bile salt secretion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rotavirus injures villus-tip enterocytes and temporarily removes brush-border lactase, so formula lactose reaches the colon and is fermented. Acid stool, positive reducing sugars, and tolerance of the same formula before the illness fit that reversible loss. Pancreatic amylase digests starch, and lactose never requires an amylase step. A congenital glucose-transport defect would have been present from the first feeds. Enterokinase activates trypsinogen and has no role in digesting milk sugar. Bile salts form lipid micelles and are not required for lactose digestion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary lactase deficiency follows mucosal injury from gastroenteritis or celiac disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: lactase sits on the villus brush border, where mucosal injury removes it first (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus structure and brush border","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pancreatic amylase digests starch, and lactose never requires an amylase step","C":"A congenital glucose-transport defect would have been present from the first feeds","D":"Enterokinase activates trypsinogen and has no role in digesting milk sugar","E":"Bile salts form lipid micelles and are not required for lactose digestion"}},{"id":"sdl26_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes starts acarbose with each meal. Two weeks later she has flatulence and loose stools that are worse after bread and pasta. The abdomen is soft and nontender. Hemoglobin A1c is 7.8% (reference range: <5.7%). Inhibition of which digestive step most directly explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Brush-border oligosaccharide hydrolysis","B":"Pancreatic triglyceride ester hydrolysis","C":"Basolateral monosaccharide exit","D":"Apical sodium-glucose cotransport","E":"Gastric pepsin protein hydrolysis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Acarbose inhibits brush-border α-glucosidases, so maltose, maltotriose, and α-limit dextrins are not cleaved to glucose and are fermented in the colon. Gas and loose stools after bread and pasta follow that block of oligosaccharide hydrolysis. Triglyceride hydrolysis is lipid digestion and is blocked by a different medication. Basolateral sugar exit moves free monosaccharides and is not a digestive cleavage. Apical sodium-glucose uptake transports free glucose and does not split oligosaccharides. Gastric pepsin hydrolyzes protein and does not digest dietary carbohydrate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: α-glucosidase inhibitors lower postprandial glucose by delaying carbohydrate digestion (First Aid Endocrine, diabetes drugs). COMLEX: oligosaccharides that escape digestion produce osmotic diarrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — brush-border enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Triglyceride hydrolysis is lipid digestion and is blocked by a different medication","C":"Basolateral sugar exit moves free monosaccharides and is not a digestive cleavage","D":"Apical sodium-glucose uptake transports free glucose and does not split oligosaccharides","E":"Gastric pepsin hydrolyzes protein and does not digest dietary carbohydrate"}},{"id":"sdl26_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old boy has severe watery diarrhea on breast milk and on standard infant formula. Diarrhea stops when the only carbohydrate in the formula is fructose. He is below birth weight, with a sunken fontanelle and a pulse of 162/min. Serum urea nitrogen is 28 mg/dL (reference range: 7–18 mg/dL), and stool pH is 5.0 (reference range: 6.5–7.5). Which transporter is most likely defective?","choices":{"A":"Apical fructose-specific uniporter","B":"Basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump","C":"Brush-border lactase hydrolase","D":"Apical sodium-glucose cotransporter","E":"Basolateral monosaccharide uniporter"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Glucose-galactose malabsorption is loss of apical SGLT1, so glucose and galactose stay in the lumen, while fructose still enters on GLUT5. Relief on a fructose-only formula, acid stool, and an elevated urea nitrogen fit dehydrated sugar malabsorption from birth. Loss of the fructose carrier would leave glucose and galactose absorption intact. Failure of the basolateral sodium pump would not spare a fructose-only feeding. Isolated lactase loss would still permit absorption of a glucose-based formula. A shared basolateral exit block would trap fructose inside the cell too.","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucose-galactose malabsorption is an autosomal recessive SGLT1 defect (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: fructose is absorbed independently of sodium (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — solute-coupled water absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Loss of the fructose carrier would leave glucose and galactose absorption intact","B":"Failure of the basolateral sodium pump would not spare a fructose-only feeding","C":"Isolated lactase loss would still permit absorption of a glucose-based formula","E":"A shared basolateral exit block would trap fructose inside the cell too"}},{"id":"sdl26_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has profuse rice-water diarrhea for 12 hours after drinking untreated water during an outbreak of rice-water diarrhea. Plain water has not stayed down. The pulse is 112/min, blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg, and the mucous membranes are dry. Serum bicarbonate is 17 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Why does an oral rehydration solution correct his volume deficit?","choices":{"A":"Glucose blocks crypt chloride secretion","B":"Sodium enters through the apical GLUT5","C":"Glucose stimulates pancreatic fluid outflow","D":"Sodium uptake no longer requires ATP use","E":"Glucose-coupled sodium uptake is preserved"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cholera toxin keeps crypt chloride channels open, but apical SGLT1 still couples glucose entry to sodium entry, and water follows the absorbed sodium. Metabolic acidosis with tachycardia shows volume loss that this preserved cotransport can replace. Glucose does not close the chloride channels that the toxin holds open. The apical fructose carrier transports fructose and carries no sodium ion. Stimulating pancreatic fluid secretion would add luminal volume, not restore it. The driving sodium gradient still depends on ATP use by the basolateral pump.","boardPrep":"Step 1: oral rehydration solution uses SGLT1 cotransport of sodium and glucose (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: cholera toxin raises cAMP in crypt cells and opens CFTR chloride channels (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — cholera toxin and secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Glucose does not close the chloride channels that the toxin holds open","B":"The apical fructose carrier transports fructose and carries no sodium ion","C":"Stimulating pancreatic fluid secretion would add luminal volume, not restore it","D":"The driving sodium gradient still depends on ATP use by the basolateral pump"}},{"id":"sdl26_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman training for a marathon tolerates more carbohydrate each hour from a gel that mixes glucose and fructose than from glucose alone. She has no diarrhea or weight change on long runs. The pulse is 58/min and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Fasting glucose is 84 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL). Which property of intestinal sugar absorption best explains the higher tolerable dose?","choices":{"A":"Fructose activates the glucose cotransporter","B":"Fructose uses a separate apical carrier","C":"Glucose stimulates fructose cotransport","D":"Fructose enters against its gradient","E":"Glucose exits through apical GLUT5"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Glucose uptake is limited by apical SGLT1, whereas fructose enters on a separate carrier, GLUT5, by facilitated diffusion, so the sugars add absorptive capacity. Better tolerance of the mixed gel with a normal fasting glucose fits that dual apical route. Fructose does not turn on or accelerate the sodium-glucose cotransporter. Glucose does not drive a coupled fructose transporter at the apical membrane. Fructose can move only down its gradient by facilitated diffusion. Glucose does not leave enterocytes through the apical fructose carrier.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLUT2 carries glucose, galactose, and fructose across the basolateral membrane (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: fructose absorption is passive and depends on its concentration gradient (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — monosaccharide transporters","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Fructose does not turn on or accelerate the sodium-glucose cotransporter","C":"Glucose does not drive a coupled fructose transporter at the apical membrane","D":"Fructose can move only down its gradient by facilitated diffusion","E":"Glucose does not leave enterocytes through the apical fructose carrier"}},{"id":"sdl26_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":5,"stem":"A 63-year-old man is seen one year after total gastrectomy for a gastric mass. He eats regular meals, has no diarrhea, and his weight has been stable for months. There is a healed midline scar and the abdomen is soft. Serum albumin is 4.0 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL), and fecal nitrogen is 1.4 g/day (reference range: 1–2 g/day). Which statement best describes his protein digestion?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal proteases complete digestion","B":"Protein digestion falls by about half","C":"Free amino acid uptake becomes defective","D":"Trypsinogen activation fails in duodenum","E":"Undigested protein reaches the colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancreatic proteases and brush-border peptidases finish protein digestion without pepsin, so absorption stays normal after total gastrectomy. Stable weight, normal albumin, and normal fecal nitrogen show that dietary protein is still assimilated. A one-half reduction overstates how much the gastric protease contributes. Intestinal carriers for free amino acids do not require an intact stomach. Trypsinogen is activated by brush-border enterokinase, not by gastric acid. Fecal nitrogen would rise if large amounts of protein were left undigested.","boardPrep":"Step 1: after total gastrectomy, vitamin B₁₂ deficiency follows loss of intrinsic factor (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: pepsin accounts for a minor share of protein digestion (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 4 — intrinsic factor and vitamin B12","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"B":"A one-half reduction overstates how much the gastric protease contributes","C":"Intestinal carriers for free amino acids do not require an intact stomach","D":"Trypsinogen is activated by brush-border enterokinase, not by gastric acid","E":"Fecal nitrogen would rise if large amounts of protein were left undigested"}},{"id":"sdl26_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man with years of heavy alcohol use has bulky, floating stools and weight loss. He has recurrent epigastric pain. He is thin, with mild epigastric tenderness. Fecal elastase is 80 µg/g (reference range: >200 µg/g), and fecal nitrogen is 5 g/day (reference range: 1–2 g/day). Which digestive step is most impaired for dietary protein?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis of lactose to monosaccharides","B":"Hydrolysis of proteins to oligopeptides","C":"Activation of pepsinogen in the stomach","D":"Apical uptake of small peptides","E":"Basolateral exit of amino acids"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancreatic insufficiency removes trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, and the carboxypeptidases, so protein is not hydrolyzed to absorbable oligopeptides. Low fecal elastase and high fecal nitrogen mark that missing luminal step. Lactose is split by brush-border lactase, which does not come from the pancreas. Pepsinogen activation depends on gastric acid and does not require the pancreas. Apical proton-coupled peptide uptake is an epithelial step spared by pancreatic failure. Basolateral amino acid exit is epithelial facilitated diffusion, not a pancreatic enzyme.","boardPrep":"Step 1: low fecal elastase is a marker of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: pancreatic enzyme replacement treats both steatorrhea and creatorrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lactose is split by brush-border lactase, which does not come from the pancreas","C":"Pepsinogen activation depends on gastric acid and does not require the pancreas","D":"Apical proton-coupled peptide uptake is an epithelial step spared by pancreatic failure","E":"Basolateral amino acid exit is epithelial facilitated diffusion, not a pancreatic enzyme"}},{"id":"sdl26_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":5,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman has taken high-dose omeprazole for two years for reflux. Heartburn is controlled, and she has no diarrhea or weight loss. The abdomen is nontender. Intragastric pH during monitoring is 6.0 (reference range: 1.5–3.5). Which change in gastric protein digestion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Pepsin activity rises in the stomach","B":"Trypsinogen stays inactive in the duodenum","C":"Pepsinogen stays inactive in the stomach","D":"Protein absorption ceases in the jejunum","E":"Chief cells stop secreting pepsinogen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pepsinogen converts to pepsin only at low pH, and pepsin is denatured above pH 5, so a gastric pH of 6.0 leaves pepsinogen inactive. Absent diarrhea and stable weight fit continued protein digestion by pancreatic and brush-border proteases. Activity of the gastric protease falls, rather than rises, as luminal pH climbs. Trypsinogen is activated in the duodenum by enterokinase, independent of gastric acid. Jejunal protein uptake does not cease when gastric acid is pharmacologically suppressed. Chief cells keep secreting pepsinogen under vagal drive despite the higher pH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors irreversibly block the parietal cell H⁺-K⁺ ATPase (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: pepsin isozymes have optimal activity between pH 1 and 3 (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation and pharmacologic inhibition of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"A":"Activity of the gastric protease falls, rather than rises, as luminal pH climbs","B":"Trypsinogen is activated in the duodenum by enterokinase, independent of gastric acid","D":"Jejunal protein uptake does not cease when gastric acid is pharmacologically suppressed","E":"Chief cells keep secreting pepsinogen under vagal drive despite the higher pH"}},{"id":"sdl26_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-month-old boy has failure to thrive, diarrhea, and generalized edema. He has been formula-fed and has had no respiratory infections. He has anasarca and a soft abdomen. Serum albumin is 2.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL), sweat chloride is 15 mEq/L (reference range: <30 mEq/L), duodenal trypsinogen protein is normal, and adding a brush-border extract restores proteolysis. Which finding was most likely in the untreated duodenal fluid?","choices":{"A":"Low pancreatic trypsinogen secretion","B":"Low brush-border lactase activity","C":"Low pancreatic α-amylase activity","D":"Low trypsin and elastase activity","E":"Low gastric pepsin enzyme activity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Congenital enterokinase deficiency leaves trypsinogen unactivated, so trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, and the carboxypeptidases stay inactive. Normal precursor protein, normal sweat chloride, and rescue by a brush-border extract place the defect on that activator. The precursor protein is present, so failure of trypsinogen secretion is not the problem. Lactase digests lactose and plays no part in activating pancreatic proteases. Pancreatic amylase is secreted in active form and does not await trypsin. Pepsin is switched on by gastric acid and does not need an intestinal activator.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypoproteinemic edema reflects low oncotic pressure from protein malabsorption (First Aid Cardiovascular, edema). COMLEX: absence of trypsin mimics loss of the pancreatic proteases (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — pancreatic proteases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The precursor protein is present, so failure of trypsinogen secretion is not the problem","B":"Lactase digests lactose and plays no part in activating pancreatic proteases","C":"Pancreatic amylase is secreted in active form and does not await trypsin","E":"Pepsin is switched on by gastric acid and does not need an intestinal activator"}},{"id":"sdl26_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man has severe epigastric pain radiating to the back after a weekend of heavy drinking. He has vomited twice and is not jaundiced. Temperature is 38.2°C, the pulse is 116/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg, with marked epigastric tenderness. Serum lipase is 1,450 U/L (reference range: 0–160 U/L). Premature activation of which zymogen inside the pancreas most directly starts this autodigestive cascade?","choices":{"A":"Chymotrypsinogen","B":"Proelastase","C":"Trypsinogen","D":"Procarboxypeptidase A","E":"Pepsinogen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Premature trypsinogen activation lets trypsin turn on itself and the other pancreatic zymogens, which starts autodigestion. Severe pain after heavy drinking and a lipase many times the reference limit fit that intrapancreatic cascade. Chymotrypsinogen is cleaved later and cannot ignite the remaining zymogens. Proelastase is a downstream precursor and does not activate its companion enzymes. Procarboxypeptidase A is activated later and does not start the enzyme cascade. Pepsinogen is produced by gastric chief cells and is not a pancreatic precursor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: alcohol and gallstones are the leading causes of acute pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: lipase is more specific than amylase for acute pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Chymotrypsinogen is cleaved later and cannot ignite the remaining zymogens","B":"Proelastase is a downstream precursor and does not activate its companion enzymes","D":"Procarboxypeptidase A is activated later and does not start the enzyme cascade","E":"Pepsinogen is produced by gastric chief cells and is not a pancreatic precursor"}},{"id":"sdl26_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":5,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl has had three episodes of acute pancreatitis. She does not drink alcohol, and ultrasound shows no gallstones. She recovers fully between episodes. Serum triglycerides are 110 mg/dL (reference range: <150 mg/dL). Genetic testing shows loss of a protein stored with the digestive zymogens in acinar cells. Which protective mechanism is most likely lost?","choices":{"A":"Blocking of early active trypsin","B":"Enterokinase storage in acinar cells","C":"Bicarbonate secretion by duct cells","D":"Secretin release from duodenal cells","E":"Bile reflux into the pancreatic duct"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancreatic trypsin inhibitor is packaged in acinar granules and blocks trypsin that forms too early, so its loss allows recurrent autodigestion. Normal triglycerides and no alcohol or gallstones point away from the common metabolic and obstructive triggers. Enterokinase resides on the intestinal brush border and is not an acinar granule protein. Ductal bicarbonate neutralizes acidic chyme and is not stored inside acinar granules. Secretin comes from duodenal S cells and is not packaged with acinar zymogens. Bile reflux can provoke pancreatitis, but it is not a protective granule protein.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SPINK1 and PRSS1 mutations cause hereditary pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: zymogen granules are impermeable to proteins, keeping enzymes out of the cytoplasm (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"B":"Enterokinase resides on the intestinal brush border and is not an acinar granule protein","C":"Ductal bicarbonate neutralizes acidic chyme and is not stored inside acinar granules","D":"Secretin comes from duodenal S cells and is not packaged with acinar zymogens","E":"Bile reflux can provoke pancreatitis, but it is not a protective granule protein"}},{"id":"sdl26_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy has a photosensitive scaly rash and episodes of ataxia that flare when his diet is low in protein. Growth and school performance are otherwise normal. There is a rash on sun-exposed skin and no edema. Urine contains excess neutral amino acids, and serum albumin is 4.3 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Which mechanism best explains his normal protein nutrition despite this transport defect?","choices":{"A":"Absorption on basolateral GLUT2 carriers","B":"Paracellular leak of free amino acids","C":"Pinocytosis of intact dietary protein","D":"Colonic absorption of amino acids","E":"Absorption as dipeptides and tripeptides"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hartnup disease disables the sodium-dependent transporter for neutral amino acids, but dipeptides and tripeptides still enter on a proton-dependent carrier and preserve nutrition. Normal albumin, a photosensitive rash, and neutral aminoaciduria fit tryptophan loss with intact peptide uptake. GLUT2 exports monosaccharides across the basolateral membrane and ignores amino acids. Paracellular leakage is not a meaningful absorptive route for dietary amino acids. Intact dietary protein cannot cross the mucosa in nutritionally useful amounts. Colonic mucosa does not absorb enough amino acids to maintain protein stores.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hartnup disease causes pellagra-like findings from tryptophan loss (First Aid Biochemistry). COMLEX: neutral, acidic, basic, and imino amino acids use separate Na⁺ cotransporters (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — amino acid cotransporters","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"GLUT2 exports monosaccharides across the basolateral membrane and ignores amino acids","B":"Paracellular leakage is not a meaningful absorptive route for dietary amino acids","C":"Intact dietary protein cannot cross the mucosa in nutritionally useful amounts","D":"Colonic mucosa does not absorb enough amino acids to maintain protein stores"}},{"id":"sdl26_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":2,"stem":"Jejunal mucosa from a 41-year-old man is studied in vitro after bariatric surgery. He has had no diarrhea or protein deficiency and does not take acid-suppressing drugs. The tissue is viable, with a stable potential difference. A selective block of apical sodium-hydrogen exchange drops glycylglycine uptake sharply, while brush-border peptidase activity is unchanged. What best explains the fall in dipeptide uptake?","choices":{"A":"Loss of the luminal sodium gradient","B":"Inhibition of brush-border peptidases","C":"Block of basolateral amino acid exit","D":"Loss of the luminal proton gradient","E":"Reduced pancreatic protease release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Apical dipeptide cotransport uses a proton gradient created by sodium-hydrogen exchange, so blocking that exchanger removes the driving force. Sharp loss of glycylglycine uptake, with peptidase activity left intact, ties the result to that gradient. The sodium gradient is what drives apical uptake of free amino acids. Surface peptidase activity was unchanged, so enzyme loss does not explain the fall. Basolateral amino acid exit does not control how fast apical peptide entry occurs. An isolated mucosal sheet no longer receives proteases secreted by the pancreas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the apical peptide transporter uses H⁺ cotransport (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: free amino acid uptake mirrors glucose uptake by Na⁺ cotransport (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The sodium gradient is what drives apical uptake of free amino acids","B":"Surface peptidase activity was unchanged, so enzyme loss does not explain the fall","C":"Basolateral amino acid exit does not control how fast apical peptide entry occurs","E":"An isolated mucosal sheet no longer receives proteases secreted by the pancreas"}},{"id":"sdl26_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man has recurrent flank pain and passes hexagonal urinary crystals. He has had two prior stones and has no diarrhea or growth failure. There is left costovertebral angle tenderness. Serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL), and the urine contains excess cystine, lysine, arginine, and ornithine. The same carrier fails in the intestine. Which mechanism normally moves these amino acids into enterocytes?","choices":{"A":"Proton-dependent cotransport","B":"Primary active ATP pumping","C":"Sodium-dependent cotransport","D":"Receptor-mediated endocytosis","E":"Paracellular solvent drag"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cystinuria is loss of the sodium-dependent cotransporter for cystine, ornithine, lysine, and arginine in kidney and intestine. Hexagonal crystals, dibasic aminoaciduria, and a normal creatinine identify that shared sodium-coupled carrier. Proton-dependent cotransport is the apical route for dipeptides and tripeptides. These free amino acids are not pumped by a carrier that hydrolyzes ATP directly. Receptor-mediated endocytosis is not how free dibasic amino acids enter enterocytes. Paracellular solvent drag is not the membrane route described for these amino acids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystinuria causes hexagonal cystine stones with a positive cyanide-nitroprusside test (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: unabsorbed dibasic amino acids are excreted in feces (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption by enterocytes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Proton-dependent cotransport is the apical route for dipeptides and tripeptides","B":"These free amino acids are not pumped by a carrier that hydrolyzes ATP directly","D":"Receptor-mediated endocytosis is not how free dibasic amino acids enter enterocytes","E":"Paracellular solvent drag is not the membrane route described for these amino acids"}},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has bloating and watery diarrhea after juice sweetened with cane sugar. He tolerates milk without symptoms, and the pattern has been present since infancy. The abdomen is soft and mildly distended. After a sucrose load, stool pH is 5.0 (reference range: 6.5–7.5) and breath hydrogen rises by 28 ppm (a rise above 20 ppm is abnormal). Deficiency of which brush-border enzyme is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Lactase","B":"Trehalase","C":"Amylase","D":"Lipase","E":"Sucrase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brush-border sucrase, the sucrose-splitting activity of sucrase-isomaltase, releases glucose and fructose, so its loss leaves cane sugar in the lumen. Acid stool and a positive sucrose hydrogen breath test after cane sugar, with continued tolerance of milk, fit unabsorbed sucrose. Lactase deficiency would cause symptoms after milk, which this child tolerates. Trehalase splits trehalose from mushrooms and does not digest cane sugar. Amylase acts on starch within the lumen and is not a brush-border disaccharidase. Lipase hydrolyzes triglyceride and has no action on dietary sucrose.","boardPrep":"Step 1: congenital sucrase-isomaltase deficiency presents when sucrose enters the diet (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption). COMLEX: sucrase also contributes to digestion of starch oligosaccharides to glucose (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lactase deficiency would cause symptoms after milk, which this child tolerates","B":"Trehalase splits trehalose from mushrooms and does not digest cane sugar","C":"Amylase acts on starch within the lumen and is not a brush-border disaccharidase","D":"Lipase hydrolyzes triglyceride and has no action on dietary sucrose"}},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":3,"stem":"Jejunal mucosa from a 42-year-old woman is studied immediately after bariatric surgery. She has no history of sugar intolerance. The mucosa is mounted in a bath containing glucose, galactose, and fructose, and baseline serosal appearance of glucose is measurable. A compound that selectively blocks the basolateral GLUT2 transporter is then added on the serosal side. Which result is expected?","choices":{"A":"Fructose exit continues through basolateral GLUT5","B":"Glucose exit shifts onto basolateral SGLT1 carriers","C":"Galactose exit continues by sodium cotransport","D":"Exit of all three sugars to the serosa falls","E":"Apical sugar entry rises to offset the block"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Glucose and galactose enter on apical SGLT1 and fructose enters on apical GLUT5, but all three monosaccharides leave through basolateral GLUT2. Blocking that shared exit therefore cuts serosal delivery of every sugar in the bath. Fructose does not exit across a basolateral GLUT5 protein. The sodium-glucose carrier faces the lumen and cannot serve as a basolateral exit. Galactose is not driven out of the cell by sodium cotransport. Sugar trapped in the cytosol reduces, rather than raises, further apical entry.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SGLT1 and GLUT5 bring sugars across the apical membrane, while GLUT2 carries glucose, galactose, and fructose out across the basolateral membrane (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Oral rehydration solutions rely on the intact SGLT1 pathway to drive sodium and water uptake.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Fructose does not exit across a basolateral GLUT5 protein","B":"The sodium-glucose carrier faces the lumen and cannot serve as a basolateral exit","C":"Galactose is not driven out of the cell by sodium cotransport","E":"Sugar trapped in the cytosol reduces, rather than raises, further apical entry"}},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":3,"stem":"A 31-year-old man has diarrhea, weight loss, and leg swelling for six months. He has an itchy blistering rash on the elbows and does not drink heavily. There is pitting ankle edema and a tympanitic abdomen. Serum albumin is 2.2 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL), IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is positive, and fecal elastase is 400 µg/g (reference range: >200 µg/g). Duodenal biopsy shows flattened villi with many intraepithelial lymphocytes. Which step of protein digestion is most impaired?","choices":{"A":"Activation of pepsinogen by gastric acid","B":"Secretion of pancreatic endopeptidases","C":"C-terminal cleavage by carboxypeptidase","D":"Acid denaturation of dietary protein","E":"Brush-border oligopeptide hydrolysis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Celiac disease, shown by transglutaminase antibody and flat villi, removes brush-border peptidases that finish oligopeptides made by pancreatic proteases. Hypoalbuminemia with leg edema fits loss of that mucosal digestive step. Pepsinogen activation by acid occurs in the stomach and is not essential here. Normal fecal elastase shows that pancreatic endopeptidase secretion is preserved. Carboxypeptidase cleavage is a pancreatic function, and pancreatic output is intact. Acid denaturation of dietary protein occurs in the stomach, upstream of this injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pancreatic proteases yield amino acids, dipeptides, tripeptides, and oligopeptides, and brush-border proteases finish the oligopeptides (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Mucosal disease can cause protein loss and edema even when pancreatic function is normal.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pepsinogen activation by acid occurs in the stomach and is not essential here","B":"Normal fecal elastase shows that pancreatic endopeptidase secretion is preserved","C":"Carboxypeptidase cleavage is a pancreatic function, and pancreatic output is intact","D":"Acid denaturation of dietary protein occurs in the stomach, upstream of this injury"}},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":3,"stem":"A 3-month-old boy has failure to thrive, diarrhea, and edema on standard formula. He has had no respiratory infections. Duodenal aspirate contains normal amounts of trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, and proelastase protein but almost no protease activity. Serum albumin is 2.0 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Addition of a small amount of purified active trypsin sharply raises trypsin, chymotrypsin, and elastase activities. Which property of trypsin explains this result?","choices":{"A":"It replaces gastric pepsin in digestion","B":"It activates trypsinogen and other zymogens","C":"It shields the zymogens from acid denaturation","D":"It binds colipase to restore lipase action","E":"It is the sole enzyme able to digest elastin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The zymogens are present but inactive until a little trypsin autocatalyzes more trypsinogen and activates chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, and the procarboxypeptidases. Hypoalbuminemia with absent activity that returns after trypsin is added fits a failed activation step. Pepsin is unnecessary for protein digestion and does not activate these zymogens. These precursors are activated in alkaline duodenal fluid, not shielded from acid. Colipase anchors lipase to fat droplets and does not switch on proteases. Elastin is cleaved by elastase, so trypsin is not the sole elastin-cleaving enzyme.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterokinase activates trypsinogen, and trypsin then activates more trypsinogen and the other pancreatic zymogens (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Premature trypsin activation inside the pancreas sets off the autodigestion of acute pancreatitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pepsin is unnecessary for protein digestion and does not activate these zymogens","C":"These precursors are activated in alkaline duodenal fluid, not shielded from acid","D":"Colipase anchors lipase to fat droplets and does not switch on proteases","E":"Elastin is cleaved by elastase, so trypsin is not the sole elastin-cleaving enzyme"}},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":3,"stem":"A strip of jejunal mucosa from a 36-year-old man is mounted between two chambers at surgery. He had no diarrhea beforehand. The luminal chamber contains free leucine, and the tissue potential is stable. Ouabain is added on the serosal side to inhibit the basolateral sodium-potassium pump. Which transport step is most directly reduced?","choices":{"A":"Apical Na⁺-coupled uptake of free leucine","B":"Basolateral exit by facilitated diffusion","C":"Cytosolic hydrolysis of absorbed dipeptides","D":"Brush-border cleavage of oligopeptides","E":"Apical fructose entry through GLUT5"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Free amino acids enter on apical sodium-coupled carriers driven by the sodium gradient that the basolateral sodium-potassium ATPase maintains. Serosal ouabain dissipates that gradient, so uptake of luminal leucine falls first. Basolateral amino acid exit is facilitated diffusion and does not require sodium. Cytosolic peptidases split dipeptides with no dependence on the sodium gradient. Brush-border proteases cleave luminal peptides and are not sodium-driven transporters. Fructose crosses the apical membrane on GLUT5 by sodium-independent diffusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Free amino acids enter by Na⁺-dependent cotransport and leave by facilitated diffusion, while dipeptides and tripeptides enter by H⁺-dependent cotransport (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Defects in specific amino acid cotransporters cause disorders such as Hartnup disease and cystinuria.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Basolateral amino acid exit is facilitated diffusion and does not require sodium","C":"Cytosolic peptidases split dipeptides with no dependence on the sodium gradient","D":"Brush-border proteases cleave luminal peptides and are not sodium-driven transporters","E":"Fructose crosses the apical membrane on GLUT5 by sodium-independent diffusion"}},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with long-standing heavy alcohol use has recurrent epigastric pain radiating to the back, bulky foul-smelling stools, and weight loss. He has not started enzyme capsules. He is thin, with mild epigastric tenderness. CT shows calcifications throughout the pancreas, and fecal elastase is 40 µg/g (reference range: >200 µg/g). Digestion of which dietary carbohydrate is most impaired?","choices":{"A":"Lactose","B":"Sucrose","C":"Starch","D":"Trehalose","E":"Maltose"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Calcific chronic pancreatitis with low fecal elastase removes pancreatic amylase, so starch is not split into maltose, maltotriose, and α-limit dextrins. Salivary amylase adds little because gastric acid inactivates it before the intestine. Lactose is hydrolyzed by brush-border lactase and never needs pancreatic amylase. Sucrose is split by brush-border sucrase and does not require pancreatic amylase. Trehalose is cleaved by brush-border trehalase without any pancreatic amylase step. Maltose is split by mucosal maltase, which remains on an intact brush border.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pancreatic amylase digests starch to oligosaccharides that brush-border enzymes then convert to glucose, while dietary disaccharides need only brush-border enzymes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Pancreatic enzyme replacement is the treatment for maldigestion in chronic pancreatitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lactose is hydrolyzed by brush-border lactase and never needs pancreatic amylase","B":"Sucrose is split by brush-border sucrase and does not require pancreatic amylase","D":"Trehalose is cleaved by brush-border trehalase without any pancreatic amylase step","E":"Maltose is split by mucosal maltase, which remains on an intact brush border"}},{"id":"grid_sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":4,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has bulky stools and weight loss. Six months ago she had a total pancreatectomy for an intraductal tumor, and she stopped her enzyme capsules a month ago. The abdomen is soft, without tenderness. Hemoglobin A1c is 7.9% (reference range: <5.7%), and fecal fat is 18 g/day (reference range: <7 g/day). Which of the following sets of carbohydrate digestion findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Lactose digestion","Sucrose digestion","Starch digestion"]},"choices":{"A":["↔","↔","↓"],"B":["↓","↔","↔"],"C":["↔","↓","↔"],"D":["↓","↓","↓"],"E":["↔","↔","↔"]},"correct":"A","explanation":"Choice A (↔ lactose digestion, ↔ sucrose digestion, ↓ starch digestion) is correct. Without the pancreas there is no pancreatic α-amylase to split starch into maltose, maltotriose, and α-limit dextrins, so starch digestion falls; salivary amylase is inactivated by gastric acid and gives little help. Lactose and sucrose are already disaccharides and are split by brush-border lactase and sucrase, which are intact. Choice B (↓ lactose digestion, ↔ sucrose digestion, ↔ starch digestion) is lactase deficiency. Choice C (↔ lactose digestion, ↓ sucrose digestion, ↔ starch digestion) is sucrase-isomaltase deficiency, in which sucrose goes undigested while pancreatic amylase and maltase still handle most starch. Choice D (↓ lactose digestion, ↓ sucrose digestion, ↓ starch digestion) is diffuse villous injury, as in celiac disease, which removes brush-border enzymes. Choice E (↔ lactose digestion, ↔ sucrose digestion, ↔ starch digestion) is normal digestion, as with adequate enzyme replacement. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.27; Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption); First Aid Gastrointestinal (malabsorption syndromes). The ↔ cells, including starch in sucrase-isomaltase deficiency, are inferred because the sources do not grade them. Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diabetes after total pancreatectomy reflects loss of islet insulin and glucagon (First Aid Endocrine). COMLEX: brush-border α-dextrinase, maltase, and sucrase finish starch digestion to glucose (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":1,"stem":"Pepsin cleaves dietary protein at which sites?","choices":{"A":"Interior peptide bonds","B":"Only the C-terminal amino acid","C":"Only the N-terminal amino acid","D":"Glycosidic bonds in starch","E":"Ester bonds in triglyceride"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pepsin is an endopeptidase that hydrolyzes interior peptide bonds, yielding large polypeptides, proteoses, and peptones. Removing the C-terminal amino acid is the action of carboxypeptidases, not pepsin. N-terminal trimming is an exopeptidase action, not pepsin's. Glycosidic bonds in starch are hydrolyzed by amylase. Triglyceride ester bonds are the substrate of lipases.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Removing the C-terminal amino acid is the action of carboxypeptidases, not pepsin","C":"N-terminal trimming is an exopeptidase action, not pepsin's","D":"Glycosidic bonds in starch are hydrolyzed by amylase","E":"Triglyceride ester bonds are the substrate of lipases"}},{"id":"sdl26_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man has a sample of gastric juice taken an hour after a protein meal, during a research study. He has no diarrhea, no weight loss, and takes no acid-suppressing drug. The abdomen is soft. Intragastric pH is 1.8 (reference range: 1.5–3.5) and albumin is 4.4 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). The protein fragments in the sample are large polypeptides rather than free amino acids. Which products has pepsin mainly released?","choices":{"A":"Free amino acids ready for portal absorption","B":"Glucose and galactose","C":"Proteoses and peptones","D":"Micelles of monoglyceride","E":"Intact triglyceride droplets"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pepsin's endopeptidase action releases large polypeptides, proteoses, and peptones, not a pool of free amino acids. Free amino acids are a later product of pancreatic and brush-border peptidases, not the main gastric yield. Glucose and galactose are carbohydrate products and are not released by pepsin. Monoglyceride micelles are the result of lipid digestion, not of pepsin. Intact triglyceride is fat that pepsin does not hydrolyze.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Free amino acids are a later product of pancreatic and brush-border peptidases, not the main gastric yield","B":"Glucose and galactose are carbohydrate products and are not released by pepsin","D":"Monoglyceride micelles are the result of lipid digestion, not of pepsin","E":"Intact triglyceride is fat that pepsin does not hydrolyze"}},{"id":"sdl26_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has epigastric burning that improves on an acid-suppressing drug. She has no diarrhea or weight loss. The abdomen is soft and there is no mass. Hemoglobin is 13.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) and albumin is 4.2 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). A gastric biopsy highlights the cells that package the inactive precursor of the gastric protease. Which cells are they?","choices":{"A":"Parietal cells","B":"Chief cells","C":"Goblet cells of the colon","D":"Hepatocytes","E":"Pancreatic duct cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chief cells secrete pepsinogen, the inactive precursor that acid converts to pepsin. Parietal cells secrete the acid that activates pepsinogen, and they do not package that precursor. Colonic goblet cells secrete mucin, not pepsinogen. Hepatocytes do not synthesize the gastric protease precursor. Pancreatic duct cells secrete bicarbonate, not pepsinogen.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Parietal cells secrete the acid that activates pepsinogen, and they do not package that precursor","C":"Colonic goblet cells secrete mucin, not pepsinogen","D":"Hepatocytes do not synthesize the gastric protease precursor","E":"Pancreatic duct cells secrete bicarbonate, not pepsinogen"}},{"id":"sdl26_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old man has duodenal juice sampled in a research protocol an hour after a meal. He has no abdominal pain and does not drink alcohol. The abdomen is soft. Serum lipase is 28 U/L (reference range: 10–140 U/L) and triglycerides are 90 mg/dL (reference range: <150 mg/dL). A small amount of trypsin appears, and then more trypsinogen converts even though no further enterokinase is added. What converts that remaining trypsinogen?","choices":{"A":"Pepsin spilling back from the stomach","B":"Bile salts acting as proteases","C":"Salivary amylase","D":"Trypsin already present","E":"Gastric intrinsic factor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Once a little trypsin is present, it autocatalyzes activation of the remaining trypsinogen. Pepsin is denatured once duodenal pH rises, and it is not the enzyme that continues the trypsinogen cascade. Bile salts emulsify fat and are not proteases. Salivary amylase digests starch and does not cleave trypsinogen. Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12 and has no protease activity.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pepsin is denatured once duodenal pH rises, and it is not the enzyme that continues the trypsinogen cascade","B":"Bile salts emulsify fat and are not proteases","C":"Salivary amylase digests starch and does not cleave trypsinogen","E":"Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12 and has no protease activity"}}],"27":[{"id":"sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes each triglyceride molecule into which products?","choices":{"A":"One monoglyceride and two fatty acids","B":"Glycerol and three free fatty acids","C":"Two monoglycerides and one fatty acid","D":"Cholesterol and two free fatty acids","E":"Lysolecithin and one free fatty acid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancreatic lipase, secreted in active form, splits a triglyceride into one monoglyceride and two fatty acids. Pancreatic lipase does not release free glycerol, and two monoglycerides would need a second glycerol backbone. Cholesterol ester hydrolase splits cholesterol esters into free cholesterol and fatty acids, and lysolecithin comes from phospholipids. Glycerol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase cleaving triglyceride ester bonds. Lysolecithin plus a fatty acid is the product of phospholipase A2 acting on phospholipids, not of lipase acting on triglyceride. Pancreatic lipase yields one monoglyceride, not two, from each triglyceride. Free cholesterol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase acting on cholesterol esters, not lipase acting on triglyceride. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lingual and gastric lipases hydrolyze about 10 percent of ingested triglyceride before the chyme reaches the duodenum (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the optimum pH for pancreatic lipase is about 6, so duodenal acid must be neutralized by bicarbonate (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Glycerol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase cleaving triglyceride ester bonds; pancreatic lipase yields.","E":"Lysolecithin plus a fatty acid is the product of phospholipase A2 acting on phospholipids, not of lipase acting on triglyceride.","C":"Pancreatic lipase yields one monoglyceride, not two, from each triglyceride.","D":"Free cholesterol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase acting on cholesterol esters, not lipase acting on triglyceride."}},{"id":"sdl27_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the main function of colipase in lipid digestion?","choices":{"A":"Emulsifying lipid droplets in the gastric lumen","B":"Activating trypsinogen in the duodenal lumen","C":"Displacing bile salts from lipase","D":"Hydrolyzing cholesterol esters to free cholesterol","E":"Packaging lipids into chylomicrons in enterocytes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile salts displace pancreatic lipase from emulsified droplets. Colipase, activated from procolipase by trypsin, displaces the bile salts and binds lipase so digestion can proceed. Dietary proteins emulsify fat in the stomach, enterokinase activates trypsinogen, cholesterol ester hydrolase acts on cholesterol esters, and enterocytes assemble chylomicrons. Trypsinogen is activated by brush-border enterokinase; colipase is instead activated from procolipase by trypsin. In the stomach, lipid droplets are emulsified by dietary proteins; colipase acts in the small intestine. Cholesterol ester hydrolase, not colipase, hydrolyzes cholesterol esters. Chylomicrons are assembled inside enterocytes, not by colipase in the lumen. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption of fat and fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: CCK released by fat in the duodenum slows gastric emptying and gives pancreatic enzymes time to act (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Trypsinogen is activated by brush-border enterokinase; colipase is instead activated from procolipase by trypsin.","A":"In the stomach, lipid droplets are emulsified by dietary proteins; colipase acts in the small intestine.","D":"Cholesterol ester hydrolase, not colipase, hydrolyzes cholesterol esters.","E":"Chylomicrons are assembled inside enterocytes, not by colipase in the lumen."}},{"id":"sdl27_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic phospholipase A2 hydrolyzes dietary phospholipids into which products?","choices":{"A":"Glycerol and fatty acids","B":"Cholesterol and fatty acids","C":"Monoglyceride and fatty acids","D":"Lysolecithin and fatty acids","E":"Bile acids and free glycine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Phospholipase A2, secreted as a proenzyme and activated by trypsin, converts phospholipids to lysolecithin and fatty acids. Lysolecithin then joins bile salts in emulsifying fat. The other pairs come from triglyceride or cholesterol ester hydrolysis, and bile acids with glycine come from deconjugation. Free cholesterol and fatty acid are made by cholesterol ester hydrolase acting on cholesterol ester, not by phospholipase A2. A monoglyceride and fatty acids are what pancreatic lipase makes from triglyceride, not phospholipase A2 products. Glycerol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase acting on triglyceride ester bonds. Removing glycine from bile salts is bacterial deconjugation, not phospholipase A2 action. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile salts are amphipathic, so the hydrophilic side faces water and the hydrophobic side faces the lipid core (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the end products of lipid digestion, other than glycerol, are insoluble in water and must be carried in micelles (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Free cholesterol and fatty acid are made by cholesterol ester hydrolase acting on cholesterol ester, not by phospholipase A2.","C":"A monoglyceride and fatty acids are what pancreatic lipase makes from triglyceride, not phospholipase A2 products.","A":"Glycerol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase acting on triglyceride ester bonds.","E":"Removing glycine from bile salts is bacterial deconjugation, not phospholipase A2 action."}},{"id":"sdl27_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":1,"stem":"In which organelle do enterocytes re-esterify absorbed lipid digestion products?","choices":{"A":"Rough endoplasmic reticulum","B":"Golgi secretory apparatus","C":"Apical brush-border membrane","D":"Lysosomal digestive compartment","E":"Smooth endoplasmic reticulum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Inside the enterocyte, monoglycerides, fatty acids, cholesterol, and lysolecithin are re-esterified on the smooth endoplasmic reticulum to triglycerides, cholesterol esters, and phospholipids. The Golgi packages chylomicrons into secretory vesicles afterward. Lipids cross the brush border by diffusion, and lysosomes digest heme, not lipids. Re-esterification occurs on the smooth, not rough, endoplasmic reticulum. The Golgi packages chylomicrons into secretory vesicles after re-esterification. Lipids diffuse across the brush border; re-esterification happens inside the cell. Lysosomal enzymes digest heme iron inside enterocytes; they do not re-esterify lipids. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chylomicrons carry dietary triglyceride and are cleared by lipoprotein lipase in peripheral capillaries (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: lipid crosses the apical membrane by diffusion, so absorption depends on mucosal surface area (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Re-esterification occurs on the smooth, not rough, endoplasmic reticulum.","B":"The Golgi packages chylomicrons into secretory vesicles afterward.","C":"Lipids diffuse across the brush border; re-esterification happens inside the cell.","D":"Lysosomal enzymes digest heme iron inside enterocytes; they do not re-esterify lipids."}},{"id":"sdl27_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":1,"stem":"Chylomicrons leave the intestinal villus by entering which vessels?","choices":{"A":"Portal venules","B":"Villus blood capillaries","C":"Hepatic sinusoids","D":"Lymphatic lacteals","E":"Mesenteric arterioles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chylomicrons are about 1000 Å across, too large to enter vascular capillaries, so they move between endothelial cells into lacteals and reach the blood through the thoracic duct. Portal venules and villus capillaries carry water-soluble nutrients. Hepatic sinusoids and mesenteric arterioles are not the exit route. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: injury to the thoracic duct leaks chylomicron-rich lymph, giving milky fluid high in triglyceride (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: glycerol is water soluble and does not need micelles for absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Portal venules carry water-soluble nutrients.","B":"Villus capillaries carry water-soluble nutrients.","C":"Hepatic sinusoids are not the exit route from the villus.","E":"Mesenteric arterioles carry blood toward the gut, not absorbed lipid away from it."}},{"id":"sdl27_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":1,"stem":"What happens to the bile salts of a micelle when its lipids are absorbed at the brush border?","choices":{"A":"They enter the enterocyte with the lipids","B":"They are packaged into chylomicrons","C":"They remain in the intestinal lumen","D":"They are re-esterified in the cytosol","E":"They are degraded by brush-border enzymes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Micelles release lipids at the apical membrane, but the micelles themselves do not enter the cell. The bile salts stay in the lumen and are absorbed downstream in the ileum. They are not part of chylomicrons, not re-esterified, and not degraded at the brush border. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mixed micelles are disks about 50 Å across with bile salts lining the outside (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: bile salt reuse through the enterohepatic circulation keeps the pool large without heavy hepatic synthesis (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Micelles do not enter the cell; only their lipids diffuse in.","B":"Chylomicrons contain re-esterified lipids and apoproteins.","D":"Re-esterification is a step for absorbed lipid products, not for bile salts.","E":"Brush-border enzymes do not degrade the bile salts."}},{"id":"sdl27_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":1,"stem":"In which intestinal segment are conjugated bile salts actively reabsorbed?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Proximal jejunum","C":"Ascending colon","D":"Gastric antrum","E":"Terminal ileum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Conjugated bile salts are reabsorbed in the ileum and returned to the liver in portal blood, the enterohepatic circulation. About 95 percent of secreted bile acids return this way. The duodenum and jejunum are where bile salts work on lipids. The colon and stomach do not reabsorb them. The duodenum is where bile salts work on lipids; their recirculation step is in the terminal ileum. Bile salts stay in high concentration through the jejunum because reabsorption is located in the terminal ileum. The recirculation step is located in the terminal small intestine, not the colon. The gastric antrum contains pyloric glands with G cells and mucous cells that secrete gastrin and mucus, and it is not a site of bile salt reabsorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Bile; Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 7α-hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: Crohn disease of the terminal ileum can interrupt bile salt reabsorption and cause fat malabsorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The duodenum is where bile salts work on lipids; their recirculation step is in the terminal ileum.","B":"Bile salts stay in high concentration through the jejunum because reabsorption is located in the terminal ileum.","C":"The recirculation step is located in the terminal small intestine, not the colon.","D":"The gastric antrum contains pyloric glands with G cells and mucous cells that secrete gastrin and mucus, and it is not a site of bile salt reabsorption."}},{"id":"sdl27_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does surgical removal of the intestinal segment that reabsorbs bile salts shrink the total bile salt pool?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic synthesis stops after resection","B":"Hepatic synthesis lags behind fecal loss","C":"Bile salts are deconjugated in the bile","D":"Gallbladder storage is lost to the colon","E":"Micelles enter the ileal wall intact"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Resecting the reabsorptive segment interrupts the enterohepatic circulation, so bile salts go out in feces. The liver increases synthesis, but synthesis falls short of the fecal loss and the pool shrinks. Synthesis rises rather than stopping. Deconjugation is a bacterial overgrowth mechanism. Gallbladder storage is unaffected, and micelles do not enter cells. After ileal resection, bile acid synthesis is strongly stimulated rather than stopped. Deconjugation is a bacterial overgrowth mechanism, not a result of ileal loss. Micelles do not enter the intestinal cells; only their lipids diffuse in. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ileal bile salt malabsorption from short bowel or Crohn disease can cause fat malabsorption and bile acid diarrhea (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: a smaller bile salt pool limits micelle formation and causes steatorrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"After ileal resection, bile acid synthesis is strongly stimulated rather than stopped.","C":"Deconjugation is a bacterial overgrowth mechanism, not a result of ileal loss.","E":"Micelles do not enter the intestinal cells; only their lipids diffuse in."}},{"id":"sdl27_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":5,"stem":"By which intestinal level are most ingested lipids absorbed?","choices":{"A":"Mid-jejunum","B":"Terminal ileum","C":"Gastric antrum","D":"Proximal colon","E":"Rectosigmoid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Most dietary lipid is absorbed by the mid-jejunum, so the work of bile salts is done long before they reach the terminal ileum for reabsorption. The ileum recycles bile salts rather than absorbing most fat, and the stomach and colon absorb little lipid. The gastric antrum contains G cells and mucous cells that secrete gastrin and mucus, and it is not a site of dietary lipid absorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: short bowel syndrome follows extensive small bowel resection and causes malabsorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, short bowel syndrome). COMLEX: bile salts left in the lumen after lipid uptake travel on to the ileum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"B":"The terminal ileum reabsorbs bile salts rather than most dietary fat.","D":"The proximal colon absorbs little lipid.","E":"The rectosigmoid absorbs little lipid.","C":"The gastric antrum contains G cells and mucous cells that secrete gastrin and mucus, and it is not a site of dietary lipid absorption."}},{"id":"sdl27_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cells secrete intrinsic factor?","choices":{"A":"Gastric chief cells","B":"Pancreatic acinar cells","C":"Gastric parietal cells","D":"Salivary gland cells","E":"Duodenal S cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Intrinsic factor is a glycoprotein secreted by gastric parietal cells, the same cells that secrete H+. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen, acinar cells secrete pancreatic enzymes, salivary glands secrete R proteins, and S cells secrete secretin. Gastric chief cells secrete pepsinogen; intrinsic factor comes from parietal cells. Pancreatic acinar cells secrete digestive enzymes. Salivary juices supply R proteins, not intrinsic factor. S cells of the duodenum secrete secretin in response to H+ and fatty acids in the lumen, not intrinsic factor. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune destruction of parietal cells causes chronic gastritis and pernicious anemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products). COMLEX: after gastrectomy, oral B12 is not absorbed and B12 must be given by injection (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gastric chief cells secrete pepsinogen; intrinsic factor comes from parietal cells.","B":"Pancreatic acinar cells secrete digestive enzymes.","D":"Salivary juices supply R proteins, not intrinsic factor.","E":"S cells of the duodenum secrete secretin in response to H+ and fatty acids in the lumen, not intrinsic factor."}},{"id":"sdl27_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":1,"stem":"Free vitamin B12 released in the stomach first binds to which carrier?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic factor","B":"R protein","C":"Transferrin","D":"Apoferritin","E":"Calbindin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Free B12 binds R proteins from salivary secretions. In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases degrade R proteins and B12 moves to intrinsic factor. Transferrin and apoferritin carry iron, and calbindin binds calcium. Vitamin B12 moves onto intrinsic factor only in the duodenum, after pancreatic proteases degrade the R proteins it first binds. Transferrin is the plasma β-globulin that carries absorbed iron, not a vitamin B12 binder. Apoferritin binds free iron inside intestinal cells; it does not carry vitamin B12. Calbindin D-28K is the vitamin D-induced Ca²⁺-binding protein of intestinal cells, not a vitamin B12 carrier. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency can impair B12 absorption as well as fat absorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: most water-soluble vitamins use sodium-dependent cotransport, unlike B12 (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Vitamin B12 moves onto intrinsic factor only in the duodenum, after pancreatic proteases degrade the R proteins it first binds.","C":"Transferrin is the plasma β-globulin that carries absorbed iron, not a vitamin B12 binder.","D":"Apoferritin binds free iron inside intestinal cells; it does not carry vitamin B12.","E":"Calbindin D-28K is the vitamin D-induced Ca²⁺-binding protein of intestinal cells, not a vitamin B12 carrier."}},{"id":"sdl27_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":1,"stem":"What event in the duodenum allows vitamin B12 to bind intrinsic factor?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic proteases","B":"Gastric hydrochloric acid","C":"Conjugated bile salts","D":"Brush-border lactase","E":"Salivary amylase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases degrade R proteins, so B12 transfers to intrinsic factor. The B12-intrinsic factor complex resists these proteases and travels to the ileum. Acid and pepsin act earlier in the stomach, and bile salts, lactase, and amylase do not free B12. Gastric pepsin releases vitamin B12 from food, but its transfer to intrinsic factor requires pancreatic proteases degrading the R proteins in the duodenum. Bile salts do not free B12. Lactase is a brush-border carbohydrate enzyme; B12 transfer requires protease degradation of R proteins. Salivary amylase digests starch; B12 transfer requires pancreatic protease degradation of R proteins. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia with neurologic findings (Guyton Ch. 72). COMLEX: intrinsic factor is needed for B12 uptake in the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Gastric pepsin releases vitamin B12 from food, but its transfer to intrinsic factor requires pancreatic proteases degrading the R proteins in the duodenum.","C":"Bile salts do not free B12.","D":"Lactase is a brush-border carbohydrate enzyme; B12 transfer requires protease degradation of R proteins.","E":"Salivary amylase digests starch; B12 transfer requires pancreatic protease degradation of R proteins."}},{"id":"sdl27_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Active vitamin D increases intestinal calcium absorption mainly by inducing which protein?","choices":{"A":"Apoferritin storage protein","B":"Transferrin carrier","C":"Pancreatic colipase","D":"Calbindin D-28K","E":"Intrinsic factor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol induces the vitamin D-dependent calcium-binding protein calbindin D-28K in enterocytes, which promotes calcium absorption. Apoferritin and transferrin handle iron, colipase helps lipase, and intrinsic factor binds B12. Apoferritin binds free iron inside intestinal cells; it is not the vitamin D-induced calcium-binding protein. Transferrin is the plasma carrier of absorbed iron, not the protein that vitamin D induces for calcium absorption. Intrinsic factor is the parietal-cell glycoprotein needed for ileal vitamin B12 absorption, not for calcium. Colipase displaces bile salts to help lipase; it is not the vitamin D-induced protein. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inadequate calcium absorption causes rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: 25-hydroxycholecalciferol is the main circulating but inactive form of vitamin D (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Apoferritin binds free iron inside intestinal cells; it is not the vitamin D-induced calcium-binding protein.","B":"Transferrin is the plasma carrier of absorbed iron, not the protein that vitamin D induces for calcium absorption.","E":"Intrinsic factor is the parietal-cell glycoprotein needed for ileal vitamin B12 absorption, not for calcium.","C":"Colipase displaces bile salts to help lipase; it is not the vitamin D-induced protein."}},{"id":"sdl27_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"How do absorbed vitamins A, D, E, and K leave the enterocyte?","choices":{"A":"Bound to albumin in portal blood","B":"By sodium cotransport into blood","C":"In micelles across the basal membrane","D":"Bound to transferrin in portal blood","E":"In chylomicrons into lymph"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fat-soluble vitamins are handled like dietary lipids. They travel in micelles, diffuse into the cell, are incorporated into chylomicrons, and are extruded into lymph. Sodium cotransport handles most water-soluble vitamins, micelles do not enter the cell, and transferrin carries iron. Fat-soluble vitamins leave in chylomicrons via lymph, not bound to albumin in portal blood. Na+-dependent cotransport absorbs most water-soluble vitamins, not vitamins A, D, E, and K. Micelles do not enter the cell. Transferrin carries iron in the circulation, not fat-soluble vitamins. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: orlistat inhibits gastric and pancreatic lipase and decreases absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (First Aid Gastrointestinal, orlistat). COMLEX: vitamin K deficiency prolongs the prothrombin time (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Fat-soluble vitamins leave in chylomicrons via lymph, not bound to albumin in portal blood.","B":"Na+-dependent cotransport absorbs most water-soluble vitamins, not vitamins A, D, E, and K.","C":"Micelles do not enter the cell.","D":"Transferrin carries iron in the circulation, not fat-soluble vitamins."}},{"id":"sdl27_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"How is heme iron converted to free iron inside the enterocyte?","choices":{"A":"Digestion by pancreatic proteases","B":"Digestion by lysosomal enzymes","C":"Hydrolysis by brush-border peptidases","D":"Cleavage by gastric pepsin","E":"Release by bile salt micelles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Heme iron crosses the apical membrane intact. Inside the enterocyte, lysosomal enzymes digest heme and release free iron, which binds apoferritin and then crosses the basolateral membrane. The other enzymes act in the lumen, and micelles carry lipids. Heme crosses the apical membrane intact and is digested inside the cell by lysosomal enzymes, not luminal pancreatic proteases. Heme is digested inside the cell by lysosomal enzymes, not by brush-border peptidases. Heme is digested inside the enterocyte by lysosomal enzymes, not by gastric pepsin. Micelles carry lipid digestion products, not heme iron. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hereditary hemochromatosis increases intestinal iron absorption through low hepcidin (First Aid Hematology, hemochromatosis). COMLEX: transferrin carries absorbed iron to the liver and bone marrow (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Heme crosses the apical membrane intact and is digested inside the cell by lysosomal enzymes, not luminal pancreatic proteases.","C":"Heme is digested inside the cell by lysosomal enzymes, not by brush-border peptidases.","D":"Heme is digested inside the enterocyte by lysosomal enzymes, not by gastric pepsin.","E":"Micelles carry lipid digestion products, not heme iron."}},{"id":"sdl27_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman starts a weight-loss drug that inhibits the enzymes splitting triglyceride in the stomach and duodenum. She has never had pancreatitis. One month later she has oily, foul-smelling stools and a soft abdomen. Stool fat is 14 g/day (reference range: <7 g/day). Which digestive step is most directly impaired?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis of dietary triglyceride","B":"Exocytosis of chylomicrons into lymph","C":"Synthesis of apolipoprotein B in enterocytes","D":"Reabsorption of bile salts in the ileum","E":"Activation of procolipase by trypsin enzyme"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Blocking lipase leaves dietary triglyceride unhydrolyzed, so fat appears in the stool. A normal abdomen and no pancreatic history keep the defect at that luminal hydrolysis step. Chylomicron export into lymph happens only after triglyceride has already been split and absorbed. Apoprotein synthesis inside enterocytes is downstream of the hydrolysis this drug blocks. Ileal bile-salt reabsorption is not the enzyme this weight-loss drug inhibits. Activation of procolipase by trypsin is a separate step and is not the drug target.","boardPrep":"Step 1: orlistat causes steatorrhea and decreased absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (First Aid Gastrointestinal, orlistat). COMLEX: lingual and gastric lipases normally digest only about 10 percent of triglyceride (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Chylomicron export into lymph happens only after triglyceride has","C":"Apoprotein synthesis inside enterocytes is downstream of the hydrolysis","D":"Ileal bile-salt reabsorption is not the enzyme this weight-loss drug","E":"Activation of procolipase by trypsin is a separate step"}},{"id":"sdl27_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":5,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has recurrent duodenal ulcers, diarrhea, and greasy stools. Serum alkaline phosphatase is 78 U/L (reference range: 40–129 U/L), and ultrasound shows no biliary dilation. The abdomen is soft, with mild epigastric tenderness. Fasting gastrin is 1,200 pg/mL (reference range: <100 pg/mL), duodenal aspirate pH is 3 (reference range: 6–7), and measured exocrine enzyme output is normal. Which mechanism best explains the fat malabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Loss of hepatic bile salt secretion","B":"Defective chylomicron assembly in enterocytes","C":"Excess colipase release from acini","D":"Reduced ileal absorptive surface","E":"Acid inactivation of pancreatic lipase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Excess gastrin keeps duodenal pH near 3, and that acid inactivates pancreatic lipase even though enzyme output is normal. Ulcers plus a gastrin of 1,200 pg/mL identify the acid load. Hepatic bile-salt secretion is intact, and the problem is the low duodenal pH. Enterocytes assemble chylomicrons normally once lipid products are formed. Colipase helps lipase work and is not being released in excess as the cause. The ileal surface is intact, and enzyme output was measured as normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is a gastrin-secreting tumor, often of the pancreas or duodenum (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: a proton pump inhibitor reduces the H+ load delivered to the duodenum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and gastrin","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"A":"Hepatic bile-salt secretion is intact, and the problem is","B":"Enterocytes assemble chylomicrons normally once lipid products are","C":"Colipase helps lipase work and is not being released","D":"The ileal surface is intact, and enzyme output was"}},{"id":"sdl27_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has painless jaundice, dark urine, pale bulky stools, and a 6 kg weight loss. He has never had pancreatitis. The abdomen is soft, and the gallbladder is palpable and nontender. A mass blocks the common bile duct. Total bilirubin is 9.2 mg/dL (reference range: 0.2–1.2 mg/dL), CA 19-9 is 240 U/mL (reference range: <37 U/mL), and pancreatic enzyme secretion is normal. Which mechanism best explains the steatorrhea?","choices":{"A":"Loss of pancreatic lipase activity in the duodenal lumen","B":"Failure of trypsin to activate procolipase","C":"Failure of emulsification and micelle formation","D":"Defective apoprotein synthesis in enterocytes","E":"Obstruction of intestinal lymphatic drainage"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile salts fail to form micelles because bile never reaches the duodenum, while pancreatic enzyme secretion stays normal. Jaundice, a bile-duct mass, and an elevated CA 19-9 mark that block. Pancreatic lipase output was measured as normal, so lipase activity is not the missing piece. Trypsin is still available to activate procolipase, because enzyme secretion is intact. Enterocyte apoprotein synthesis is not interrupted by a bile-duct mass. Intestinal lymphatics are open, and the missing factor is bile, not lymph flow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pale stools and dark urine point to obstructed bile flow with conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: bile salts are amphipathic, which lets them line micelles in the aqueous lumen (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile formation and micelles","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pancreatic lipase output was measured as normal, so lipase","B":"Trypsin is still available to activate procolipase, because enzyme","D":"Enterocyte apoprotein synthesis is not interrupted by a bile-duct","E":"Intestinal lymphatics are open, and the missing factor is"}},{"id":"sdl27_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-month-old boy, born at term and breastfed, has poor growth and pale, foul-smelling stools. There is no jaundice. He is small, with generalized edema and a soft abdomen. The blood smear shows acanthocytes, fasting triglyceride is 8 mg/dL (reference range: 30–150 mg/dL), and duodenal enterocytes are filled with lipid droplets. Which defect best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"Failure of micelle formation","B":"Inactive pancreatic lipase","C":"Loss of ileal bile salt reabsorption","D":"Failure of chylomicron assembly","E":"Deficient brush-border lactase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Enterocytes full of lipid with acanthocytes and a triglyceride of 8 mg/dL mean chylomicrons cannot be assembled for export. Fat is absorbed into the cell and then trapped there. Micelles are forming, because lipid has already entered the enterocytes. Pancreatic lipase is working, or the cells would not be packed with re-esterified lipid. Ileal bile-salt reabsorption is intact in this mucosal assembly defect. Lactase deficiency causes osmotic diarrhea from milk sugar, not lipid-laden enterocytes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: abetalipoproteinemia presents with acanthocytes, steatorrhea, and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: apoproteins cover about 20 percent of the chylomicron surface (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 5 — abetalipoproteinemia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Micelles are forming, because lipid has already entered the","B":"Pancreatic lipase is working, or the cells would not","C":"Ileal bile-salt reabsorption is intact in this mucosal assembly","E":"Lactase deficiency causes osmotic diarrhea from milk sugar, not lipid-laden"}},{"id":"sdl27_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man develops a milky right pleural effusion five days after esophagectomy, once he resumes a regular diet. He is afebrile, and breath sounds are reduced at the right base. Pleural-fluid triglyceride is 420 mg/dL (reference range: <110 mg/dL). He is placed on medium-chain triglycerides in place of long-chain fats. Why does this diet reduce the leak?","choices":{"A":"Medium-chain fats enter portal blood directly","B":"Medium-chain fats bypass bile salt emulsification","C":"Medium-chain fats increase lacteal reabsorption","D":"Medium-chain fats block chylomicron uptake by tissues","E":"Medium-chain fats seal the thoracic duct wall"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Medium-chain fats are water soluble enough to enter portal blood directly, so less lipid travels through the injured thoracic duct. A milky effusion with a triglyceride of 420 mg/dL is chyle from that duct. Medium-chain fats still interact with bile salts, and bypassing emulsification is not why the leak falls. They do not increase lacteal uptake, which would send more fat into the injured duct. They do not block tissue uptake of chylomicrons as the reason the pleural leak slows. They do not seal the duct wall.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the thoracic duct carries intestinal lymph and chylomicrons into the left subclavian vein (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: chylomicrons are too large to enter vascular capillaries (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus lacteals","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Medium-chain fats still interact with bile salts, and bypassing","C":"They do not increase lacteal uptake, which would send","D":"They do not block tissue uptake of chylomicrons as","E":"They do not seal the duct"}},{"id":"sdl27_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman has bloating, weight loss, and greasy stools. A sister has type 1 diabetes, and she eats wheat daily. She is thin, without jaundice. Hemoglobin is 10.4 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL), IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is 110 U/mL (reference range: <4 U/mL), and anti-endomysial antibody is positive. Duodenal biopsy shows villous atrophy, and pancreatic function is normal. Which mechanism best explains the impaired fat absorption?","choices":{"A":"Absent pancreatic lipase secretion into the duodenum","B":"Reduced mucosal surface for lipid diffusion","C":"Deficient apoprotein B synthesis in enterocytes","D":"Loss of bile salt conjugation","E":"Blocked lacteal drainage by tumor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Villous atrophy shrinks the mucosal surface across which lipid products diffuse. A high anti-tissue transglutaminase titer, endomysial antibody, and normal pancreatic function place the defect on that lost surface. Pancreatic lipase secretion is normal, so enzyme absence is not the mechanism. Apoprotein B synthesis fails in a different disease, with lipid trapped in enterocytes rather than flat villi. Bile-salt conjugation is a hepatic step and is intact here. No lymphatic tumor is blocking lacteal drainage in this mucosal injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease mainly affects the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum and is linked to iron deficiency anemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac disease gives an abnormal D-xylose test, unlike pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus structure and brush border","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pancreatic lipase secretion is normal, so enzyme absence is","C":"Apoprotein B synthesis fails in a different disease, with","D":"Bile-salt conjugation is a hepatic step and is intact","E":"No lymphatic tumor is blocking lacteal drainage in this mucosal"}},{"id":"sdl27_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":5,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman had 100 cm of terminal ileum removed for chronic stricturing ileitis with transmural inflammation. She now has greasy stools and flank pain. IgA ASCA is positive and p-ANCA is negative. Stone analysis shows calcium oxalate, and serum calcium is 9.1 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.2 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains the kidney stone?","choices":{"A":"Increased colonic oxalate absorption","B":"Increased hepatic bile acid synthesis","C":"Decreased renal calcium filtration","D":"Increased intestinal calcium uptake","E":"Decreased colonic bacterial overgrowth"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loss of the terminal ileum lets unabsorbed fatty acids bind calcium, so free oxalate is absorbed in the colon and forms a calcium oxalate stone. Positive ASCA with a negative p-ANCA fits the ileitis that required the resection. The liver does increase bile-acid synthesis after ileal loss, but that does not precipitate the oxalate stone. Renal calcium filtration is not reduced, and the serum calcium is normal. Intestinal calcium uptake is not increased; calcium is trapped in the lumen by fatty acids. Bacterial overgrowth is not decreased by this resection, and that is not the stone mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fat malabsorption from ileal bile salt loss causes calcium to bind fat, so oxalate is absorbed and forms calcium oxalate stones (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: about 95 percent of secreted bile acids normally return to the liver (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"B":"The liver does increase bile-acid synthesis after ileal loss,","C":"Renal calcium filtration is not reduced, and the serum","D":"Intestinal calcium uptake is not increased; calcium is trapped","E":"Bacterial overgrowth is not decreased by this resection, and"}},{"id":"sdl27_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man had 30 cm of terminal ileum removed after a penetrating injury. He now has watery diarrhea several times a day without weight loss, and it improves when he takes a bile-acid binder. Stool fat is 6 g/day (reference range: <7 g/day), and serum albumin is 4.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Which mechanism best explains the diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Undigested lactose holding water in the lumen","B":"Gastrin-driven acid overload of the duodenum","C":"Bile acids stimulating colonic secretion","D":"Bacterial deconjugation in the jejunum","E":"Loss of colonic surface for water uptake"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A limited ileal resection lets bile acids spill into the colon, where they stimulate secretion, and a binder stops the diarrhea. Normal stool fat shows this is not fat maldigestion. Lactose is not the unabsorbed solute, and the diarrhea responds to a bile-acid binder. Gastrin-driven acid is not produced by loss of a short ileal segment. Bacterial deconjugation in the jejunum causes steatorrhea, and stool fat here is normal. The colonic surface is intact, and the stimulus is bile acid reaching it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ileal bile salt malabsorption can cause bile acid diarrhea as well as fat malabsorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: longer ileal resections deplete the pool faster than the liver can replace it, adding steatorrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lactose is not the unabsorbed solute, and the diarrhea","B":"Gastrin-driven acid is not produced by loss of a","D":"Bacterial deconjugation in the jejunum causes steatorrhea, and stool","E":"The colonic surface is intact, and the stimulus is"}},{"id":"sdl27_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":5,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has bloating, weight loss, and greasy stools years after surgery that left a blind loop of small bowel. The abdomen is mildly distended. A hydrogen breath test rises soon after glucose, and vitamin B12 is 160 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Which mechanism best explains the fat malabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Acid inactivation of pancreatic lipase","B":"Loss of the ileal bile salt carrier","C":"Failure of chylomicron export to lymph","D":"Bacterial deconjugation of bile salts","E":"Autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bacteria in the blind loop strip glycine and taurine from bile salts, so the salts are absorbed too early and micelles fail. An early hydrogen rise and a low B12 are the bacterial clues. Acid inactivation of lipase is the gastrinoma mechanism, and there is no ulcer history. The ileal bile-salt carrier itself is present; bacteria are destroying the salts. Chylomicron export is intact once micelles deliver lipid to the mucosa. Intrinsic factor is not lost in a blind-loop syndrome, though bacteria consume B12.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bacterial overgrowth can cause B12 deficiency because bacteria consume the vitamin (Guyton Ch. 72). COMLEX: low luminal pH also promotes early bile acid absorption by converting bile acids to the nonionized form (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"A":"Acid inactivation of lipase is the gastrinoma mechanism, and","B":"The ileal bile-salt carrier itself is present; bacteria are","C":"Chylomicron export is intact once micelles deliver lipid to the","E":"Intrinsic factor is not lost in a blind-loop syndrome,"}},{"id":"sdl27_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has fatigue and numbness in both feet. She eats a mixed diet and has had no ileal surgery. She is pale, and vibration sense is reduced at the ankles. Hemoglobin is 9.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL), mean corpuscular volume is 112 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), and antibodies to parietal cells are positive. Which route of vitamin B12 replacement is most reliable?","choices":{"A":"Oral tablets taken with food","B":"Oral tablets with antacid","C":"Sublingual tablets at bedtime","D":"Oral liquid with pancreatic enzymes","E":"Intramuscular injection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Parietal-cell antibodies remove intrinsic factor, so B12 taken by mouth is not absorbed and must be injected. A macrocytic anemia with numb feet and those antibodies defines the need for that route. Oral tablets with food still require intrinsic factor, which she no longer makes. An antacid does not replace the missing intrinsic factor. A sublingual tablet is still an enteral dose and is not reliable without intrinsic factor. Pancreatic enzymes do not restore gastric intrinsic factor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pernicious anemia reflects autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor from parietal cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products). COMLEX: B12 deficiency also causes neurologic findings, which folate does not correct (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Oral tablets with food still require intrinsic factor, which","B":"An antacid does not replace the missing intrinsic","C":"A sublingual tablet is still an enteral dose and","D":"Pancreatic enzymes do not restore gastric intrinsic"}},{"id":"sdl27_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man had 90 cm of terminal ileum removed four years ago for chronic transmural ileitis. He now has fatigue and paresthesias and has taken no B12. He is pale, ankle vibration is reduced, and a gastric biopsy is normal. Mean corpuscular volume is 114 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), serum B12 is 110 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL), IgA ASCA is positive, and p-ANCA is negative. Which step of B12 absorption is lost?","choices":{"A":"Release of B12 from food by pepsin","B":"Ileal uptake of the B12-intrinsic factor complex","C":"Binding of free B12 to R proteins","D":"Gastric secretion of intrinsic factor by parietal cells","E":"Protease cleavage of R proteins in the duodenum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The resected ileum is where the B12-intrinsic factor complex is taken up, and that step is gone. A normal gastric biopsy and positive ASCA keep the defect at the ileum rather than at intrinsic factor. Pepsin release of B12 from food is a gastric step, and the stomach was normal. Binding of free B12 to R proteins still occurs in the stomach. Parietal cells still secrete intrinsic factor, as the gastric biopsy is normal. Proteases still cleave R proteins in the duodenum, which was not resected.","boardPrep":"Step 1: intrinsic factor is needed for B12 uptake in the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products). COMLEX: body B12 stores last years, so deficiency appears long after resection (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pepsin release of B12 from food is a gastric","C":"Binding of free B12 to R proteins still occurs","D":"Parietal cells still secrete intrinsic factor, as the gastric","E":"Proteases still cleave R proteins in the duodenum, which"}},{"id":"sdl27_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with years of heavy alcohol use has greasy stools and weight loss. He has had no gastric surgery and is not taking enzyme capsules. He is thin, with mild epigastric tenderness. Mean corpuscular volume is 106 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), serum B12 is 170 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL), and a gastric biopsy is normal. Which step of B12 absorption is most likely impaired?","choices":{"A":"Release of B12 from food in the stomach","B":"Secretion of intrinsic factor by parietal cells","C":"Uptake of the complex in the terminal ileum","D":"Degradation of R proteins in the duodenum","E":"Binding of B12 to transferrin in plasma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pancreatic proteases normally degrade R proteins so B12 can transfer to intrinsic factor, and that duodenal step fails when enzyme output is lost. A normal gastric biopsy excludes loss of intrinsic factor. Release of B12 from food by pepsin is a gastric step, and the stomach is normal. Parietal cells are present on the normal gastric biopsy. The terminal ileum is intact and would take up the complex if it were formed. Transferrin carries iron in plasma and does not bind B12.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption of fat, fat-soluble vitamins, and vitamin B12 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: pancreatic insufficiency gives a normal D-xylose test because the mucosa is intact (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Release of B12 from food by pepsin is a","B":"Parietal cells are present on the normal gastric","C":"The terminal ileum is intact and would take up","E":"Transferrin carries iron in plasma and does not bind"}},{"id":"sdl27_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man on hemodialysis has bone pain and muscle cramps. He eats a normal diet and has had no intestinal surgery. The shins are tender. Serum calcium is 7.6 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.2 mg/dL), 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal, and creatinine is 8.4 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains the low intestinal calcium absorption?","choices":{"A":"Deficient hepatic 25-hydroxylation","B":"Deficient renal 1α-hydroxylation","C":"Excess intestinal transferrin synthesis","D":"Loss of intrinsic factor secretion","E":"Impaired chylomicron formation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Failed kidneys cannot perform 1-alpha hydroxylation, so active vitamin D is low even though 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal. Bone pain on hemodialysis with a creatinine of 8.4 mg/dL localizes the block to the kidney. Hepatic 25-hydroxylation is intact, because that metabolite is normal. Intestinal transferrin synthesis does not control calcium absorption. Intrinsic factor is for B12, not for calcium. Chylomicron formation is unrelated to this renal vitamin D step.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in adults, inadequate calcium absorption causes osteomalacia (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: active vitamin D acts on intestine, kidney, and bone (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 5 — calcium absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hepatic 25-hydroxylation is intact, because that metabolite is","C":"Intestinal transferrin synthesis does not control calcium","D":"Intrinsic factor is for B12, not for","E":"Chylomicron formation is unrelated to this renal vitamin D"}},{"id":"sdl27_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has fatigue, joint pain, and darkening skin. He has never received a transfusion. The liver is enlarged and the skin is bronze. Transferrin saturation is 78% (reference range: 20–50%), ferritin is 1,900 ng/mL (reference range: 24–336 ng/mL), and he is homozygous for an HFE mutation. Which change best explains the iron overload?","choices":{"A":"High hepcidin increasing iron absorption","B":"Loss of lysosomal heme digestion","C":"Excess intrinsic factor secretion","D":"Excess vitamin D-induced calbindin","E":"Low hepcidin increasing iron absorption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The HFE mutation lowers hepcidin, and low hepcidin lets intestinal iron absorption run unchecked. Bronze skin, a saturation of 78%, and a ferritin of 1,900 ng/mL are the result. High hepcidin would decrease iron absorption, which is the opposite of this overload. Lysosomal heme digestion is not the step driven by the HFE mutation. Intrinsic factor does not regulate iron absorption. Calbindin is a vitamin D-related calcium protein and does not cause this iron load.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hereditary hemochromatosis is linked to HFE mutation on chromosome 6 (First Aid Hematology, hemochromatosis). COMLEX: iron enters enterocytes as Fe2+ or as heme iron (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 5 — iron absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"High hepcidin would decrease iron absorption, which is the","B":"Lysosomal heme digestion is not the step driven by","C":"Intrinsic factor does not regulate iron","D":"Calbindin is a vitamin D-related calcium protein and does"}},{"id":"sdl27_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl with recurrent respiratory infections and thick airway secretions has stopped her pancreatic enzyme capsules. She now bruises easily and has greasy stools. She is not taking warfarin. Scattered ecchymoses are present. The prothrombin time is 19 seconds (reference range: 11–13.5 seconds), and the platelet count is 250,000/µL (reference range: 150,000–400,000/µL). Deficiency of which vitamin best explains the bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin C","B":"Vitamin B12","C":"Vitamin K","D":"Vitamin B1","E":"Folic acid"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Missing pancreatic enzymes cause fat maldigestion and loss of vitamin K, which prolongs the prothrombin time with a normal platelet count. Easy bruising after she stopped the capsules fits that fat-soluble vitamin. Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy and does not prolong the prothrombin time. Vitamin B12 deficiency causes macrocytic anemia and neuropathy, not this coagulopathy. Vitamin B1 deficiency causes beriberi and is not the cause of the long prothrombin time. Folate deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia and does not drop the vitamin K-dependent factors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency from cystic fibrosis causes malabsorption of vitamins A, D, E, and K (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: vitamin K is needed to make clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — malabsorption and pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy and does not prolong","B":"Vitamin B12 deficiency causes macrocytic anemia and neuropathy, not this","D":"Vitamin B1 deficiency causes beriberi and is not the","E":"Folate deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia and does not drop"}},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 2-month-old girl has diarrhea, poor weight gain, and edema. She was born at term and has had no respiratory infection. Duodenal fluid after a feed contains normal lipase and bile salts, but trypsin activity is almost absent because of a congenital brush-border enzyme defect. Serum albumin is 2.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL), and the stools contain excess undigested triglyceride. Which step most directly explains the failed lipolysis?","choices":{"A":"Lipase is secreted as an inactive zymogen","B":"Bile salts cannot emulsify fat without trypsin","C":"Lingual lipase is destroyed in the duodenum","D":"Procolipase is not activated to colipase","E":"Micelles fail to form without phospholipase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Without enterokinase, trypsinogen is not activated, and trypsin is what converts procolipase to colipase. Normal lipase protein with absent trypsin and undigested triglyceride localizes the failure to that activation. Pancreatic lipase is secreted already active, so it is not waiting as a zymogen. Bile salts are present and can emulsify fat, but they are not a substitute for trypsin. Lingual lipase is not the enzyme destroyed by this brush-border defect. Micelles can still form, and the missing piece is active colipase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Colipase is secreted as procolipase, activated by trypsin, and displaces bile salts so pancreatic lipase can act (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Defects that block trypsin activation impair both protein and fat digestion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pancreatic lipase is secreted already active, so it is","B":"Bile salts are present and can emulsify fat, but","C":"Lingual lipase is not the enzyme destroyed by this brush-border","E":"Micelles can still form, and the missing piece is active"}},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old healthy man has blood drawn two hours after a large fatty breakfast. He takes no lipid-lowering drugs. Examination is normal. The plasma is milky, a fasting sample a week later is clear, and triglycerides in the fed sample are 280 mg/dL (fasting reference range: <150 mg/dL). By which route did the particles that clouded the plasma reach the blood?","choices":{"A":"Villus capillaries, then the portal vein","B":"Lacteals, then thoracic duct to veins","C":"Hepatic sinusoids after hepatocyte uptake","D":"Paracellular passage into submucosal veins","E":"Direct release into the azygos system"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chylomicrons from the meal enter lacteals and reach the veins through the thoracic duct, which is why the plasma is milky only after the fatty breakfast. A clear fasting sample shows the particles are dietary, not a fasting lipoprotein. Villus capillaries and the portal vein carry medium-chain fats and portal nutrients, not these meal chylomicrons. Hepatic sinusoids are not the first stop for chylomicrons, which bypass the portal vein. Paracellular passage into submucosal veins is not how these large particles leave the mucosa. The azygos system is not the route that delivers chylomicrons to the blood.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chylomicrons enter lacteals because they are too large for blood capillaries, then reach the blood through the thoracic duct (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Thoracic duct injury after chest surgery causes chylothorax, which worsens with long-chain dietary fat.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villus capillaries and the portal vein carry medium-chain fats","C":"Hepatic sinusoids are not the first stop for chylomicrons,","D":"Paracellular passage into submucosal veins is not how these","E":"The azygos system is not the route that delivers"}},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old man had 120 cm of distal ileum removed three years ago for chronic transmural ileitis. He now has right upper quadrant pain after fatty meals. Ultrasound shows gallbladder stones without wall thickening. IgA ASCA is positive and p-ANCA is negative. Which mechanism links the surgery to the stones?","choices":{"A":"Excess estrogen raising biliary cholesterol","B":"Reduced hepatic 7α-hydroxylase activity","C":"Lost ileal reabsorption of bile acids","D":"Bacterial infection of the biliary tree","E":"Oxalate crystals forming in the gallbladder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Loss of ileal reabsorption spills bile acids, shrinks the bile-acid pool, and lets cholesterol precipitate as stones. Positive ASCA and a long distal resection identify the missing reabsorptive segment. Estrogen is not raised by an ileal resection. Hepatic 7-alpha hydroxylase increases when bile acids are lost, rather than falling. The biliary tree is not infected as the link between the resection and the stones. Oxalate stones form in the kidney after ileal loss, not in the gallbladder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ileal disease or resection interrupts bile acid recycling and raises gallstone risk; Robbins lists it under pigment stones, while First Aid lists Crohn disease under both cholesterol and pigment stones (Robbins Ch. 18; First Aid 2025 GI pathology). COMLEX: Patients after extensive ileal resection also need monitoring for vitamin B12 deficiency and kidney stones.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Estrogen is not raised by an ileal","B":"Hepatic 7-alpha hydroxylase increases when bile acids are lost,","D":"The biliary tree is not infected as the link","E":"Oxalate stones form in the kidney after ileal loss,"}},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman had a total gastrectomy for a gastric mass seven years ago and has taken no supplements. She now has fatigue and numb feet. She is pale, and ankle vibration sense is reduced. Hemoglobin is 8.4 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL), and the mean corpuscular volume is 112 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). Which serum finding would best distinguish this anemia from folate deficiency?","choices":{"A":"Elevated serum homocysteine","B":"Low serum ferritin level","C":"Elevated serum gastrin","D":"Elevated methylmalonic acid","E":"Low transferrin saturation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"After total gastrectomy she lacks intrinsic factor, and B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid while folate deficiency does not. Numb feet years after the operation point to that B12-specific metabolite. Homocysteine rises in both B12 and folate deficiency, so it does not separate them. Ferritin is an iron marker and is not how these two megaloblastic anemias are split. Gastrin may be high after gastric surgery but does not distinguish the anemia from folate deficiency. Transferrin saturation reports iron, not the B12-versus-folate distinction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vitamin B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid and homocysteine and can cause subacute combined degeneration, whereas folate deficiency leaves methylmalonic acid normal (First Aid 2025 Biochemistry). COMLEX: Patients after total gastrectomy need lifelong parenteral vitamin B12.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Homocysteine rises in both B12 and folate deficiency, so","B":"Ferritin is an iron marker and is not how","C":"Gastrin may be high after gastric surgery but does","E":"Transferrin saturation reports iron, not the B12-versus-folate"}},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":3,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman who rarely leaves her apartment has bone pain and proximal weakness. She eats poorly and has no kidney disease. The thighs are tender. Serum calcium is 8.1 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.2 mg/dL), 25-hydroxyvitamin D is very low, PTH is elevated, and creatinine is normal. Which change most directly reduces intestinal calcium absorption?","choices":{"A":"Lack of intrinsic factor for calcium uptake","B":"Failed renal 1α-hydroxylation of vitamin D","C":"Excess hepcidin blocking calcium export","D":"Calcium bound to transferrin in the gut","E":"Less vitamin D-induced calbindin synthesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Without vitamin D she makes less calbindin, so enterocytes absorb less calcium. A shut-in patient with a very low 25-hydroxyvitamin D, a high PTH, and a normal creatinine has dietary and sunlight deficiency rather than renal failure. Intrinsic factor is required for B12, not for calcium uptake. Renal 1-alpha hydroxylation is intact, because the creatinine is normal and the missing metabolite is the 25-hydroxy form. Hepcidin regulates iron export and does not block calcium. Transferrin binds iron, not calcium in the gut lumen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Active vitamin D increases calcium absorption by inducing calbindin D-28K in intestinal cells (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Homebound older adults are at high risk for vitamin D deficiency and osteomalacia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Intrinsic factor is required for B12, not for calcium","B":"Renal 1-alpha hydroxylation is intact, because the creatinine is","C":"Hepcidin regulates iron export and does not block","D":"Transferrin binds iron, not calcium in the gut"}},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old man had a distal gastrectomy with gastrojejunostomy for ulcer disease. He now has mild steatorrhea. Fecal elastase is normal. A gastric-emptying study shows fat leaving the remnant within minutes of a meal. Which mechanism best explains the steatorrhea?","choices":{"A":"Loss of gastric lipase removes most lipolysis","B":"Bacteria deconjugate the bile salts","C":"Excess acid inactivates pancreatic lipase","D":"Villous atrophy reduces lipid uptake","E":"Fat arrives faster than enzymes can digest it"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fat leaves the small gastric remnant so quickly that it reaches the intestine before pancreatic enzymes can digest it. Normal fecal elastase shows the pancreas can still make enzymes. Gastric lipase is a minor contributor, and its loss is not most of normal lipolysis. Bile salts are not being deconjugated, and there is no blind-loop history. Duodenal acid is reduced after a distal gastrectomy, not increased. The villi are intact, and emptying, not mucosal area, is the problem.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The stomach aids lipid digestion mainly by emptying slowly, a rate that CCK further slows when fat enters the duodenum (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Rapid gastric emptying after gastric surgery can cause diarrhea and mild malabsorption.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gastric lipase is a minor contributor, and its loss","B":"Bile salts are not being deconjugated, and there is","C":"Duodenal acid is reduced after a distal gastrectomy, not","D":"The villi are intact, and emptying, not mucosal area,"}},{"id":"grid_sdl27_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":4,"stem":"A 56-year-old man with a high LDL cholesterol starts a resin that binds bile acids in the intestinal lumen. He has no liver disease and has had no intestinal surgery. Months later he has mild bloating and a soft abdomen, without jaundice. LDL cholesterol was 182 mg/dL (reference range: <130 mg/dL) before treatment. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely after treatment begins?","grid":{"headers":["Fecal bile acids","Hepatic bile acid synthesis","Vitamin K absorption"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↑","↓"],"B":["↑","↑","↓"],"C":["↑","↓","↓"],"D":["↔","↔","↓"],"E":["↓","↓","↑"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"Choice B (↑ fecal bile acids, ↑ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) is correct. Bound bile acids escape ileal reabsorption and leave in stool, interrupting the enterohepatic circulation. The liver raises synthesis to replace the loss, and a smaller luminal bile acid pool reduces micelle formation, so absorption of fat-soluble vitamin K falls. Choice A (↓ fecal bile acids, ↑ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) has the fecal loss backward; a binder increases, not decreases, stool bile acids. Choice C (↑ fecal bile acids, ↓ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) ignores the hepatic response to bile acid loss, in which reduced FXR feedback derepresses synthesis. Choice D (↔ fecal bile acids, ↔ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) fits a lipase inhibitor such as orlistat rather than a bile acid binder. Choice E (↓ fecal bile acids, ↓ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↑ vitamin K absorption) reverses every effect, as if bile acids were supplemented. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Bile; Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption; Fat-soluble vitamins). The vitamin cell is inferred from micelle dependence; the sources do not grade it for this drug. Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after ileal resection, hepatic synthesis rises but does not keep pace with fecal bile salt loss (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: bile acid loss in Crohn disease of the ileum can cause calcium oxalate kidney stones (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":1,"stem":"Bile salts returning in portal blood slow further bile acid synthesis by inhibiting which enzyme?","choices":{"A":"Cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase","B":"Pancreatic lipase","C":"Enterokinase","D":"Pepsin","E":"Salivary amylase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bile salts reabsorbed in the terminal ileum return in portal blood and inhibit cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase, matching new synthesis to fecal loss. Pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes triglyceride and is not the feedback target of returning bile salts. Enterokinase activates trypsinogen on the duodenal brush border. Pepsin is the gastric endopeptidase and is not regulated by portal bile salts. Salivary amylase starts starch digestion and is unrelated to bile acid synthesis.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes triglyceride and is not the feedback target of returning bile salts","C":"Enterokinase activates trypsinogen on the duodenal brush border","D":"Pepsin is the gastric endopeptidase and is not regulated by portal bile salts","E":"Salivary amylase starts starch digestion and is unrelated to bile acid synthesis"}},{"id":"sdl27_q32","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has greasy stools and a 5 kg weight loss. He has no ulcer history and does not drink alcohol. The abdomen is soft, and there is no jaundice. Fecal elastase is 80 µg/g (reference range: >200 µg/g) and bicarbonate is 24 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Triglyceride digestion is only partly reduced, but cholesterol esters in the lumen stay intact. Which enzyme is missing?","choices":{"A":"Salivary amylase","B":"Cholesterol ester hydrolase","C":"Pepsin","D":"Enterokinase","E":"Gastric intrinsic factor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancreatic cholesterol ester hydrolase splits cholesterol esters into free cholesterol and fatty acids. Salivary amylase digests starch and does not hydrolyze cholesterol esters. Pepsin is a gastric protease and does not cleave cholesterol esters. Enterokinase activates trypsinogen and is not the enzyme that frees cholesterol from its esters. Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12 and is not a lipase.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Salivary amylase digests starch and does not hydrolyze cholesterol esters","C":"Pepsin is a gastric protease and does not cleave cholesterol esters","D":"Enterokinase activates trypsinogen and is not the enzyme that frees cholesterol from its esters","E":"Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12 and is not a lipase"}},{"id":"sdl27_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman had 80 cm of terminal ileum removed for stricturing disease with transmural inflammation. She now has watery diarrhea after meals and pale stools. IgA anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibody is positive. Serum sodium is 136 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and albumin is 3.3 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Which apical transporter was removed with that bowel?","choices":{"A":"Epithelial sodium channel of the colon","B":"GLUT5 fructose carrier","C":"Ferroportin on the basolateral membrane of the duodenum","D":"The sodium–bile salt cotransporter","E":"PepT1 for dipeptides"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Conjugated bile salts are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum by the apical sodium–bile salt cotransporter. The epithelial sodium channel is the colonic route for sodium, not the ileal bile-salt carrier. GLUT5 carries fructose across the brush border and does not reclaim bile salts. Ferroportin exports iron from duodenal enterocytes. PepT1 carries dipeptides and tripeptides, not conjugated bile salts.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The epithelial sodium channel is the colonic route for sodium, not the ileal bile-salt carrier","B":"GLUT5 carries fructose across the brush border and does not reclaim bile salts","C":"Ferroportin exports iron from duodenal enterocytes","E":"PepT1 carries dipeptides and tripeptides, not conjugated bile salts"}},{"id":"sdl27_q34","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man had 100 cm of terminal ileum removed and then developed watery postprandial diarrhea without fever. A bile acid–binding resin stops the watery stools, but the greasy stools continue. He is afebrile and the abdomen is soft. Sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and albumin is 3.6 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Why do the greasy stools persist on the resin?","choices":{"A":"The resin increases pancreatic lipase secretion","B":"The resin restores the ileal vitamin B12 receptor","C":"The resin blocks gastric acid so pepsin stays active in the ileum","D":"The resin adds conjugated bile salts to the lumen","E":"The circulating bile salt pool is still too small for normal micelles"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A bile acid–binding resin neutralizes colonic bile acids and eases secretory diarrhea, but steatorrhea continues because the bile salt pool stays depleted. The resin does not increase pancreatic lipase output. The resin does not replace the ileal receptor for the intrinsic-factor–B12 complex. Pepsin is not kept active in the ileum by a bile acid resin, and that is not why fat malabsorption remains. The resin binds bile acids and does not replenish them.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The resin does not increase pancreatic lipase output","B":"The resin does not replace the ileal receptor for the intrinsic-factor–B12 complex","C":"Pepsin is not kept active in the ileum by a bile acid resin, and that is not why fat malabsorption remains","D":"The resin binds bile acids and does not replenish them"}}],"28":[{"id":"sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":1,"stem":"Why can a disturbance of intestinal fluid absorption deplete body fluid so rapidly?","choices":{"A":"Diarrheal fluid is hypertonic to plasma","B":"Oral intake exceeds all gut secretions","C":"Normal feces hold liters of water","D":"Daily absorbed fluid nears ECF volume","E":"Kidneys fail to conserve sodium"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Each day the intestines absorb the fluid from the diet plus the much larger volume of salivary, gastric, pancreatic, biliary, and intestinal secretions, an amount almost equal to the entire extracellular fluid volume, so failed absorption threatens body water quickly. Intestinal fluid is absorbed isosmotically, and diarrheal fluid is not described as hypertonic to plasma. Oral intake is the smaller share; gut secretions supply most of the fluid that enters the lumen. Normal feces carry a small volume of water, not liters. The kidneys respond to volume loss by activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and conserving sodium. Sources: Costanzo ch 8, intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The gut absorbs a fluid volume close to the whole ECF volume each day, mostly from secretions (Costanzo ch 8). COMLEX: Severe diarrhea calls for prompt oral or IV rehydration before shock develops.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Intestinal fluid is absorbed isosmotically, and diarrheal","B":"Oral intake is the smaller share; gut","C":"Normal feces carry a small volume of","E":"The kidneys respond to volume loss by"}},{"id":"sdl28_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the osmolarity of the fluid absorbed by the intestine compared with plasma?","choices":{"A":"Hyposmotic to plasma","B":"Isosmotic to plasma","C":"Hyperosmotic to plasma","D":"Variable with meal tonicity","E":"Hyperosmotic in the jejunum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The absorbate is isosmotic: solute and water are absorbed in proportion, as in the renal proximal tubule. It is not hyposmotic or hyperosmotic, and it does not vary with meal tonicity or segment. The absorbate is isosmotic in every segment; the jejunum differs from the ileum and colon only in which solutes it absorbs. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: jejunal transport mechanisms resemble those of the early renal proximal tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the small remaining volume of 100–200 mL is lost in feces each day (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hyposmotic to plasma is a different tonicity.","C":"Hyperosmotic to plasma is a different tonicity.","D":"Variable with meal tonicity is a different relationship.","E":"Hyperosmotic in the jejunum is a different pattern."}},{"id":"sdl28_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the first step in intestinal fluid absorption by villus cells?","choices":{"A":"Absorption of solute","B":"Absorption of water","C":"Secretion of chloride","D":"Secretion of potassium","E":"Secretion of mucus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Villus cells first absorb solute, and water follows osmotically so the absorbate stays isosmotic. Water does not move first. Chloride secretion is a crypt function, potassium secretion occurs in the colon, and mucus does not drive fluid uptake. Water absorption comes second, following solute absorption so the absorbate stays isosmotic. Chloride secretion is a crypt-cell function; villus cells absorb fluid and electrolytes. Potassium secretion occurs through apical K+ channels in the colon, not as the first step in villus absorption. Mucus is secreted by goblet cells interspersed among the villus enterocytes and does not drive absorption, which begins with solute uptake followed by water. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cell type determines direction, with villus cells absorbing and crypt cells secreting fluid (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: disease that reduces absorptive surface area, such as small intestinal infection, reduces fluid absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Water absorption comes second, following solute absorption so the absorbate stays isosmotic.","C":"Chloride secretion is a crypt-cell function; villus cells absorb fluid and electrolytes.","D":"Potassium secretion occurs through apical K+ channels in the colon, not as the first step in villus absorption.","E":"Mucus is secreted by goblet cells interspersed among the villus enterocytes and does not drive absorption, which begins with solute uptake followed by water."}},{"id":"sdl28_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":1,"stem":"The jejunum mainly produces net absorption of which salt?","choices":{"A":"Sodium chloride","B":"Potassium chloride","C":"Sodium bicarbonate","D":"Potassium bicarbonate","E":"Calcium chloride"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In the jejunum, H+ leaves on apical Na+-H+ exchange and HCO3- made by carbonic anhydrase is absorbed into blood, giving net NaHCO3 absorption. The ileum adds Cl--HCO3- exchange and absorbs net NaCl. The colon absorbs Na+ and secretes K+. Potassium chloride is not the jejunal absorbate; the colon handles K⁺, secreting it through apical K⁺ channels. Potassium bicarbonate is not absorbed in the jejunum; H⁺ is exchanged for Na⁺, so the cation absorbed with HCO₃⁻ is sodium. Net NaCl absorption is the ileal pattern, where Cl--HCO3- exchange is added; the jejunum absorbs NaHCO3. Ca2+ absorption in the small intestine depends on vitamin D-induced calbindin, not the Na+-H+ exchange that gives jejunal NaHCO3 absorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the jejunum is the major site of Na+ absorption in the small intestine (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: jejunal Na+ also enters on Na+-glucose, Na+-galactose, and Na+-amino acid cotransporters (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — SGLT1 Na⁺ cotransport and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Potassium chloride is not the jejunal absorbate; the colon handles K⁺, secreting it through apical K⁺ channels.","D":"Potassium bicarbonate is not absorbed in the jejunum.","A":"Net NaCl absorption is the ileal pattern, where Cl--HCO3- exchange is added; the jejunum absorbs NaHCO3.","E":"Ca2+ absorption in the small intestine depends on vitamin D-induced calbindin, not the Na+-H+ exchange that gives jejunal NaHCO3 absorption."}},{"id":"sdl28_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone induces synthesis of apical Na⁺ channels in colonic epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"Antidiuretic hormone","B":"Natriuretic peptide","C":"Cholecystokinin","D":"Aldosterone","E":"Somatostatin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Like renal principal cells, colonocytes have apical Na+ and K+ channels, and aldosterone induces Na+ channel synthesis. This increases Na+ absorption and, secondarily, K+ secretion. ADH acts on renal water channels, ANP promotes natriuresis, and CCK and secretin regulate pancreatic and biliary secretion. Somatostatin from D cells is inhibitory, suppressing other GI hormones and gastric H+ secretion, and does not induce colonic Na+ channels. CCK contracts the gallbladder and stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion; induction of colonic Na+ channel synthesis is the action of aldosterone. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic cells resemble the principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: increased Na+ entry raises basolateral pumping, which drives K+ into the cell for secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — SGLT1 Na⁺ cotransport and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Somatostatin from D cells is inhibitory, suppressing other GI hormones and gastric H+ secretion, and does not induce colonic Na+ channels.","C":"CCK contracts the gallbladder and stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion; induction of colonic Na+ channel synthesis is the action of aldosterone."}},{"id":"sdl28_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":1,"stem":"Which apical exchanger is present in the ileum but not the jejunum?","choices":{"A":"Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger","B":"Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchanger","C":"Na⁺-glucose cotransporter","D":"Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter","E":"H⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ileum has the jejunal mechanisms plus apical Cl--HCO3- exchange and a basolateral Cl- transporter. Combined with Na+-H+ exchange, this gives net NaCl absorption. Na+-H+ exchange and Na+-glucose cotransport are present in both segments. The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter is basolateral in crypt cells, and H+-K+ ATPase is gastric. Na+-H+ exchange is present in both the jejunum and the ileum. Na+-glucose cotransport is on the jejunal apical membrane, and the ileum contains the same mechanisms. The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter is on the basolateral membrane of crypt cells, not the ileal apical membrane. H+-K+ ATPase is the apical proton pump of gastric parietal cells that secretes H+ into the stomach, not an ileal anion exchanger. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the ileum, HCO3- is secreted into the lumen rather than absorbed into blood (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the ileal basolateral membrane has a Cl- transporter instead of an HCO3- transporter (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — SGLT1 Na⁺ cotransport and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Na+-H+ exchange is present in both the jejunum and the ileum","C":"Na+-glucose cotransport is on the jejunal apical membrane, and the ileum contains the same mechanisms.","D":"The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter is on the basolateral membrane of crypt cells, not the ileal apical membrane.","E":"H+-K+ ATPase is the apical proton pump of gastric parietal cells that secretes H+ into the stomach, not an ileal anion exchanger."}},{"id":"sdl28_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Which intestinal cells secrete chloride and water into the lumen?","choices":{"A":"Villus tip enterocytes","B":"Goblet mucus cells","C":"Paneth defensin cells","D":"Enteroendocrine I cells","E":"Crypt epithelial cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Epithelial cells lining the crypts secrete fluid and electrolytes, while cells lining the villi absorb them. Goblet cells secrete mucus, Paneth cells secrete defensins, and I cells secrete CCK. Villus cells absorb fluid and electrolytes; crypt cells are the secretory population. Goblet cells on the villi secrete mucus. I cells of the duodenal and jejunal mucosa secrete CCK in response to fatty acids and amino acids, not Cl- and water. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: crypts of Lieberkühn contain stem cells that replace enterocytes and goblet cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, digestive tract histology). COMLEX: normally villus cells reabsorb what crypt cells secrete (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — neurocrines including VIP","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villus cells absorb fluid and electrolytes; crypt cells are the secretory population.","B":"Goblet cells on the villi secrete mucus.","D":"I cells of the duodenal and jejunal mucosa secrete CCK in response to fatty acids and amino acids, not Cl- and water."}},{"id":"sdl28_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Which basolateral transporter brings chloride from the blood into chloride-secreting intestinal cells?","choices":{"A":"Na⁺-glucose cotransporter","B":"Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchanger","C":"Apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger protein","D":"Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter","E":"Basolateral K⁺ leak channel"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Crypt cells have a basolateral Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter like the one in the thick ascending limb. Cl- enters on it and exits through apical Cl- channels. Na+-glucose cotransport and Na+-H+ exchange are apical absorptive carriers, and Cl--HCO3- exchange is an apical ileal mechanism. The basolateral K⁺ leak channel only recycles K⁺ that NKCC1 brings in; it is not the carrier that loads Cl⁻ into crypt cells. Na+-glucose cotransport is an apical absorptive carrier of villus cells, not a basolateral Cl- entry route. Cl--HCO3- exchange is an apical ileal absorptive mechanism, not the basolateral Cl- loader in secreting cells. Na+-H+ exchange is an apical absorptive transporter and carries no Cl-. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the same three-ion cotransporter in the thick ascending limb is the target of loop diuretics (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: the Na+-K+ ATPase on the basolateral membrane keeps intracellular Na+ low for the cotransporter (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — neurocrines including VIP","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The basolateral K⁺ leak channel only recycles K⁺ that NKCC1 brings in; it is not the carrier that loads Cl⁻ into crypt cells.","A":"Na+-glucose cotransport is an apical absorptive carrier of villus cells, not a basolateral Cl- entry route.","B":"Cl--HCO3- exchange is an apical ileal absorptive mechanism, not the basolateral Cl- loader in secreting cells.","C":"Na+-H+ exchange is an apical absorptive transporter and carries no Cl-."}},{"id":"sdl28_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"How does sodium reach the lumen during intestinal chloride secretion?","choices":{"A":"Passively by the paracellular route","B":"Through apical Na⁺ channels into the lumen","C":"On the basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump","D":"By apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange","E":"By vesicular transcytosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cl- is secreted through apical channels, and Na+ follows passively by the paracellular route between cells. Water then follows the NaCl. Apical Na+ channels and Na+-H+ exchange move Na+ into cells, and the Na+-K+ ATPase is basolateral. The Na+-K+ ATPase pumps Na+ across the basolateral membrane toward blood, not into the lumen. Apical Na+-H+ exchange moves Na+ from the lumen into the cell, the opposite direction. Apical Na+ channels are a colonic feature that absorbs Na+ from the lumen into the cell; during Cl- secretion Na+ reaches the lumen by moving between the cells. Costanzo states that in crypt Cl- secretion Na+ follows passively by moving between the cells, not by vesicles. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leaky small-intestinal tight junctions permit substantial paracellular movement (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the colon has high-resistance tight junctions that limit paracellular flow (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — neurocrines including VIP","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The Na+-K+ ATPase pumps Na+ across the basolateral membrane toward blood, not into the lumen.","D":"Apical Na+-H+ exchange moves Na+ from the lumen into the cell, the opposite direction.","B":"Apical Na+ channels are a colonic feature that absorbs Na+ from the lumen into the cell; during Cl- secretion Na+ reaches the lumen by moving between the cells.","E":"Costanzo states that in crypt Cl- secretion Na+ follows passively by moving between the cells, not by vesicles."}},{"id":"sdl28_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Which covalent change does cholera toxin make to the αs subunit of Gs?","choices":{"A":"Tyrosine phosphorylation","B":"Glycosylation","C":"Ubiquitination","D":"Methylation","E":"ADP-ribosylation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The A subunit of cholera toxin catalyzes ADP-ribosylation of the αs subunit of the Gs protein. The other modifications are not the toxin's action. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates the αs subunit so Gs stays GTP-bound and adenylyl cyclase stays active (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the toxin enters crypt cells across the apical membrane and acts on Gs at the basolateral side (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — sodium-coupled glucose absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the direct effect of cholera toxin on the αs subunit of Gs?","choices":{"A":"Loss of its GTP binding","B":"Faster GDP rebinding","C":"Loss of its GTPase activity","D":"Faster receptor desensitizing","E":"Gain of Gi-type coupling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ADP-ribosylation inhibits the GTPase activity of αs, so GTP stays bound and the subunit stays active. GTP binding is kept, not lost. Faster GDP rebinding would end the signal, and the toxin does not change receptor desensitization or coupling. GTP binding is preserved; GTP stays permanently bound to the modified αs subunit. The toxin prevents GTP from being converted back to GDP, so no GDP rebinding occurs. Costanzo describes cholera toxin ADP-ribosylating αs so GTP stays bound and adenylyl cyclase is permanently activated, not switching to Gi coupling. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: with GTP permanently bound, cAMP stays high and apical Cl- channels stay open (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the secreted fluid volume can reach 10 to 12 L per day (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — sodium-coupled glucose absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"GTP binding is preserved; GTP stays permanently bound to the modified αs subunit.","B":"The toxin prevents GTP from being converted back to GDP, so no GDP rebinding occurs.","E":"Costanzo describes cholera toxin ADP-ribosylating αs so GTP stays bound and adenylyl cyclase is permanently activated, not switching to Gi coupling."}},{"id":"sdl28_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Why does cholera cause massive diarrhea despite intact villus cells?","choices":{"A":"Villus cells switch over to net fluid secretion","B":"Crypt secretion exceeds villus absorption","C":"Colonic water absorption is abolished","D":"Toxin destroys the villus epithelium","E":"Bacteria invade the submucosal layer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cholera stimulates crypt Cl- and fluid secretion so strongly that it overwhelms the absorptive capacity of villus cells and the colon. Villus cells keep absorbing, which is why glucose-coupled Na+ absorption still works. The toxin does not destroy the epithelium or cause invasion. Villus cells keep absorbing; the crypt secretion simply overwhelms their absorptive capacity. Colonic absorption is not abolished; it is overwhelmed by the volume secreted into the lumen. Cholera toxin acts by ADP-ribosylating Gs inside crypt cells rather than destroying the villus epithelium, and the villar cells keep absorbing but are overwhelmed. Cholera toxin enters crypt cells across the apical membrane and acts through cAMP, so the diarrhea comes from toxin-driven secretion, not bacterial invasion of the submucosa. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vibrio cholerae and some Escherichia coli strains cause secretory diarrhea through toxins (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the colon can reabsorb a maximum of about 6 to 8 L per day (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — sodium-coupled glucose absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villus cells keep absorbing; the crypt secretion simply overwhelms their absorptive capacity.","C":"Colonic absorption is not abolished; it is overwhelmed by the volume secreted into the lumen.","D":"Cholera toxin acts by ADP-ribosylating Gs inside crypt cells rather than destroying the villus epithelium, and the villar cells keep absorbing but are overwhelmed.","E":"Cholera toxin enters crypt cells across the apical membrane and acts through cAMP, so the diarrhea comes from toxin-driven secretion, not bacterial invasion of the submucosa."}},{"id":"sdl28_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":5,"stem":"Which laxative class includes lactulose and polyethylene glycol?","choices":{"A":"Osmotic laxatives","B":"Stimulant laxatives","C":"Bulk-forming laxatives","D":"Emollient laxatives","E":"Opioid antidiarrheals"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osmotic laxatives provide a nonabsorbable osmotic load that draws water into the lumen. Lactulose and polyethylene glycol act that way. Stimulant laxatives act on enteric nerves, bulk-forming agents are soluble fibers, emollients lower stool surface tension, and opioid agents slow motility. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea); First Aid Gastrointestinal (anticonstipation drugs). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lactulose also treats hepatic encephalopathy because gut bacteria acidify the colon and trap NH4+ (First Aid Gastrointestinal, anticonstipation drugs). COMLEX: bacteria degrade some nonabsorbed solutes into more osmotically active particles (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"B":"Stimulant laxatives act on enteric nerves.","C":"Bulk-forming laxatives such as psyllium and methylcellulose are indigestible hydrophilic colloids that form a gel distending the colon, not lactulose or polyethylene glycol.","D":"Stool surfactants such as docusate and glycerin soften stool by letting water and lipids penetrate, and they do not include lactulose or polyethylene glycol.","E":"Opioid antidiarrheals such as loperamide slow colonic transit and increase fecal water absorption, the opposite of a laxative."}},{"id":"sdl28_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the major cause of secretory diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Brush-border lactase deficiency","B":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","C":"Nonabsorbable laxative ingestion","D":"Loss of absorptive mucosal area","E":"Enteropathic bacterial overgrowth"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo names overgrowth of enteropathic bacteria such as Vibrio cholerae or Escherichia coli as the major cause of secretory diarrhea. Lactase deficiency and laxatives cause osmotic diarrhea, pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption, and reduced mucosal area is a separate category. Lactase deficiency leaves lactose in the lumen, where it retains water and causes osmotic, not secretory, diarrhea. Nonabsorbable solutes cause osmotic diarrhea, not secretory diarrhea. Loss of absorptive area from infection or inflammation is its own diarrhea category, separate from secretory diarrhea. Pancreatic insufficiency impairs fat digestion and causes steatorrhea, whereas secretory diarrhea is excessive crypt fluid secretion, mostly from enteropathic bacteria. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIPoma causes watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria (First Aid Gastrointestinal, VIP). COMLEX: enteritis increases mucosal secretion and motility to flush the infectious agent (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lactase deficiency leaves lactose in the lumen, where it retains water and causes osmotic, not secretory, diarrhea.","C":"Nonabsorbable solutes cause osmotic diarrhea, not secretory diarrhea.","D":"Loss of absorptive area from infection or inflammation is its own diarrhea category, separate from secretory diarrhea.","B":"Pancreatic insufficiency impairs fat digestion and causes steatorrhea, whereas secretory diarrhea is excessive crypt fluid secretion, mostly from enteropathic bacteria."}},{"id":"sdl28_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":1,"stem":"Diarrheal fluid is rich in which anion because intestinal secretions contain it?","choices":{"A":"Chloride ion","B":"Phosphate ion","C":"Bicarbonate","D":"Sulfate ion","E":"Lactate ion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Salivary, pancreatic, and intestinal juices have a high HCO3- content, so diarrheal fluid does too. Loss of HCO3- relative to Cl- causes hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. Chloride is retained relative to bicarbonate. Phosphate, sulfate, and lactate are not the named anion. Chloride is retained relative to bicarbonate, which is why diarrhea causes hyperchloremic acidosis. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea causes normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: diarrheal fluid is also high in K+ from flow-dependent colonic secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Chloride is retained relative to bicarbonate, which is why diarrhea causes hyperchloremic acidosis."}},{"id":"sdl28_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man has a high-output jejunostomy after extensive bowel resection. He drinks about 2 L a day. The stoma is pink, blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg, and the mucous membranes are dry. Stoma output is 3.5 L a day, and the blood urea nitrogen is 31 mg/dL (reference range: 7–20 mg/dL). Which best explains why output can exceed what he drinks?","choices":{"A":"Upper gut secretions add to dietary fluid","B":"Excess colonic secretion of potassium-rich fluid","C":"Renal water loss into the bowel","D":"Bacterial fermentation of dietary starch","E":"Stoma mucosa secreting mucus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Saliva, gastric juice, bile, and pancreatic juice add a large volume to what he drinks, and a jejunostomy vents that fluid before the bowel can reabsorb it. An output of 3.5 L from 2 L of intake is that added secretion. The colon, which secretes some potassium-rich fluid, is downstream of a jejunostomy and is not the source of this excess. The kidneys are not pouring water into the bowel. Bacterial fermentation of starch produces gas, not liters of stoma fluid. Mucus from the stoma is a small volume and cannot account for an extra 1.5 L.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a high-output stoma can cause dehydration and prerenal azotemia because proximal secretions are not reabsorbed (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: diarrheal loss of extracellular-type fluid lowers arterial pressure (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion adds to luminal fluid","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The colon, which secretes some potassium-rich fluid, is downstream","C":"The kidneys are not pouring water into the","D":"Bacterial fermentation of starch produces gas, not liters of stoma","E":"Mucus from the stoma is a small volume and"}},{"id":"sdl28_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man receives a hypertonic enteral feed directly into the jejunum and within the hour develops cramps and watery output. He has no fever. Serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Which process best explains the early increase in luminal fluid?","choices":{"A":"Active water pumping by crypt epithelial cells","B":"Closure of jejunal tight junctions","C":"Water entry until contents are isosmotic","D":"Toxin-driven chloride channel opening","E":"Aldosterone-driven colonic secretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Water enters the lumen until the hypertonic feed is brought to the osmolality of plasma. Cramps and watery output right after a hypertonic jejunal feed, with a normal serum sodium, are that osmotic equilibration. Crypt cells are not actively pumping water in response to a hypertonic meal. Jejunal tight junctions do not close and thereby add fluid. No toxin has opened chloride channels in this feeding complication. Aldosterone acts on the colon and does not cause this immediate jejunal response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperosmotic luminal contents draw water into the lumen until they become isosmotic (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: intestinal absorbate is isosmotic, much like proximal tubular fluid (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — duodenal osmolality and gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Crypt cells are not actively pumping water in response","B":"Jejunal tight junctions do not close and thereby add","D":"No toxin has opened chloride channels in this feeding","E":"Aldosterone acts on the colon and does not cause"}},{"id":"sdl28_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has an end ileostomy after colectomy for refractory colitis. The stoma is pink and blood pressure is 108/68 mm Hg. Effluent sodium stays near her plasma sodium of 140 mEq/L, whereas formed stool sodium is typically near 30 mEq/L. Which feature of the small intestine explains the failure to lower effluent sodium?","choices":{"A":"Absent basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase","B":"Resistance of enterocytes to aldosterone","C":"High colonic potassium secretion","D":"Bile salt-driven crypt secretion","E":"Leaky low-resistance tight junctions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Small-bowel tight junctions are leaky and low-resistance, so the lumen cannot hold a large sodium gradient the way the colon can. Ileostomy fluid therefore leaves with a sodium close to plasma. The basolateral sodium pump is present and working in this healthy stoma. Enterocytes respond to aldosterone, but the colon that would finish sodium salvage is gone. High colonic potassium secretion is a colonic event and does not set ileostomy sodium. Bile-salt-driven secretion is not why a healthy ileostomy fails to lower sodium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: aldosterone increases colonic Na+ absorption through apical Na+ channels (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: loss of the colon removes the high-resistance segment that finishes salt and water absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The basolateral sodium pump is present and working in","B":"Enterocytes respond to aldosterone, but the colon that would","C":"High colonic potassium secretion is a colonic event and","D":"Bile-salt-driven secretion is not why a healthy ileostomy fails"}},{"id":"sdl28_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":5,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman has three days of profuse watery diarrhea from norovirus. She is not taking diuretics and has not been vomiting. She is weak, the abdomen is soft, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Serum potassium is 2.8 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which mechanism most directly explains the low potassium?","choices":{"A":"Renal K⁺ wasting from loop diuretics","B":"Shift of K⁺ into cells from alkalosis","C":"Reduced dietary potassium intake","D":"Flow-dependent colonic K⁺ secretion","E":"Crypt-cell K⁺ secretion via channels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"High flow through the colon increases potassium secretion, which is why the serum potassium has fallen to 2.8 mEq/L. Watery diarrhea without diuretics or vomiting points to that flow-dependent colonic loss. She is not taking a loop diuretic, so renal wasting from that drug is not the cause. There is no alkalosis described that would shift potassium into cells. Intake has not been the limiting factor over only 3 days of diarrhea. A separate crypt-cell potassium channel is not the flow-dependent colonic secretion that dominates here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypokalemia can cause muscle weakness and flattened T waves (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: aldosterone also increases colonic K+ secretion through Na+ channel synthesis (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"A":"She is not taking a loop diuretic, so renal","B":"There is no alkalosis described that would shift potassium into","C":"Intake has not been the limiting factor over only","E":"A separate crypt-cell potassium channel is not the flow-dependent"}},{"id":"sdl28_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man has hypertension and muscle cramps. He is not taking diuretics and has no diarrhea. Blood pressure is 168/100 mm Hg. Serum potassium is 2.8 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), plasma renin is suppressed, and CT shows an adrenal adenoma. Which change in his colonic epithelium is expected?","choices":{"A":"Reduced colonic K⁺ secretion","B":"Increased opening of crypt Cl⁻ channels by cAMP","C":"Increased Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchange activity","D":"Decreased basolateral K⁺ uptake","E":"Increased apical Na⁺ channel synthesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The adenoma secretes aldosterone, which increases synthesis of apical sodium channels in the colon. Hypertension, a potassium of 2.8 mEq/L, and suppressed renin are that mineralocorticoid excess. Colonic potassium secretion increases with aldosterone, rather than falling. Crypt chloride channels opened by cyclic AMP are the secretory pathway, not this aldosterone effect. Chloride-bicarbonate exchange is not the colonic change driven by this adenoma. Basolateral potassium uptake is not decreased by aldosterone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary hyperaldosteronism causes hypertension, hypokalemia, and low renin (First Aid Endocrine). COMLEX: the colonic transport pattern mirrors the late distal tubule and collecting duct (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — colonic electrolyte transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Colonic potassium secretion increases with aldosterone, rather than","B":"Crypt chloride channels opened by cyclic AMP are the","C":"Chloride-bicarbonate exchange is not the colonic change driven by this","D":"Basolateral potassium uptake is not decreased by"}},{"id":"sdl28_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman takes acetazolamide for glaucoma. She has no diarrhea. Serum bicarbonate is 19 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which jejunal transport step is most directly reduced?","choices":{"A":"Apical Na⁺-glucose cotransport by SGLT1 carriers","B":"Supply of H⁺ for apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange","C":"Basolateral Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ uptake","D":"Apical Na⁺ channel insertion by aldosterone","E":"Paracellular water absorption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Carbonic anhydrase supplies the hydrogen ion that the apical sodium-hydrogen exchanger secretes, and inhibiting the enzyme cuts that supply. A bicarbonate of 19 mEq/L is the systemic marker of the same enzyme block. Sodium-glucose cotransport does not depend on carbonic anhydrase. Basolateral sodium-potassium-2-chloride uptake is a crypt secretory step and is not the jejunal step cut here. Aldosterone-driven sodium channels are colonic and are not the target of this glaucoma drug. Paracellular water absorption follows solute and is not the step the enzyme directly feeds.","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbonic anhydrase inhibitors cause metabolic acidosis by reducing proximal tubular HCO3- reabsorption (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: jejunal electrolyte transport is identical to that of the early proximal tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — carbonic anhydrase and H⁺/HCO₃⁻ generation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sodium-glucose cotransport does not depend on carbonic","C":"Basolateral sodium-potassium-2-chloride uptake is a crypt secretory step and","D":"Aldosterone-driven sodium channels are colonic and are not the","E":"Paracellular water absorption follows solute and is not the"}},{"id":"sdl28_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn has abdominal distension and has not passed meconium. The anus is patent. Contrast enema shows a microcolon with inspissated meconium in the distal ileum. Sweat chloride is 78 mmol/L (reference range: <30 mmol/L). Which defect best explains the thick intestinal contents?","choices":{"A":"Impaired apical Cl⁻ secretion by crypt cells","B":"Excess Na⁺ absorption by colonic channels","C":"Loss of basolateral Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ uptake","D":"Absent Na⁺-glucose cotransport on villus enterocytes","E":"Overactive apical anion exchange"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A defective cystic fibrosis channel impairs apical chloride secretion by crypt cells, so the lumen stays dry and meconium inspissates. A sweat chloride of 78 mmol/L confirms that channel defect. Colonic sodium absorption is not excessive as the cause of this thick meconium. The basolateral sodium-potassium-2-chloride cotransporter is intact, and the missing piece is the apical channel. Sodium-glucose cotransport on villus cells is preserved and is why oral rehydration still works in this disease. Anion exchange is not overactive, and the lumen is too dry rather than too alkaline from excess exchange.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystic fibrosis causes exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: Na+ follows secreted Cl- passively between cells, and water follows NaCl (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and CFTR","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Colonic sodium absorption is not excessive as the cause","C":"The basolateral sodium-potassium-2-chloride cotransporter is intact, and the missing","D":"Sodium-glucose cotransport on villus cells is preserved and is","E":"Anion exchange is not overactive, and the lumen is"}},{"id":"sdl28_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman passes about 3 L of watery stool a day, and the diarrhea continues while she fasts. She is not taking laxatives. She is weak and the abdomen is soft. Serum potassium is 2.6 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), gastric acid output is low, and chromogranin A is 340 ng/mL (reference range: <93 ng/mL). CT shows a mass in the pancreatic tail. Which mechanism best explains the diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Nonabsorbed solute holding water","B":"Bile acid stimulation of colonic epithelium","C":"Peptide-driven crypt-cell cAMP excess","D":"Mucosal invasion by bacteria","E":"Rapid transit from gastrin excess"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A peptide from the pancreatic mass drives crypt-cell cyclic AMP, opening chloride secretion that continues during fasting. Low acid output, hypokalemia, and an elevated chromogranin A fit that neuroendocrine peptide. An unabsorbed solute would stop causing diarrhea once she is fasting, and this diarrhea continues. Bile acids are not the stimulus, and there is no ileal disease. There is no mucosal invasion or fever to support an invasive bacterial diarrhea. Gastrin excess would raise gastric acid, which is low here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIPoma causes watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria (WDHA syndrome) (First Aid Gastrointestinal, VIP). COMLEX: octreotide is used to treat VIPoma (First Aid Gastrointestinal, octreotide).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — VIPoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An unabsorbed solute would stop causing diarrhea once she","B":"Bile acids are not the stimulus, and there is","D":"There is no mucosal invasion or fever to support","E":"Gastrin excess would raise gastric acid, which is low"}},{"id":"sdl28_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old farmworker has vomiting, drooling, pinpoint pupils, and profuse watery diarrhea after spraying an organophosphate. Heart rate is 48/min and bowel sounds are hyperactive. Plasma cholinesterase is 900 U/L (reference range: 3,000–8,000 U/L). Which mechanism best explains the increased fluid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Excess VIP release from a pancreatic islet tumor","B":"Excess acetylcholine at crypt cell receptors","C":"Toxin-mediated Gs activation in crypt cells","D":"Nonabsorbed pesticide holding water","E":"Loss of colonic Na⁺ channel synthesis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Organophosphate poisoning leaves excess acetylcholine at crypt-cell receptors, which drives secretion. Bradycardia, small pupils, drooling, and a cholinesterase of 900 U/L are the same cholinergic excess. A VIP-secreting islet tumor is not acquired by spraying a pesticide. Toxin-mediated activation of Gs is the cholera mechanism, not this cholinergic poisoning. The pesticide is not an unabsorbed osmotic solute holding water in the lumen. Colonic sodium-channel synthesis is an aldosterone effect and is not acutely increased here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: organophosphates irreversibly inhibit acetylcholinesterase and cause a cholinergic toxidrome (First Aid Pharmacology). COMLEX: atropine blocks muscarinic effects and pralidoxime regenerates acetylcholinesterase (First Aid Pharmacology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system and autonomic input","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A VIP-secreting islet tumor is not acquired by spraying a","C":"Toxin-mediated activation of Gs is the cholera mechanism, not","D":"The pesticide is not an unabsorbed osmotic solute holding","E":"Colonic sodium-channel synthesis is an aldosterone effect and is"}},{"id":"sdl28_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old aid worker has profuse rice-water stools two days after drinking untreated water. Blood pressure is 92/58 mm Hg and the pulse is 120/min. The abdomen is soft. Stool culture grows a comma-shaped gram-negative rod, and serum potassium is 2.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which intracellular messenger remains persistently high in his crypt cells?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Inositol trisphosphate","D":"Diacylglycerol","E":"Nitric oxide"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cholera toxin keeps cyclic AMP high in crypt cells, so chloride channels stay open. Rice-water stools, hypotension, and a comma-shaped gram-negative rod identify that locked messenger. Cyclic GMP is the messenger of the heat-stable toxin of E coli, not of cholera toxin. Inositol trisphosphate is not the messenger cholera toxin holds high. Diacylglycerol is not the persistent messenger in these crypt cells. Nitric oxide is not the messenger that cholera toxin elevates.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholera toxin causes secretory diarrhea by permanently activating Gs (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: intravenous replacement of fluid and electrolytes is the main basis of therapy in severe cholera (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — Gs/cAMP receptor signaling pathways","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cyclic GMP is the messenger of the heat-stable toxin","C":"Inositol trisphosphate is not the messenger cholera toxin holds","D":"Diacylglycerol is not the persistent messenger in these crypt","E":"Nitric oxide is not the messenger that cholera toxin"}},{"id":"sdl28_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman returns from Mexico with four days of watery diarrhea from toxigenic Escherichia coli. She is not taking diuretics and has not been vomiting. She is thirsty and the abdomen is soft. Serum sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L), chloride is 114 mEq/L (reference range: 98–106 mEq/L), and bicarbonate is 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is most likely?","choices":{"A":"High anion gap metabolic acidosis","B":"Hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis","C":"Acute respiratory acidosis","D":"Hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis","E":"Acute respiratory alkalosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Loss of bicarbonate in diarrheal fluid leaves a hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis, with chloride 114 mEq/L and bicarbonate 15 mEq/L. A normal sodium and no vomiting exclude the alkalosis of gastric loss. The anion gap is not widened, because the fall in bicarbonate is matched by the rise in chloride. Hypochloremic alkalosis follows vomiting, and her chloride is high rather than low. There is no CO2 retention to support an acute respiratory acidosis. Respiratory alkalosis would lower PCO2 and is not described by this electrolyte pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea is a classic cause of normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Escherichia coli enterotoxins cause secretory diarrhea like cholera toxin (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The anion gap is not widened, because the fall","B":"Hypochloremic alkalosis follows vomiting, and her chloride is high","C":"There is no CO2 retention to support an acute respiratory","E":"Respiratory alkalosis would lower PCO2 and is not described"}},{"id":"sdl28_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy in a flood-relief camp has had 12 watery stools in a day. He has not vomited. He is lethargic, the eyes are sunken, the heart rate is 168/min, and blood pressure is 70/40 mm Hg. Capillary refill is 4 seconds, and serum sodium is 136 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Which consequence of the diarrhea most directly threatens his life?","choices":{"A":"Hyperkalemic cardiac arrhythmia","B":"Loss of extracellular fluid volume","C":"Hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis from vomiting","D":"Bacteremic septic shock","E":"Hypernatremic seizures"},"correct":"B","explanation":"He has lost extracellular fluid volume, which is why the pressure is 70/40 mm Hg and the heart rate is 168/min. Sunken eyes and delayed refill are that volume loss, not a primary arrhythmia or alkalosis. Hyperkalemia is not the threat, and secretory diarrhea tends to lower potassium. He has not been vomiting, so a hypochloremic alkalosis is not the life-threatening piece. There is no fever or source to make bacteremic shock the direct consequence of the stool loss. The sodium is normal, so hypernatremic seizures are not the immediate threat.","boardPrep":"Step 1: without fluid replacement, up to 50 percent of patients with cholera die (Guyton Ch. 67). COMLEX: antibiotics can shorten the duration and volume of cholera diarrhea (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hyperkalemia is not the threat, and secretory diarrhea tends","C":"He has not been vomiting, so a hypochloremic alkalosis","D":"There is no fever or source to make bacteremic","E":"The sodium is normal, so hypernatremic seizures are not"}},{"id":"sdl28_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man starts milk of magnesia daily for constipation and develops watery diarrhea. The diarrhea stops within a day when he is kept fasting for a procedure. The abdomen is soft. Serum magnesium is 2.8 mg/dL (reference range: 1.7–2.2 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains the diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Peptide-driven crypt Cl⁻ secretion","B":"Toxin-driven Gs activation in crypt cells","C":"Inflammatory loss of villus absorptive surface","D":"Unabsorbed cations retaining luminal water","E":"Bile acid stimulation of colonocytes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Unabsorbed magnesium holds water in the lumen, an osmotic diarrhea that stops when intake stops. A serum magnesium above the reference range shows the salt is being absorbed in part and still left in the lumen. Peptide-driven crypt secretion would continue during fasting, and this diarrhea stops. Gs activation by a toxin does not follow a magnesium laxative and would not stop so promptly. There is no villus injury or inflammatory diarrhea in this story. Bile acids are not the osmotic cation in milk of magnesia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: osmotic laxatives such as magnesium salts may be misused by patients with bulimia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, anticonstipation drugs). COMLEX: secretory diarrhea results from excess crypt-cell secretion rather than inadequate absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — lactase deficiency as osmotic diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Peptide-driven crypt secretion would continue during fasting, and this diarrhea","B":"Gs activation by a toxin does not follow a","C":"There is no villus injury or inflammatory diarrhea in this","E":"Bile acids are not the osmotic cation in milk of"}},{"id":"sdl28_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":5,"stem":"An 18-month-old girl has four days of vomiting and watery diarrhea during a rotavirus outbreak. She is not taking laxatives. Heart rate is 150/min and capillary refill is 3 seconds. A biopsy in a similar case shows blunted villi. Which category of diarrhea best describes the primary mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Toxin-driven crypt secretion","B":"Peptide hormone-driven secretion","C":"Nonabsorbable osmotic laxative load","D":"Aldosterone-driven colonic K⁺ loss","E":"Decreased absorptive surface area"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Blunted villi reduce the absorptive surface, so fluid that would have been absorbed stays in the lumen. An outbreak illness with that histologic pattern is loss of surface rather than a secretagogue. A toxin-driven crypt secretion is the cholera pattern and is not the villus lesion described. No peptide hormone is driving secretion in this viral illness. She is not taking a nonabsorbable osmotic laxative. Aldosterone-driven potassium loss is a consequence of volume depletion, not the primary diarrheal mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rotavirus is a leading cause of watery diarrhea in young children and is preventable by vaccine (First Aid Microbiology). COMLEX: viral injury to villus tips can cause secondary lactase deficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus structure and brush border","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"A":"A toxin-driven crypt secretion is the cholera pattern and","B":"No peptide hormone is driving secretion in this viral","C":"She is not taking a nonabsorbable osmotic","D":"Aldosterone-driven potassium loss is a consequence of volume depletion,"}},{"id":"sdl28_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":5,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has had five days of profuse watery diarrhea and feels dizzy on standing. She is not taking diuretics. Blood pressure is 96/60 mm Hg, and the pulse rises when she stands. Urine sodium is 8 mEq/L (reference range: 40–220 mEq/L). Which compensatory response is most likely active?","choices":{"A":"Suppressed antidiuretic hormone release","B":"Increased atrial natriuretic peptide","C":"Activated renin-angiotensin-aldosterone","D":"Reduced sympathetic outflow","E":"Suppressed baroreceptor reflex activity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Volume loss has activated the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone axis, which is why the urine sodium is only 8 mEq/L. Dizziness, a pressure of 96/60 mm Hg, and a rise in pulse on standing are the volume stimulus. Antidiuretic hormone is released, not suppressed, when volume is low. Atrial natriuretic peptide falls when the atria are underfilled, rather than rising. Sympathetic outflow increases with hypotension and is not reduced. Baroreceptor reflexes are driving the tachycardia and are not suppressed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: low urine sodium in hypovolemia reflects avid renal sodium retention (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: compensation fails if diarrheal fluid loss is too large or too rapid (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"A":"Antidiuretic hormone is released, not suppressed, when volume is","B":"Atrial natriuretic peptide falls when the atria are underfilled,","D":"Sympathetic outflow increases with hypotension and is not","E":"Baroreceptor reflexes are driving the tachycardia and are not"}},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":3,"stem":"A healthy 29-year-old volunteer has her jejunum perfused with two solutions, each isosmotic to plasma. She has no diarrhea. Solution 1 contains sodium chloride and glucose and produces net water absorption. Solution 2 replaces those solutes with mannitol, which is not absorbed, and net water absorption falls to about zero. Which principle best explains the difference?","choices":{"A":"Mannitol closes apical water channels","B":"Water absorption follows absorbed solute","C":"Glucose itself carries water molecules","D":"Isotonic fluid moves by hydrostatic force","E":"Plasma osmolality falls during perfusion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Water absorption follows the solute that the mucosa absorbs, and mannitol is not absorbed, so water stays in the lumen. Both solutions start isosmotic, which removes a simple osmotic gradient as the explanation. Mannitol does not close apical water channels. Glucose does not itself carry water molecules across the membrane. Hydrostatic force is not what differs between these two isosmotic perfusions. Plasma osmolality does not fall during the short perfusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intestinal fluid absorption is isosmotic, with solute absorbed first and water following (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Nonabsorbable solutes such as mannitol or lactulose retain water in the lumen and cause osmotic diarrhea.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mannitol does not close apical water","C":"Glucose does not itself carry water molecules across the","D":"Hydrostatic force is not what differs between these two isosmotic","E":"Plasma osmolality does not fall during the short"}},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with heart failure starts high-dose spironolactone. He has no diarrhea. Weeks later he has mild weakness. Serum potassium is 5.8 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Besides the kidney, which intestinal change is expected?","choices":{"A":"Less jejunal Na⁺-glucose cotransport","B":"Less ileal apical Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchange","C":"Less colonic Na⁺ uptake, K⁺ secretion","D":"More crypt Cl⁻ secretion through CFTR","E":"More apical jejunal Na⁺-H⁺ exchange"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Spironolactone blocks aldosterone, so the colon takes up less sodium and secretes less potassium. A potassium of 5.8 mEq/L is the renal result, and the colon changes in the same direction. Jejunal sodium-glucose cotransport is not an aldosterone effect. Ileal chloride-bicarbonate exchange is not the transport this drug reduces. Crypt chloride secretion through the apical anion channel is not increased by aldosterone blockade. Apical jejunal sodium-hydrogen exchange is not the aldosterone-dependent step.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The colon resembles the renal principal cell, with aldosterone-induced apical Na⁺ channels that drive Na⁺ absorption and K⁺ secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Aldosterone antagonists cause hyperkalemia, so potassium must be monitored.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Jejunal sodium-glucose cotransport is not an aldosterone","B":"Ileal chloride-bicarbonate exchange is not the transport this drug","D":"Crypt chloride secretion through the apical anion channel is","E":"Apical jejunal sodium-hydrogen exchange is not the aldosterone-dependent"}},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with constipation starts a minimally absorbed oral peptide that binds the same enterocyte receptor as the heat-stable toxin of enterotoxigenic E. coli. Within days the stools become soft and frequent. The abdomen is soft and vital signs are normal. Through which apical channel does the extra chloride secretion occur?","choices":{"A":"ClC-2","B":"ENaC","C":"NKCC1","D":"CFTR","E":"Aquaporin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The peptide, like heat-stable toxin, opens apical CFTR so chloride is secreted. Soft frequent stools after a luminally acting peptide, with a normal examination, are that channel opening. ClC-2 is not the apical channel this toxin receptor opens. ENaC absorbs sodium in the colon and is not the secretory chloride channel. NKCC1 is the basolateral entry step, not the apical channel. Aquaporin conducts water and does not secrete chloride.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Linaclotide activates guanylate cyclase-C, raising cGMP and opening CFTR, while lubiprostone activates ClC-2 (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: Both drugs treat constipation by increasing intestinal fluid secretion, and diarrhea is their main side effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"ClC-2 is not the apical channel this toxin receptor","B":"ENaC absorbs sodium in the colon and is not","C":"NKCC1 is the basolateral entry step, not the apical","E":"Aquaporin conducts water and does not secrete"}},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":3,"stem":"A 26-year-old aid worker has passed 15 L of rice-water stool in two days after drinking untreated water. Blood pressure is 88/54 mm Hg and the pulse is 124/min. Culture grows a comma-shaped gram-negative rod. In a model of this illness, which drug action on crypt cells would most reduce the fluid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Blocking the apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger","B":"Blocking apical Na⁺-glucose cotransport","C":"Activating Gs to raise cyclic AMP","D":"Stimulating basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","E":"Blocking basolateral Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ entry"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cholera secretion needs chloride entry across the basolateral membrane on the sodium-potassium-2-chloride cotransporter, and blocking that entry dries up the lumen. Rice-water volume and a comma-shaped rod identify the toxin-driven secretion. Blocking the apical sodium-hydrogen exchanger would impair absorption, not stop this secretion. Blocking sodium-glucose cotransport would remove a useful absorptive path and would not cut the toxin-driven secretion. Activating Gs would raise cyclic AMP further and increase secretion. Stimulating the basolateral sodium pump would support secretion rather than reduce it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crypt chloride secretion uses basolateral Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ entry and apical chloride channels opened by cAMP, with Na⁺ and water following paracellularly (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Cholera kills by rapid volume loss, so rehydration is the priority.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Blocking the apical sodium-hydrogen exchanger would impair absorption, not","B":"Blocking sodium-glucose cotransport would remove a useful absorptive path","C":"Activating Gs would raise cyclic AMP further and increase","D":"Stimulating the basolateral sodium pump would support secretion rather"}},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":5,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman has large-volume watery diarrhea that continues despite fasting. She is not taking laxatives. She is weak, and serum potassium is 2.6 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). CT shows a pancreatic mass, and chromogranin A is 360 ng/mL (reference range: <93 ng/mL). Which drug reduces the tumor's hormone secretion?","choices":{"A":"Loperamide","B":"Octreotide","C":"Lactulose","D":"Bisacodyl","E":"Psyllium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Octreotide suppresses hormone release from this neuroendocrine tumor and thereby slows the secretory diarrhea. Fasting diarrhea, a pancreatic mass, and an elevated chromogranin A identify the hormone-driven illness. Loperamide slows motility and does not reduce the tumor hormone output. Lactulose is an osmotic laxative and would worsen the diarrhea. Bisacodyl stimulates secretion and is not a treatment for this tumor. Psyllium adds bulk and does not suppress tumor hormone release.","boardPrep":"Step 1: octreotide is also used for acute variceal bleeds, acromegaly, and carcinoid tumors (First Aid Gastrointestinal, octreotide). COMLEX: octreotide can cause steatorrhea and cholelithiasis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, octreotide).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"A":"Loperamide slows motility and does not reduce the tumor hormone","C":"Lactulose is an osmotic laxative and would worsen the","D":"Bisacodyl stimulates secretion and is not a treatment for this","E":"Psyllium adds bulk and does not suppress tumor hormone"}},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man is lightheaded during two days of watery diarrhea from viral gastroenteritis. He is not taking diuretics. Standing blood pressure is 98/60 mm Hg and the pulse is 108/min. His family asks why a glucose-and-salt solution is better than plain water. Which mechanism makes the solution more effective?","choices":{"A":"Coupled Na⁺-glucose uptake pulls in water","B":"Glucose closes the crypt chloride channels","C":"Salt induces apical Na⁺ channels in the jejunum","D":"Hypertonic glucose draws water into the blood","E":"Sodium blocks flow-driven colonic K⁺ secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sodium-glucose cotransport pulls water in with the solute, so the solution replaces volume that plain water does not. Standing hypotension during viral diarrhea is the deficit that cotransport corrects. Glucose does not close the crypt chloride channels. Salt does not induce colonic sodium channels in the jejunum as the reason the solution works. A hypertonic glucose drink would hold water in the lumen rather than draw it into the blood. Sodium in the solution does not act by blocking colonic potassium secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Oral rehydration solution uses Na⁺-glucose cotransport to stimulate absorption of fluid and electrolytes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Oral rehydration is first-line therapy for mild to moderate dehydration from diarrhea.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Glucose does not close the crypt chloride","C":"Salt does not induce colonic sodium channels in the","D":"A hypertonic glucose drink would hold water in the","E":"Sodium in the solution does not act by blocking"}},{"id":"grid_sdl28_q01","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":4,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has had two days of profuse rice-water stools during an outbreak of rice-water diarrhea. He is not taking diuretics and has not been vomiting. He has not yet received intravenous fluid. Blood pressure is 88/56 mm Hg, the heart rate is 124/min, and the abdomen is soft. Stool culture grows a comma-shaped gram-negative rod. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely before treatment?","grid":{"headers":["Plasma aldosterone","Serum K⁺","Serum HCO₃⁻"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↓","↓"],"B":["↑","↑","↓"],"C":["↑","↓","↓"],"D":["↑","↔","↑"],"E":["↓","↑","↑"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Choice C (↑ plasma aldosterone, ↓ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻) is correct. Loss of extracellular-type fluid lowers arterial pressure and activates the renin-angiotensin II-aldosterone system. Diarrheal fluid is high in K⁺ from flow-dependent colonic secretion, which aldosterone adds to, so hypokalemia follows. Diarrheal fluid is also rich in HCO₃⁻ from intestinal secretions, causing hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. Choice A (↓ plasma aldosterone, ↓ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻) misses the volume response. Choice B (↑ plasma aldosterone, ↑ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻) ignores fecal K⁺ loss. Choice D (↑ plasma aldosterone, ↔ serum K⁺, ↑ serum HCO₃⁻) fits vomiting more than diarrhea, with loss of gastric H⁺ raising HCO₃⁻. Choice E (↓ plasma aldosterone, ↑ serum K⁺, ↑ serum HCO₃⁻) reverses each change. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Colon; Diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea causes normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, whereas vomiting causes metabolic alkalosis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: intravenous fluid and electrolyte replacement is the main basis of therapy in severe cholera (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q31","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man has an end ileostomy after colectomy for a perforation. Output is watery but he is eating, and the stoma is pink. He is not taking diuretics. Sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L), potassium is 4.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and bicarbonate is 24 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Before the colectomy, net absorption in the ileum was mainly of which salt?","choices":{"A":"Sodium chloride","B":"Sodium bicarbonate, with no chloride transport","C":"Potassium chloride secreted as the only net flux","D":"Calcium phosphate","E":"Magnesium sulfate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ileum has both sodium–hydrogen and chloride–bicarbonate exchange, so its net absorption is sodium chloride. Net sodium bicarbonate absorption is the jejunal pattern, which lacks the ileal chloride–bicarbonate exchanger. Potassium secretion is a colonic feature and is not the ileum's net salt absorption. Calcium phosphate is not the salt the ileum absorbs in net fashion. Magnesium sulfate is an osmotic load, not the ileum's net absorbate.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Net sodium bicarbonate absorption is the jejunal pattern, which lacks the ileal chloride–bicarbonate exchanger","C":"Potassium secretion is a colonic feature and is not the ileum's net salt absorption","D":"Calcium phosphate is not the salt the ileum absorbs in net fashion","E":"Magnesium sulfate is an osmotic load, not the ileum's net absorbate"}},{"id":"sdl28_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":1,"stem":"Which diarrhea continues while the patient is fasting?","choices":{"A":"Diarrhea from an unabsorbed sugar","B":"Diarrhea that stops as soon as milk is removed","C":"Diarrhea driven only by a nonabsorbable salt","D":"Secretory diarrhea","E":"Diarrhea that ends when the osmotic load is withdrawn"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secretory diarrhea is active crypt secretion and persists during fasting, whereas osmotic diarrhea stops when the unabsorbed solute is removed. An unabsorbed sugar is an osmotic load, and that diarrhea abates with fasting. Diarrhea that stops when milk is removed is osmotic, from unabsorbed lactose. A nonabsorbable salt holds water in the lumen and the diarrhea ends when the salt is stopped. Withdrawal of an osmotic load is what ends osmotic diarrhea, not secretory diarrhea.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An unabsorbed sugar is an osmotic load, and that diarrhea abates with fasting","B":"Diarrhea that stops when milk is removed is osmotic, from unabsorbed lactose","C":"A nonabsorbable salt holds water in the lumen and the diarrhea ends when the salt is stopped","E":"Withdrawal of an osmotic load is what ends osmotic diarrhea, not secretory diarrhea"}},{"id":"sdl28_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy has watery diarrhea and gas after apple juice. The stools stop when juice is withheld, and he has no fever. He is well hydrated and the abdomen is soft and nontender. Stool sodium is 30 mEq/L and stool potassium is 20 mEq/L, with a measured stool osmolality of 320 mOsm/kg and a gap well above 50 mOsm/kg. Serum sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Which mechanism best fits these stools?","choices":{"A":"An unabsorbed solute holding water in the lumen","B":"Toxin-locked opening of crypt chloride channels that continues during a fast","C":"Blood and leukocytes pouring from an invaded mucosa","D":"Failure to make chylomicrons","E":"Portal hypertension with protein-losing enteropathy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A stool osmotic gap above 50 mOsm/kg, with diarrhea that stops when the solute is removed, is osmotic diarrhea. Toxin-driven crypt secretion persists during fasting and leaves a low, not a high, osmotic gap. Inflamed, invaded mucosa produces blood and fecal leukocytes, which this child does not have. Failure of chylomicron assembly causes fat malabsorption, not a sugar-related osmotic gap. Portal hypertension is not the cause of a high stool osmotic gap that ends when juice is stopped.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Toxin-driven crypt secretion persists during fasting and leaves a low, not a high, osmotic gap","C":"Inflamed, invaded mucosa produces blood and fecal leukocytes, which this child does not have","D":"Failure of chylomicron assembly causes fat malabsorption, not a sugar-related osmotic gap","E":"Portal hypertension is not the cause of a high stool osmotic gap that ends when juice is stopped"}},{"id":"sdl28_q34","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has passed more than 8 L of rice-water stool in a day, and the diarrhea continues though he has taken nothing by mouth. He is weak, the pulse is 120/min, and the abdomen is soft. Sodium is 134 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L), potassium is 2.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and bicarbonate is 14 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). The stool gap is small. Which cells are pouring chloride into the lumen?","choices":{"A":"Villus-tip cells that only absorb","B":"Parietal cells","C":"Crypt epithelial cells","D":"Hepatocytes","E":"Pancreatic islet alpha cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Secretory diarrhea is excessive chloride and water secretion by crypt epithelial cells, and it continues during fasting. Villus epithelial cells are the absorptive population, not the source of this secretion. Parietal cells secrete gastric acid, not the chloride flood of this watery diarrhea. Hepatocytes are not the crypt cells secreting chloride into the gut lumen. Islet alpha cells secrete glucagon and do not secrete intestinal chloride.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villus epithelial cells are the absorptive population, not the source of this secretion","B":"Parietal cells secrete gastric acid, not the chloride flood of this watery diarrhea","D":"Hepatocytes are not the crypt cells secreting chloride into the gut lumen","E":"Islet alpha cells secrete glucagon and do not secrete intestinal chloride"}},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q07","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":3,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman has large-volume watery diarrhea that continues while she fasts. She is not taking laxatives, has no fever, and the stool is not bloody. She is weak, the pulse rises when she stands, and the abdomen is soft. Serum potassium is 2.7 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), serum bicarbonate is 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), stool sodium is 100 mEq/L, stool potassium is 40 mEq/L, and stool osmolality is 290 mOsm/kg. Which process most likely accounts for her diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Unabsorbed luminal solute retaining water","B":"Colonic fermentation of undigested sugar","C":"Increased crypt chloride secretion","D":"Bile acid loss impairing fat absorption","E":"Mucosal inflammation with exudation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The stool osmotic gap is 290 minus 2 times (100 + 40), or 10 mOsm/kg, a low gap, so this is secretory diarrhea from increased crypt chloride secretion. An unabsorbed luminal solute would leave a gap above 50 mOsm/kg, and that diarrhea stops when intake stops. Colonic fermentation of undigested sugar produces gas and acidic stool of moderate volume, and it ends when the sugar is stopped. Bile acid loss that impairs fat absorption produces steatorrhea, and her stool is watery. Mucosal inflammation with exudation brings fever, blood, or fecal leukocytes, and she has none of these.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An unabsorbed luminal solute would leave a gap above 50 mOsm/kg, and that diarrhea stops when intake stops","B":"Colonic fermentation of undigested sugar produces gas and acidic stool of moderate volume, and it ends when the sugar is stopped","D":"Bile acid loss that impairs fat absorption produces steatorrhea, and her stool is watery","E":"Mucosal inflammation with exudation brings fever, blood, or fecal leukocytes, and she has none of these"}}],"29":[{"id":"sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":1,"stem":"Besides hernias, adhesions, intussusception, and volvulus, which causes make up most of the remaining mechanical obstructions?","choices":{"A":"Gallstones and bezoars","B":"Ileus and pseudo-obstruction","C":"Swallowed foreign bodies","D":"Meckel diverticula","E":"Tumors and infarctions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins lists tumors, infarctions, and other causes of strictures such as Crohn disease as the remainder after the four major mechanical causes. Gallstones and bezoars can obstruct the bowel but are not the group Robbins names for the remainder. Ileus and pseudo-obstruction are failures of motility without a mechanical blockage. Swallowed foreign bodies are not among the causes Robbins lists for most remaining obstructions. Meckel diverticula can serve as a lead point for intussusception, which is already one of the four. Sources: Robbins ch 17, intestinal obstruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hernias, adhesions, intussusception, and volvulus cause most mechanical obstruction, and tumors, infarction, and strictures cause most of the rest (Robbins ch 17). COMLEX: New obstruction in an older adult without prior surgery or hernia should raise concern for a tumor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gallstones and bezoars can obstruct the bowel","B":"Ileus and pseudo-obstruction are failures of motility","C":"Swallowed foreign bodies are not among the","D":"Meckel diverticula can serve as a lead"}},{"id":"sdl29_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most frequent cause of intestinal obstruction worldwide?","choices":{"A":"Adhesions","B":"Volvulus","C":"Tumors","D":"Hernias","E":"Strictures"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hernias are the most frequent cause of intestinal obstruction worldwide and the third most common in the United States. Inguinal hernias, with narrow orifices and large sacs, are most often involved. Adhesions are the leading cause of obstruction in the United States and developed nations, not worldwide. Volvulus is rare and occurs most often in the sigmoid colon, so it is not the most frequent cause of obstruction. Tumors fall in the minority of mechanical obstructions left after hernias, adhesions, intussusception, and volvulus. Strictures, such as those of Crohn disease, are an uncommon cause compared with hernias, which lead worldwide. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hernias). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: femoral hernias protrude below the inguinal ligament and carry a greater risk of strangulation than inguinal hernias (First Aid Gastrointestinal, hernias). COMLEX: acquired hernias are found in up to 5% of the population (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Adhesions are the leading cause of obstruction in the United States and developed nations, not worldwide.","B":"Volvulus is rare and occurs most often in the sigmoid colon, so it is not the most frequent cause of obstruction.","C":"Tumors fall in the minority of mechanical obstructions left after hernias, adhesions, intussusception, and volvulus.","E":"Strictures, such as those of Crohn disease, are an uncommon cause compared with hernias, which lead worldwide."}},{"id":"sdl29_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the segment of bowel that telescopes into the distal bowel in intussusception called?","choices":{"A":"Intussusceptum","B":"Intussuscipiens","C":"Hernia sac","D":"Mesenteric root","E":"Closed loop"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In intussusception, a segment constricted by a peristaltic wave (the intussusceptum) telescopes into the immediately distal segment (the intussuscipiens). The hernia sac is the serosa-lined peritoneal pouch that protrudes through an abdominal wall defect, not a telescoping bowel segment. A closed loop is formed by fibrous adhesions that trap sliding viscera, an internal hernia mechanism rather than telescoping. The mesentery is pulled along behind the telescoping intussusceptum; it is not the segment that telescopes. The intussuscipiens is the distal receiving segment. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intestinal obstruction, Fig. 17.25). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intussusception most commonly occurs at the ileocecal junction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception). COMLEX: ultrasound or CT of intussusception may show a target sign (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The hernia sac is the serosa-lined peritoneal pouch that protrudes through an abdominal wall defect, not a telescoping bowel segment.","E":"A closed loop is formed by fibrous adhesions that trap sliding viscera, an internal hernia mechanism rather than telescoping.","D":"The mesentery is pulled along behind the telescoping intussusceptum; it is not the segment that telescopes.","B":"The intussuscipiens is the distal receiving segment."}},{"id":"sdl29_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":1,"stem":"What causes most acute arterial obstruction of the mesenteric circulation?","choices":{"A":"Systemic vasculitis","B":"Mesenteric venous clot","C":"Tumor compression","D":"Thrombosis or embolism","E":"Vasoconstrictive drugs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In a large majority of cases, acute mesenteric obstruction is caused by thrombosis or embolism. Severe atherosclerosis is the main risk for thrombosis, and emboli come mostly from aortic atheromas or cardiac mural thrombi. Mesenteric venous thrombosis is a venous cause tied to hypercoagulable states, cirrhosis, and neoplasms, not acute arterial obstruction. Vasoconstrictive drugs such as cocaine cause nonocclusive hypoperfusion rather than arterial obstruction. Vasculitides are a less common cause of mesenteric arterial thrombosis than atherosclerotic thrombosis or embolism. Invasive neoplasms are a cause of mesenteric venous thrombosis, whereas acute arterial obstruction is mostly thrombosis or embolism. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: risk factors for acute mesenteric ischemia include atrial fibrillation, peripheral arterial disease, recent MI, and CHF (First Aid Gastrointestinal, acute mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: mesenteric venous thrombosis is an uncommon cause of ischemic bowel disease (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mesenteric venous thrombosis is a venous cause tied to hypercoagulable states, cirrhosis, and neoplasms, not acute arterial obstruction.","E":"Vasoconstrictive drugs such as cocaine cause nonocclusive hypoperfusion rather than arterial obstruction.","A":"Vasculitides are a less common cause of mesenteric arterial thrombosis than atherosclerotic thrombosis or embolism.","C":"Invasive neoplasms are a cause of mesenteric venous thrombosis, whereas acute arterial obstruction is mostly thrombosis or embolism."}},{"id":"sdl29_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":1,"stem":"Which intestinal component is most vulnerable to ischemic injury?","choices":{"A":"Surface epithelium","B":"Crypt stem cells","C":"Basal crypt cells","D":"Muscularis propria","E":"Myenteric neurons"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Capillaries run from crypt to surface before a hairpin turn, so the surface epithelium is most vulnerable while crypt stem cells are protected. Surface atrophy with normal or hyperproliferative crypts is the signature of ischemia. The muscularis propria is injured only in transmural infarction, typically from acute major vascular occlusion. Crypt stem cells are protected because capillaries reach the crypts before turning at the surface. Basal crypts receive blood first and are spared, often becoming hyperproliferative in ischemia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic mesenteric ischemia causes postprandial epigastric pain, food aversion, and weight loss (First Aid Gastrointestinal, chronic mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: chronic intestinal ischemia is accompanied by fibrous scarring of the lamina propria (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The muscularis propria is injured only in transmural infarction, typically from acute major vascular occlusion.","B":"Crypt stem cells are protected because capillaries reach the crypts before turning at the surface.","C":"Basal crypts receive blood first and are spared, often becoming hyperproliferative in ischemia."}},{"id":"sdl29_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":1,"stem":"Generalized hypotension most often causes ischemic injury at which colonic site?","choices":{"A":"Ileocecal junction","B":"Hepatic flexure","C":"Splenic flexure","D":"Mid transverse colon","E":"Ascending colon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Watershed zones at the end of arterial territories are most vulnerable, and the splenic flexure, where the superior and inferior mesenteric circulations terminate, is the main one. The ileocecal junction is well supplied by the ileocolic branch of the superior mesenteric artery. The hepatic flexure lies within the superior mesenteric territory rather than at a watershed. The mid transverse colon is supplied by the middle colic artery and is not a named watershed. The ascending colon lies within the superior mesenteric territory and is not a watershed zone. Sources: Robbins ch 17, ischemic bowel disease pathogenesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Colonic ischemia favors watershed zones such as the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid (Robbins ch 17; First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: Crampy left-sided pain then hematochezia in an older adult after hypotension suggests ischemic colitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The ileocecal junction is well supplied by","B":"The hepatic flexure lies within the superior","D":"The mid transverse colon is supplied by","E":"The ascending colon lies within the superior"}},{"id":"sdl29_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is angiodysplasia most common?","choices":{"A":"Gastric antrum region","B":"Cecum or right colon","C":"Proximal jejunal loops","D":"Sigmoid colon segment","E":"Lower rectum and anus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Angiodysplasia is most common in the cecum or right colon and usually comes to attention after the sixth decade. The narrower sigmoid colon lacks the large diameter and high wall tension that drive this lesion. It arises from distention-related occlusion of submucosal colonic veins, so the stomach, proximal jejunum, and rectum and anus are outside that segment. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia presents with hematochezia from tortuous dilated vessels (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: angiodysplasia bleeding ranges from chronic and intermittent to acute and massive (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The narrower sigmoid colon lacks the large diameter and high wall tension that drive this lesion.","A":"The stomach is not the site of this colonic venous lesion.","C":"The proximal jejunum is not a high-tension colonic segment.","E":"The rectum and anus are not the high-tension segment."}},{"id":"sdl29_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which test confirms angiodysplasia?","choices":{"A":"Barium swallow","B":"Stool culture","C":"Plain radiograph","D":"Angiography","E":"Breath testing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Visceral or aortic angiography (with colonoscopy) is the diagnostic test for angiodysplasia. The lesion consists of malformed, dilated, thin-walled submucosal and mucosal vessels. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (angiodysplasia); Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia is more common in older patients (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: angiodysplasia has a prevalence of less than 1% in adults (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cardiac valve lesion is classically associated with bleeding from angiodysplasia?","choices":{"A":"Mitral stenosis","B":"Mitral valve prolapse","C":"Aortic stenosis","D":"Tricuspid regurgitation","E":"Pulmonic stenosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Angiodysplasia is associated with end-stage renal disease, von Willebrand disease, and aortic stenosis. Mitral stenosis is a rheumatic valve lesion not listed among the associations of angiodysplasia. Mitral valve prolapse is not among the conditions linked to angiodysplasia. Tricuspid regurgitation is not described as an association of angiodysplasia. Pulmonic stenosis is a congenital lesion with no listed link to angiodysplasia. Sources: First Aid 2025 GI, angiodysplasia; Robbins ch 17, angiodysplasia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Angiodysplasia is linked to end-stage renal disease, von Willebrand disease, and aortic stenosis and is confirmed by angiography (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: Recurrent painless hematochezia in an older patient with a systolic ejection murmur suggests this pairing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mitral stenosis is a rheumatic valve lesion","B":"Mitral valve prolapse is not among the","D":"Tricuspid regurgitation is not described as an","E":"Pulmonic stenosis is a congenital lesion with"}},{"id":"sdl29_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":1,"stem":"How does a CFTR defect lead to exocrine pancreatic insufficiency in cystic fibrosis?","choices":{"A":"Autoantibodies destroy acinar cells","B":"Trypsinogen gains resistance to lysis","C":"The duodenum stops releasing secretin","D":"Gallstones block the ampulla","E":"Inspissated secretions plug ducts"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Faulty CFTR reduces bicarbonate, sodium, and water secretion, so secretions dehydrate and form intraductal concretions that obstruct the ducts, causing low-grade chronic pancreatitis, autodigestion, and eventual exocrine insufficiency. Autoantibodies against acinar cells describe autoimmune pancreatitis, not cystic fibrosis. Trypsinogen that resists inactivation is the mechanism of hereditary pancreatitis from PRSS1 mutations. Secretin release is not the defect; the ducts cannot secrete fluid in response to it. Gallstones blocking the ampulla cause acute gallstone pancreatitis, not the ductal plugging of cystic fibrosis. Sources: Robbins ch 17, malabsorption and cystic fibrosis; Robbins ch 19, pancreatitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In cystic fibrosis, dehydrated secretions plug pancreatic ducts and cause exocrine insufficiency, impairing the intraluminal phase of absorption (Robbins ch 17). COMLEX: Treat steatorrhea in cystic fibrosis with pancreatic enzyme replacement and fat-soluble vitamins.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Autoantibodies against acinar cells describe autoimmune pancreatitis,","B":"Trypsinogen that resists inactivation is the mechanism","C":"Secretin release is not the defect; the","D":"Gallstones blocking the ampulla cause acute gallstone"}},{"id":"sdl29_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most sensitive serologic test for celiac disease?","choices":{"A":"IgG antigliadin antibodies","B":"IgA anti-TG2 antibodies","C":"Anti-parietal cell antibodies","D":"Antimitochondrial antibodies","E":"Perinuclear ANCA antibodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The most sensitive noninvasive test for celiac disease is IgA against tissue transglutaminase 2. IgG anti-TG2 can be used in patients with IgA deficiency. IgA antibodies to tissue transglutaminase (TG2) are the most sensitive serologic test for celiac disease, ahead of antigliadin antibodies. Antibodies to parietal cell H+,K+-ATPase and intrinsic factor mark autoimmune atrophic gastritis, not celiac disease. Antimitochondrial antibody against PDC-E2 is the characteristic finding in primary biliary cholangitis, not celiac disease. p-ANCA is found in up to 75% of ulcerative colitis patients and is not a celiac test. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac serology also includes anti-endomysial and anti-deamidated gliadin peptide antibodies (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac disease shows an abnormal d-xylose test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"IgA antibodies to tissue transglutaminase (TG2) are the most sensitive serologic test for celiac disease, ahead of antigliadin antibodies.","C":"Antibodies to parietal cell H+,K+-ATPase and intrinsic factor mark autoimmune atrophic gastritis, not celiac disease.","D":"Antimitochondrial antibody against PDC-E2 is the characteristic finding in primary biliary cholangitis, not celiac disease. p-ANCA is found in up to 75% of ulcerative colitis patients and is not a celiac test.","E":"p-ANCA is found in up to 75% of ulcerative colitis patients and is not a celiac test."}},{"id":"sdl29_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most common cancer in people with celiac disease?","choices":{"A":"Gastric diffuse large B-cell lymphoma tumor","B":"Distal esophageal Barrett adenocarcinoma","C":"Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma","D":"Hepatocellular carcinoma of the liver","E":"Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The most common cancer in celiac disease is enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma, an aggressive lymphoma of intraepithelial T cells. Small intestinal adenocarcinoma is also more frequent. The most common cancer in celiac disease is a T-cell lymphoma of the intestine (EATL), not a gastric B-cell lymphoma. The cancers increased in celiac disease are enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma (most common) and small intestinal adenocarcinoma, not Barrett adenocarcinoma. Hepatocellular carcinoma is driven mainly by chronic hepatitis B and C infection (70% to 85% of cases worldwide), not by celiac disease. Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma is not among the cancers increased in celiac disease (T-cell lymphoma and small intestinal adenocarcinoma). Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease is associated with dermatitis herpetiformis and decreased bone density (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: a gluten-free diet may reduce celiac complications including anemia, female infertility, and osteoporosis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The most common cancer in celiac disease is a T-cell lymphoma of the intestine (EATL), not a gastric B-cell lymphoma.","B":"The cancers increased in celiac disease are enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma (most common) and small intestinal adenocarcinoma, not Barrett adenocarcinoma.","D":"Hepatocellular carcinoma is driven mainly by chronic hepatitis B and C infection (70% to 85% of cases worldwide), not by celiac disease.","E":"Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma is not among the cancers increased in celiac disease (T-cell lymphoma and small intestinal adenocarcinoma)."}},{"id":"sdl29_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":1,"stem":"Mutation of which gene causes IPEX?","choices":{"A":"CFTR","B":"MYO5B","C":"HLA-DQ2","D":"RET","E":"FOXP3"},"correct":"E","explanation":"IPEX (immune dysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, X-linkage) is caused by germline loss-of-function mutations in the X-linked FOXP3 gene. CFTR mutation causes cystic fibrosis, impairing chloride and bicarbonate transport and leading to pancreatic insufficiency. HLA-DQ2 is the susceptibility allele of celiac disease, present in about 90% of cases. MYO5B mutations cause microvillus inclusion disease, not IPEX. Loss-of-function RET mutations cause Hirschsprung disease by halting neural crest migration, not the Treg failure of IPEX. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: microvillus inclusion disease is caused by MYO5B mutations and presents with intractable diarrhea in early infancy (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: autoimmune enteropathy is treated with immunosuppressants such as cyclosporine or with stem cell transplantation (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CFTR mutation causes cystic fibrosis, impairing chloride and bicarbonate transport and leading to pancreatic insufficiency.","C":"HLA-DQ2 is the susceptibility allele of celiac disease, present in about 90% of cases.","B":"MYO5B mutations cause microvillus inclusion disease, not IPEX.","D":"Loss-of-function RET mutations cause Hirschsprung disease by halting neural crest migration, not the Treg failure of IPEX."}},{"id":"sdl29_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which defective molecule causes abetalipoproteinemia?","choices":{"A":"Cystic fibrosis transmembrane regulator","B":"Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein","C":"Sodium-glucose linked transporter protein","D":"Low-density lipoprotein receptor protein","E":"Apolipoprotein E lipid carrier protein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Abetalipoproteinemia is an autosomal recessive defect in microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP), which transfers lipids to nascent apolipoprotein B in the ER. CFTR dysfunction causes cystic fibrosis, whose malabsorption comes from pancreatic insufficiency rather than failed lipoprotein assembly. The LDL receptor is not the defect. Apolipoprotein E is not the missing molecule. SGLT1 is the Na+-glucose cotransporter for glucose and galactose uptake, not a lipoprotein assembly protein. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: abetalipoproteinemia causes acanthocytes on the peripheral smear (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: abetalipoproteinemia presents in infancy with failure to thrive, diarrhea, and steatorrhea (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CFTR dysfunction causes cystic fibrosis, whose malabsorption comes from pancreatic insufficiency rather than failed lipoprotein assembly.","D":"The LDL receptor is not the defect.","E":"Apolipoprotein E is not the missing molecule.","C":"SGLT1 is the Na+-glucose cotransporter for glucose and galactose uptake, not a lipoprotein assembly protein."}},{"id":"sdl29_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":1,"stem":"In regions where environmental enteric dysfunction is endemic, which observation suggests an underlying defect in mucosal immune function?","choices":{"A":"High oral vaccine failure rates","B":"Common selective IgA deficiency","C":"Absent Peyer patches at birth","D":"Positive anti-TG2 serology","E":"Rapid cure with oral antibiotics"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Robbins notes that relatively high oral vaccine failure rates in regions where environmental enteric dysfunction is endemic point to an underlying defect in mucosal immune function. Selective IgA deficiency is not described as a feature of environmental enteric dysfunction. Absent Peyer patches at birth are not reported in this acquired disorder of early childhood. Anti-TG2 serology is a marker of celiac disease, and no accepted laboratory test diagnoses environmental enteric dysfunction. Oral antibiotics and nutritional supplements do not reverse or prevent environmental enteric dysfunction. Sources: Robbins ch 17, environmental enteric dysfunction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Environmental enteric dysfunction follows chronic fecal pathogen exposure, has no single causal organism, and is linked to oral vaccine failure (Robbins ch 17). COMLEX: Prevention relies on sanitation and clean water, since antibiotics and supplements do not reverse it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Selective IgA deficiency is not described as","C":"Absent Peyer patches at birth are not","D":"Anti-TG2 serology is a marker of celiac","E":"Oral antibiotics and nutritional supplements do not"}},{"id":"sdl29_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-month-old boy has intermittent screaming episodes with his legs drawn up, beginning a week after a viral illness. Between episodes he is comfortable. A sausage-shaped mass is felt on the right, and there is no hernia. The stool looks like red currant jelly. What most likely served as the leading edge?","choices":{"A":"Intraluminal adenocarcinoma","B":"Inspissated meconium plug","C":"Fibrous adhesion band","D":"Hyperplastic Peyer patches","E":"Incarcerated hernia sac"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intussusception after a viral illness uses hyperplastic Peyer patches as the lead point that lets ileum telescope into colon. Intermittent pain, a right-sided sausage mass, and currant-jelly stool are that event in a toddler. An intraluminal adenocarcinoma is an adult lead point and is rare at 14 months. Inspissated meconium plugs the ileum of a newborn with a sweat-chloride defect, not a toddler after a virus. A fibrous adhesion follows surgery, which this child has not had. There is no hernia sac on examination to serve as the lead point.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in idiopathic intussusception of young children, contrast or air enemas are both diagnostic and therapeutic (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Meckel diverticulum and bowel wall hematoma from IgA vasculitis are pediatric intussusception lead points (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An intraluminal adenocarcinoma is an adult lead point and","B":"Inspissated meconium plugs the ileum of a newborn with","C":"A fibrous adhesion follows surgery, which this child has not","E":"There is no hernia sac on examination to serve"}},{"id":"sdl29_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman has abdominal pain, distention, and vomiting. She had a hysterectomy years ago and has no external hernia. The abdomen is distended and tympanitic, with high-pitched bowel sounds and no peritonitis. An abdominal radiograph shows air-fluid levels in the small bowel and little colonic gas. What is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Twisting of the sigmoid colon","B":"Postoperative fibrous bands","C":"Ileocecal segment telescoping","D":"Inguinal sac herniation of bowel","E":"Gallstone lodged at the valve"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Prior pelvic surgery leaves fibrous bands that obstruct the small bowel. Distention, vomiting, high-pitched sounds, and air-fluid levels without a hernia are that mechanical block. Twisting of the sigmoid colon would dilate the colon, and the gas here is in the small bowel. Ileocecal telescoping is a toddler pattern with currant-jelly stool, not a postoperative adult picture. There is no inguinal hernia on examination. A gallstone at the ileocecal valve is uncommon and is not linked to a prior hysterectomy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: small bowel obstruction is managed with GI decompression, volume resuscitation, and bowel rest (First Aid Gastrointestinal, small bowel obstruction). COMLEX: adhesions can follow peritoneal inflammation such as endometriosis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 2 — physiologic herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Twisting of the sigmoid colon would dilate the colon,","C":"Ileocecal telescoping is a toddler pattern with currant-jelly stool,","D":"There is no inguinal hernia on","E":"A gallstone at the ileocecal valve is uncommon and"}},{"id":"sdl29_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old man in a nursing home has sudden abdominal distention. He has had chronic constipation and has never had abdominal surgery. The abdomen is massively tympanitic and nontender, with high-pitched bowel sounds. A plain film shows a coffee-bean sign. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Midgut volvulus","B":"Cecal carcinoma","C":"Sigmoid volvulus","D":"Colonic ileus","E":"Ischemic colitis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A coffee-bean shadow in an elderly constipated man is sigmoid volvulus. Massive tympany without peritonitis fits a twist of the redundant sigmoid. Midgut volvulus is a neonatal emergency with bilious vomiting, not a coffee-bean film in an older adult. A cecal carcinoma would obstruct more gradually and would not draw a coffee-bean loop. Colonic ileus dilates the colon without the coffee-bean twist. Ischemic colitis causes pain and bloody diarrhea, not this closed-loop gas pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric volvulus in adults is more common with paraesophageal hernia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, volvulus). COMLEX: because it is rare, volvulus can be overlooked clinically with catastrophic results (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Midgut volvulus is a neonatal emergency with bilious vomiting,","B":"A cecal carcinoma would obstruct more gradually and would","D":"Colonic ileus dilates the colon without the coffee-bean","E":"Ischemic colitis causes pain and bloody diarrhea, not this"}},{"id":"sdl29_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has sudden severe abdominal pain. He has atrial fibrillation and stopped anticoagulation after a recent fall, and there is no hernia. The abdomen is soft, the pain is far worse than the tenderness, and the pulse is 118/min. Serum lactate is 5.2 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). What is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Embolic occlusion of the SMA","B":"Thrombosis of a mesenteric vein","C":"Twisting of the sigmoid mesentery","D":"Vasculitis of small serosal arteries","E":"Rupture of a cecal vascular ectasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Atrial fibrillation throws an embolus into the superior mesenteric artery, producing pain out of proportion to a soft abdomen. A lactate of 5.2 mmol/L shows the bowel is already ischemic. Mesenteric venous thrombosis is usually slower and is linked to hypercoagulable states, not a cardiac embolus. Sigmoid twisting causes distention and a coffee-bean film, not this soft abdomen with a high lactate. Small-vessel vasculitis is not the acute event in new atrial fibrillation. A cecal vascular ectasia bleeds into the lumen and does not cause this ischemic pain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute arterial infarction produces a sharp demarcation between normal and ischemic bowel (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: coagulative necrosis of the muscularis propria develops within 1 to 4 days of infarction (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mesenteric venous thrombosis is usually slower and is linked","C":"Sigmoid twisting causes distention and a coffee-bean film, not","D":"Small-vessel vasculitis is not the acute event in new atrial","E":"A cecal vascular ectasia bleeds into the lumen and"}},{"id":"sdl29_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman has crampy left lower abdominal pain and then bloody diarrhea while recovering from septic shock. She has no history of chronic colitis. The left abdomen is tender without peritonitis, and the pulse is 104/min. The leukocyte count is 14,000/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). A colonic biopsy is taken. Which finding best supports ischemia?","choices":{"A":"Granulomas scattered in lamina propria","B":"Crypt abscesses in diffuse colitis","C":"Villous blunting with lymphocytes","D":"Upper mucosa lost but crypts spared","E":"Thickened subepithelial collagen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ischemia strips the surface mucosa and spares the crypt bases, which is the biopsy pattern after shock. Left-sided cramps and bloody diarrhea in that setting match a watershed injury. Scattered granulomas suggest a transmural inflammatory disease, not shock-related ischemia. Crypt abscesses throughout a diffuse colitis are the pattern of idiopathic colitis, not a short ischemic segment. Villous blunting with lymphocytes is a small-bowel pattern and is not this colonic injury. A thickened subepithelial collagen band causes watery diarrhea and is not produced by a shock episode.","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic ischemia commonly occurs at the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: neutrophils are recruited into ischemic mucosa within hours of reperfusion (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Scattered granulomas suggest a transmural inflammatory disease, not shock-related","B":"Crypt abscesses throughout a diffuse colitis are the pattern","C":"Villous blunting with lymphocytes is a small-bowel pattern and","E":"A thickened subepithelial collagen band causes watery diarrhea and"}},{"id":"sdl29_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with widespread atherosclerosis has ischemic injury confined to the right colon. The right side of the abdomen is tender. CT shows right-colon wall thickening, and the inferior mesenteric artery is patent. Why does this location carry a worse prognosis?","choices":{"A":"The right colon wall is especially thin","B":"The right colon lacks a submucosal layer","C":"The right colon has no collateral arcades","D":"Bacteria are far denser in the right colon","E":"Its artery also feeds the small intestine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The right colon is supplied by the same artery that feeds the small intestine, so ischemia there often means a long segment of bowel is threatened. Wall thickening limited to the right side is more ominous than a short left-sided watershed injury. Wall thickness is not what makes right-sided ischemia more dangerous. The right colon has a submucosa, like the rest of the colon. The right colon does have collateral arcades, and the danger is the shared trunk with the small bowel. Bacterial density is not the reason this location has a worse prognosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic mesenteric ischemia most commonly involves the SMA (First Aid Gastrointestinal, chronic mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: COPD and symptoms lasting more than 2 weeks are poor prognostic signs in colonic ischemia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Wall thickness is not what makes right-sided ischemia more","B":"The right colon has a submucosa, like the rest","C":"The right colon does have collateral arcades, and the","D":"Bacterial density is not the reason this location has"}},{"id":"sdl29_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman has recurrent painless hematochezia and fatigue. She has a harsh systolic murmur of aortic stenosis and is not taking anticoagulants. The abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 9.0 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL). Colonoscopy shows small flat red mucosal lesions. What will histology most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Glands showing high-grade dysplasia","B":"Granulation tissue in an ulcer bed","C":"Ectatic nests of thin-walled vessels","D":"Mucosal outpouching through the muscle","E":"Noncaseating granulomas in the mucosa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The flat red lesions are nests of ectatic thin-walled vessels in the mucosa. Aortic stenosis, painless bleeding, and iron deficiency are the setting in which these angiodysplasias are found. High-grade dysplasia is a neoplastic glandular change and is not the histology of these flat red spots. Granulation tissue lines an ulcer and is not the lesion seen here. A mucosal outpouching through muscle is a diverticulum, not a flat red spot. Noncaseating granulomas are an inflammatory finding and do not form these vascular lesions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: false diverticula occur where vasa recta penetrate the muscularis externa (First Aid Gastrointestinal, diverticula of the GI tract). COMLEX: angiodysplasia usually comes to clinical attention after the sixth decade (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"High-grade dysplasia is a neoplastic glandular change and is","B":"Granulation tissue lines an ulcer and is not the","D":"A mucosal outpouching through muscle is a diverticulum, not","E":"Noncaseating granulomas are an inflammatory finding and do not"}},{"id":"sdl29_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old man has bleeding from colonic vascular ectasias clustered in the cecum. He has aortic stenosis. Hemoglobin is 9.2 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Which mechanical factor is thought to explain that typical location?","choices":{"A":"Lowest blood flow at a watershed zone","B":"Thinnest muscularis of any colonic part","C":"Highest bacterial count in the colon lumen","D":"Largest diameter gives most wall tension","E":"Most lymphoid tissue in the large bowel"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The cecum has the largest diameter, so wall tension is highest there and the submucosal veins are repeatedly distended. Bleeding vascular ectasias cluster at that wide segment. The cecum is not a watershed between the two mesenteric arteries. A thinner muscularis is not the mechanical explanation given for this site. The highest bacterial count is not why the vessels ectasia in the cecum. Lymphoid tissue is richer in the ileum and appendix than in the cecum as an explanation for these vessels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia in older patients is associated with aortic stenosis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: the pathogenesis of angiodysplasia has been attributed to mechanical and congenital factors (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The cecum is not a watershed between the two mesenteric","B":"A thinner muscularis is not the mechanical explanation given","C":"The highest bacterial count is not why the vessels","E":"Lymphoid tissue is richer in the ileum and appendix"}},{"id":"sdl29_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man has recurrent hematochezia from colonic vascular ectasias. He has no liver disease and is not taking anticoagulants. The abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Which condition is classically associated with this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Type 2 diabetes mellitus","B":"End-stage renal disease","C":"Primary hypothyroidism","D":"Gouty arthritis history","E":"Chronic obstructive lung"},"correct":"B","explanation":"End-stage renal disease is classically associated with bleeding colonic vascular ectasias. Recurrent painless hematochezia with a normal abdominal examination is the presentation that prompts that association. Type 2 diabetes is not the classic systemic association of these vascular lesions. Primary hypothyroidism does not cluster with colonic vascular ectasia. Gout is not the associated condition. Chronic lung disease is not the classic association.","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia is confirmed by angiography (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: normal gut distention may intermittently occlude submucosal veins crossing the muscularis propria (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Type 2 diabetes is not the classic systemic association","C":"Primary hypothyroidism does not cluster with colonic vascular","D":"Gout is not the associated condition","E":"Chronic lung disease is not the classic"}},{"id":"sdl29_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has bloating, fatigue, and an itchy blistering rash on her elbows. She eats wheat daily, and a cousin has type 1 diabetes. She is pale and the abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 10.0 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL), IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is 130 U/mL (reference range: <4 U/mL), and anti-endomysial antibody is positive. A biopsy of the second part of the duodenum is taken. What will it most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Lymphocytosis with villous atrophy","B":"Vacuolated enterocytes filled with lipid","C":"Foamy macrophages packed in the lamina","D":"Transmural granulomas with deep fissuring","E":"Normal villi without inflammatory cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Dermatitis herpetiformis with a high anti-tissue transglutaminase titer is celiac disease, and the duodenum shows lymphocytes in the epithelium with villous atrophy. The elbow rash and iron deficiency are the clinical companions of that biopsy. Lipid-filled enterocytes are the picture when chylomicrons cannot be assembled, which does not cause this rash. Foamy macrophages packed in the lamina propria are a different storage disease. Transmural granulomas and fissures are a chronic inflammatory disease of the ileum, not this duodenal rash illness. Normal villi would not accompany this serology and rash.","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease is associated with HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac disease in adults most commonly presents between ages 30 and 60 (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lipid-filled enterocytes are the picture when chylomicrons cannot be","C":"Foamy macrophages packed in the lamina propria are a","D":"Transmural granulomas and fissures are a chronic inflammatory disease","E":"Normal villi would not accompany this serology and"}},{"id":"sdl29_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has chronic diarrhea, and gluten-sensitive enteropathy is suspected. He eats wheat daily. He is thin and the abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 11.4 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). HLA-DQ typing is ordered. How is this genetic result most useful?","choices":{"A":"Positive result confirms the diagnosis","B":"Result predicts the lymphoma risk","C":"Result sets the diet duration needed","D":"Result replaces the need for biopsy","E":"Negative result argues against it"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A negative HLA-DQ result argues against gluten-sensitive enteropathy, because almost every patient carries DQ2 or DQ8. The test is ordered to help exclude the disease, not to confirm it. A positive result is common in healthy people and does not confirm the diagnosis. The typing does not predict the risk of lymphoma. The result does not set how long the diet must be continued. A genetic result does not replace the biopsy when the diagnosis is still in doubt.","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac concordance in monozygotic twins is about 75% (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: celiac disease is associated with type 1 diabetes, thyroiditis, Sjögren syndrome, and IgA nephropathy (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A positive result is common in healthy people and","B":"The typing does not predict the risk of","C":"The result does not set how long the diet","D":"A genetic result does not replace the biopsy when"}},{"id":"sdl29_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl has bulky greasy stools and poor weight gain. She has recurrent respiratory infections and has stopped her enzyme capsules. She is thin and the abdomen is soft. Sweat chloride is 80 mmol/L (reference range: <30 mmol/L), and fecal elastase is 40 µg/g (reference range: >200 µg/g). Which phase of nutrient absorption is primarily impaired?","choices":{"A":"Brush-border digestion","B":"Intraluminal digestion","C":"Transepithelial transport","D":"Lymphatic lipid transport","E":"Mucosal nutrient uptake"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Missing pancreatic enzymes impair intraluminal digestion, which is why the stools are greasy when she stops the capsules. A sweat chloride of 80 mmol/L identifies the disease that destroys the exocrine pancreas. Brush-border digestion is intact, and the enzymes missing are secreted into the lumen. Transepithelial transport works once nutrients are digested. Lymphatic lipid transport is intact in this enzyme deficiency. The mucosa can still take up nutrients that have been digested in the lumen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency gives a normal d-xylose test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic insufficiency). COMLEX: malabsorption can disturb intraluminal digestion, terminal digestion, transepithelial transport, or lymphatic transport (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and CFTR","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Brush-border digestion is intact, and the enzymes missing are","C":"Transepithelial transport works once nutrients are","D":"Lymphatic lipid transport is intact in this enzyme","E":"The mucosa can still take up nutrients that have"}},{"id":"sdl29_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-month-old girl has steatorrhea and poor growth. She was born at term, and there is no jaundice. She has edema and a soft abdomen. The blood smear shows acanthocytes, plasma lacks apoB-containing lipoproteins, and triglycerides are 10 mg/dL (reference range: 30–150 mg/dL). What will a small-bowel biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Lipid-vacuolated enterocytes","B":"Flattened villi with lymphocytes","C":"Neutrophils within the crypts","D":"PAS-positive foamy macrophages","E":"Absent ganglion cell clusters"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Without apoB, enterocytes cannot export chylomicrons and fill with lipid vacuoles. Acanthocytes and absent apoB-containing lipoproteins are the blood findings that match those vacuolated cells. Flattened villi with lymphocytes are the gluten-sensitive pattern and do not remove apoB. Neutrophils in the crypts are an infectious or idiopathic colitis finding. PAS-positive foamy macrophages are a different infiltration and are not the enterocyte lesion here. Ganglion cells are present, and this is not an aganglionic segment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: failure to absorb essential fatty acids in abetalipoproteinemia causes fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: abetalipoproteinemia is autosomal recessive (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Flattened villi with lymphocytes are the gluten-sensitive pattern and","C":"Neutrophils in the crypts are an infectious or idiopathic colitis","D":"PAS-positive foamy macrophages are a different infiltration and are","E":"Ganglion cells are present, and this is not an aganglionic"}},{"id":"sdl29_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy has severe persistent diarrhea, type 1 diabetes, and eczema. A maternal uncle died in infancy with the same picture. He is small, with an eczematous rash and a distended abdomen. Glucose is 280 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL). Which cell type is defective?","choices":{"A":"Intraepithelial CD8+ T cells","B":"Mature B lymphocytes","C":"CD4+ regulatory T cells","D":"Intestinal goblet cells","E":"Small-bowel enterocytes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This X-linked pattern of diarrhea, diabetes, and eczema is loss of CD4-positive regulatory T cells. The affected uncle and the early autoimmune injury point to that missing regulator. Intraepithelial CD8-positive T cells are increased in gluten-sensitive enteropathy, which is not this multisystem illness. Mature B cells are not the lineage lost in this regulatory-cell disease. Goblet cells are present, and mucus production is not the defective step. Enterocytes are not the cell type whose loss produces diabetes and eczema together.","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoantibodies to enterocytes and goblet cells are common in autoimmune enteropathy (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: intraepithelial lymphocytes in autoimmune enteropathy increase less than in celiac disease (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Intraepithelial CD8-positive T cells are increased in gluten-sensitive enteropathy,","B":"Mature B cells are not the lineage lost in","D":"Goblet cells are present, and mucus production is not","E":"Enterocytes are not the cell type whose loss produces"}},{"id":"sdl29_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl from a rural community with poor sanitation has stunted growth and chronic malabsorption. She eats wheat, antibiotics have not helped, and no single pathogen is found. She is small, the abdomen is distended, and there is no rash. Hemoglobin is 10.2 g/dL (reference range: 11.0–14.0 g/dL), and IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is 2 U/mL (reference range: <4 U/mL). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Classic gluten-sensitive enteropathy","B":"Microvillus inclusion disease","C":"Congenital lipoprotein deficiency","D":"Inherited autoimmune enteropathy","E":"Environmental enteric dysfunction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Chronic exposure to fecal contamination with stunting, malabsorption, and a negative celiac serology is environmental enteric dysfunction. Wheat in the diet and a normal anti-tissue transglutaminase titer argue against gluten-sensitive disease. Gluten-sensitive enteropathy would raise anti-tissue transglutaminase, which is normal here. Microvillus inclusion disease presents in early infancy with intractable diarrhea, not this community pattern. Congenital lipoprotein deficiency shows acanthocytes and absent apoB, which are not described. Inherited autoimmune enteropathy is a genetic multisystem illness and is not tied to poor sanitation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: biopsies in environmental enteric dysfunction show more immune cell infiltration and less villous atrophy than advanced celiac disease (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: stunting from environmental enteric dysfunction may be accompanied by uncorrectable cognitive deficits (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gluten-sensitive enteropathy would raise anti-tissue transglutaminase, which is normal","B":"Microvillus inclusion disease presents in early infancy with intractable","C":"Congenital lipoprotein deficiency shows acanthocytes and absent apoB, which","D":"Inherited autoimmune enteropathy is a genetic multisystem illness and"}},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":3,"stem":"A 74-year-old man has 10 hours of vomiting and a tender, tense, irreducible bulge in the right groin. He has had no prior bowel resection. The pulse is 110/min and the abdomen is distended. At surgery a loop of ileum in the hernia sac is swollen, dusky, and congested. Serum lactate is 3.1 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). Which event most likely started the damage?","choices":{"A":"Embolic occlusion of an ileal artery","B":"Compressed venous outflow at the neck","C":"Reperfusion injury after reduction","D":"Twisting of bowel around an adhesion","E":"A hyperplastic lymphoid tissue lead point"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The narrow neck compresses venous outflow first, so the loop swells and becomes dusky before the artery is occluded. An irreducible groin bulge with a congested loop is that sequence. An arterial embolus would infarct bowel in the mesentery, not a loop trapped in the groin. Reperfusion injury follows reduction and is not the event that first damaged the trapped loop. Twisting around an adhesion is an internal closed loop and is not this groin hernia. Hyperplastic lymphoid tissue is a toddler lead point and is not inside this hernia sac.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In a hernia, pressure at the neck impairs venous drainage, and the resulting edema leads to incarceration and then strangulation (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: A tender, irreducible hernia is a surgical emergency because strangulation can follow.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 2 — physiologic herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An arterial embolus would infarct bowel in the mesentery,","C":"Reperfusion injury follows reduction and is not the event","D":"Twisting around an adhesion is an internal closed loop","E":"Hyperplastic lymphoid tissue is a toddler lead point and"}},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":3,"stem":"A 77-year-old woman has crampy left-sided pain and bloody diarrhea one day after cardiac surgery with several hours of hypotension. She has no history of colitis. The pulse is 104/min and blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg. CT shows wall thickening only at the splenic flexure, and both the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries are patent. Why was this segment most vulnerable?","choices":{"A":"Its mucosa lacks a muscularis mucosae","B":"Its wall is thinnest where diameter is largest","C":"It lies where two arterial territories end","D":"It receives a single end artery from the aorta","E":"Its veins are compressed by the spleen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The splenic flexure lies where the two mesenteric arterial territories end, so it is the first to suffer when pressure falls. Patent main arteries and a short segment of wall thickening after hypotension mark that watershed. The mucosa has a muscularis mucosae, and that is not why this segment is vulnerable. Wall thickness and diameter are not the reason the flexure is the watershed. It does not receive a single end artery directly from the aorta. The splenic vein does not compress this segment of colon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypoperfusion injures watershed zones, chiefly the splenic flexure and the rectosigmoid (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Left-sided pain with bloody stools after hypotension suggests ischemic colitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The mucosa has a muscularis mucosae, and that is","B":"Wall thickness and diameter are not the reason the","D":"It does not receive a single end artery directly","E":"The splenic vein does not compress this segment of"}},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 78-year-old man has recurrent painless maroon stools and fatigue. He has no liver disease and is not taking anticoagulants. There is a harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the right upper sternal border that radiates to the carotids. Hemoglobin is 8.6 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Colonoscopy shows small, flat, cherry-red vessels in the cecum. Which mechanism links the heart disease to the bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Raised portal pressure dilating veins","B":"Emboli from the valve infarcting mucosa","C":"Inherited weakness of capillary walls","D":"Shear loss of von Willebrand factor","E":"Platelet consumption on the stenotic valve"},"correct":"D","explanation":"High shear across the stenotic aortic valve cleaves von Willebrand factor, so the cecal vascular ectasias bleed. The radiating murmur and iron deficiency are that association. Portal pressure is not raised, and there is no liver disease to dilate veins. Emboli from the valve would infarct mucosa rather than cause recurrent painless bleeding from flat vessels. The vessel walls are not congenitally weak as the link to the murmur. Platelets are not consumed on the valve as the mechanism of this bleeding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Heyde syndrome links aortic stenosis to bleeding angiodysplasia through shear-induced loss of von Willebrand factor (Bootcamp Gastroenterology). COMLEX: Older patients with recurrent painless lower GI bleeding and aortic stenosis should be evaluated for angiodysplasia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Portal pressure is not raised, and there is no","B":"Emboli from the valve would infarct mucosa rather than","C":"The vessel walls are not congenitally weak as the","E":"Platelets are not consumed on the valve as the"}},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":3,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman has bloating, loose stools, and fatigue. She eats wheat daily, and a sister has thyroid disease. She is pale and the abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 10.0 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL), IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is 120 U/mL (reference range: <4 U/mL), and anti-endomysial antibody is positive. The duodenum shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytes, and HLA typing shows DQ2. Which step lets dietary gluten activate her CD4-positive T cells?","choices":{"A":"Gliadin binding to class I on enterocytes","B":"Direct toxicity of gliadin to enzymes","C":"IgE cross-linking on mucosal mast cells","D":"Bacterial cleavage of gluten in the colon","E":"Deamidation of gliadin peptides by TG2"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tissue transglutaminase deamidates gliadin peptides so they bind HLA-DQ2 and activate CD4-positive T cells. The high antibody titer, endomysial antibody, and flat villi are the result of that step. Gliadin is not presented on class I molecules to these CD4-positive cells. Gliadin is not directly toxic to brush-border enzymes as the activating step. IgE on mast cells is not how gluten drives this T-cell injury. Bacterial cleavage of gluten in the colon is not the step that activates duodenal T cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In celiac disease, TG2 deamidates gliadin so it binds HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 and activates CD4+ T cells, and IgA anti-TG2 is the most sensitive serologic test (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: A strict gluten-free diet is the treatment, and persistent symptoms on the diet warrant evaluation for complications.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gliadin is not presented on class I molecules to","B":"Gliadin is not directly toxic to brush-border enzymes as","C":"IgE on mast cells is not how gluten drives","D":"Bacterial cleavage of gluten in the colon is not"}},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man from southern China has bloating and gas after milk, present since adolescence. He has no weight loss. The abdomen is soft and mildly distended. Hemoglobin is 14.6 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and the symptoms resolve when he avoids milk. What will a duodenal biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Flattened villi","B":"Lipid vacuoles","C":"Unremarkable mucosa","D":"Crypt abscesses","E":"Foamy macrophages"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adult-type lactase nonpersistence leaves the mucosa unremarkable, because the missing enzyme is not visible as villous injury. Gas after milk since adolescence, with a normal hemoglobin and relief on avoidance, is that isolated enzyme pattern. Flattened villi would mean a diffuse mucosal injury, which this milk-only symptom is not. Lipid vacuoles are the apoB-deficiency lesion and are unrelated to milk sugar. Crypt abscesses are an inflammatory colitis finding and are not expected here. Foamy macrophages are a storage disease and do not follow milk ingestion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lactose hydrogen breath test is positive if breath hydrogen rises more than 20 ppm over baseline (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: congenital lactose intolerance is a rare autosomal recessive disorder (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion by amylase and brush-border enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Flattened villi would mean a diffuse mucosal injury, which","B":"Lipid vacuoles are the apoB-deficiency lesion and are unrelated","D":"Crypt abscesses are an inflammatory colitis finding and are","E":"Foamy macrophages are a storage disease and do not"}},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has two months of colicky periumbilical pain, vomiting, and a 5 kg weight loss. She has had no recent viral illness, no abdominal surgery, and no recent vaccination. The abdomen is distended, bowel sounds are high-pitched, and there is no hernia. CT shows a segment of ileum telescoped into the adjacent distal ileum. Hemoglobin is 10.8 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL). Which finding at surgery is most likely at the leading edge?","choices":{"A":"An intraluminal tumor at the lead point","B":"Hyperplastic Peyer patches after infection","C":"A congenital band from malrotation","D":"Inspissated stool in cystic fibrosis","E":"A fibrous adhesion from prior surgery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Adult intussusception without a viral illness or prior surgery is led by an intraluminal tumor. Weight loss and a telescoped ileal segment are the clues that a mass, not lymphoid hyperplasia, is the lead point. Hyperplastic Peyer patches lead intussusception in toddlers after a viral illness, which she has not had. A congenital band from malrotation presents in infancy, not as a new adult telescoping. Inspissated stool is a newborn problem and does not telescope adult ileum. She has had no surgery to leave a fibrous adhesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Childhood intussusception is usually idiopathic with lymphoid hyperplasia as the lead point, whereas adult cases usually have a tumor at the leading edge (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Adult intussusception needs surgical exploration because a neoplasm is likely.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — segmentation versus peristalsis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Hyperplastic Peyer patches lead intussusception in toddlers after a","C":"A congenital band from malrotation presents in infancy, not","D":"Inspissated stool is a newborn problem and does not","E":"She has had no surgery to leave a fibrous"}},{"id":"grid_sdl29_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":4,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy has chronic diarrhea, bulky greasy stools, and poor growth. He lives in a rural village with poor sanitation, failed to respond to two doses of an oral vaccine, and eats a mixed diet that includes wheat. There is no family history of gastrointestinal disease. He is small for his age, the abdomen is distended, and there is no rash. Hemoglobin is 10.2 g/dL (reference range: 11.0–14.0 g/dL). Which of the following sets of findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Fecal fat","Villus height","Anti-TG2 IgA","Serum apoB"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↓","↑","↔"],"B":["↑","↔","↔","↔"],"C":["↑","↔","↔","↓"],"D":["↑","↓","↔","↔"],"E":["↔","↔","↔","↔"]},"correct":"D","explanation":"Choice D (↑ fecal fat, ↓ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is correct. Environmental enteric dysfunction (tropical sprue) follows chronic exposure to fecal pathogens where sanitation is poor; the notes list stunting and high oral vaccine failure rates, and describe pronounced lamina propria immune cell infiltration with villous atrophy that is less advanced than in celiac disease. Loss of absorptive surface causes malabsorption with steatorrhea, and the disorder is non-genetic and not driven by gluten, so celiac serology is negative. Choice A (↑ fecal fat, ↓ villus height, ↑ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is celiac disease, in which IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is the most sensitive serum marker. Choice B (↑ fecal fat, ↔ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, as in cystic fibrosis, where intraluminal digestion fails but the mucosa is intact. Choice C (↑ fecal fat, ↔ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↓ serum apoB) is abetalipoproteinemia, in which absent apoB-containing lipoproteins leave enterocytes unable to export chylomicrons, causing steatorrhea in infancy with acanthocytes on the smear. Choice E (↔ fecal fat, ↔ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is lactase deficiency, an osmotic diarrhea from unabsorbed lactose without fat malabsorption. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 29, sections 4 and 5: general malabsorption, cystic fibrosis, celiac disease, EED, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia); Robbins Ch. 17 (malabsorption and diarrhea). The ↑ fecal fat for environmental enteric dysfunction is inferred from loss of absorptive surface, and intact villi in the pancreatic, abetalipoproteinemia, and lactase disorders are inferred because the sources describe no villous injury in them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: villous atrophy with negative celiac serology in a child from an area of poor sanitation points to environmental enteric dysfunction (Exam 2 notes, SDL 29). COMLEX: steatorrhea with normal villi suggests a luminal (pancreatic) or export (abetalipoproteinemia) defect rather than mucosal loss.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — micelle formation and chylomicron export","isHighYield":true}]};
